// U ML092:286-353 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Allah says in the Quran (anNisa4:59) یَـٰۤأَیُّهَا ٱلَّذِینَ ءَامَنُوۤا۟ أَطِیعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَطِیعُوا۟ ٱلرَّسُولَ وَأُو۟لِی ٱلۡأَمۡرِ مِنكُمۡۖ. So Allah first in the Quran says, (obey Allah, obey the Messenger), and He doesn't say: and obey Ulu alAmr. Allah doesn't say: وأطيعوا أولي الأمر. But Allah says: أَطِیعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَطِیعُوا۟ ٱلرَّسُولَ. Why does Allah not say obey Uli alAmr? Because they're fallible! They can make mistakes. So if you say the Ulu alAmr here are the Imams, then you admit that the Imams are fallible. Why do you admit the Imams are fallible? Because Allah didn't say أطیعوا (obey them). So this complete obedience is only given to Allah and the Messenger, because these two sources are infallible. Now, if you claim that Ulu alAmr are referring to the Imams, then you did one thing: you highlighted that your Imams are fallible.", "And then we go on to read. Then Allah says: فَإِن تَنَازَعْتُمْ فِي شَيْءٍ (If you disagree over anything). Then what do you do? Return it to Allah and the Messenger (anNisa4:59). So it doesn't say return it to the Imams, which means again that the Imams are fallible. Because if the Imams were, as you claim, infallible, and this verse is talking about them, the verse should say: if you disagree over anything, return it to Uli alAmr, return it to the Imams.", "And you misinterpret the word Uli alAmr, which clearly does not mean Imams; it means Uli alAmr. That's what the word means in the Arabic language: the people of authority. And people of authority: that can refer to many things. Now it doesn't say Imams there anywhere. So you can claim all you want \"this refers to the Imams\"; it doesn't! But even if I were to accept it, even if I were to say: OK, Ulu alAmr are the Imams, you're proving that therefore they are fallible, and if we disagree over anything, we don't need to care about what the Imams say.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Shia Girl Confronts Muslim On His Claims Then This Happens! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LcbjBQElXe0", ((853, "14:13–14:57"), (900, "15:00–15:18"), (927, "15:27–15:50"),))
