#passage(("This idea of it being the end of time is false: that Prophet Muhammad did not name a successor because it was the end of times. Jesus did not come back yet. The Jewish people were waiting for the Messiah, and Prophet Muhammad cannot be the Messiah. So it cannot be the end of times. And in fact, both the Sunni and the Shia tradition believe the Prophet named the successor. Of course, we disagree on who the successor is named, but both the Sunni tradition and the Shia tradition believes it.", "The Shia tradition will tell you the Prophetﷺ named 'Ali ibn Abi Talib as the successor. The Sunni tradition will tell you that the Prophetﷺ named Abu Bakr asSiddiq, because a woman came and asked the Prophetﷺ: who should I come to if I don't find you here next year? He said to her, come to Abu Bakr. When the Prophet became sick, he put Abu Bakr to lead the people in the prayer. And these was all, among many, many other evidences, the people of the Sunna use, as the Prophetﷺ was teaching the people that Abu Bakr was the person in charge after the death of the Prophetﷺ. And then the Shia tradition, of course, will tell you: no, it was 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, and they would refer to an incident that happened in history where the Prophetﷺ said: \"Whoever is an ally to me, they should also be an ally to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib\". And they interpreted the word 'ally' there differently.", "This idea that Prophet Muhammad did not name a successor is refuted both in the Sunni and the Shia tradition. He didn't say specifically in these words \"successor\"; he didn't use these specific terminologies, yes. But that had nothing to do with the idea of the end of times.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((4458, "1:14:18–1:14:50"), (4493, "1:14:53–1:15:37"), (4540, "1:15:40–1:15:59"),))
