// U ML223:1070-1083 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("We have no issues with 'Ali; we're not criticising him. Virtues of 'Ali, there are many that we have in our books. You can open the Bukhari and Muslim; these books, they will tell you about virtues of 'Ali ؓ, as well as other Companions. They will have chapters on their virtues; we believe them. There're narrations about their bravery, the good things they did, the praise of the Prophetﷺ to them. But just the Prophetﷺ praising someone, like he did with Abu Bakr and 'Umar, does not mean the whole religion has become about them now, and leaving the Prophet himself, and the Quran!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Fully Dismantles The Shia Position! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t7vhKiTrvNA", ((2894, "48:14–48:53"),))

=== 42.4 Proving the Twelve Imams from Shia sources
// NODE P11.C01.S04
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR097:1-393 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("[Ammar Nakshawani] isn't some random [Shia] that likes to preach about shiism; he's a PhD holder in Islamic Sciences from the University of [Exeter], and was the holder of the Imam Ali chair for [Shia] studies at Hartford Seminary. He's also one of the top 500 most influential Muslims in the world. So when [Nakshawani] puts out a 50 minute video for the evidence for the 12 imams, it's going to be as good as it gets. Before even starting, I'd like to point out that [Dr. Ammar] mentions that there's no proof for each of the [Imams] in Sunni literature, but you have to rely on [Shia] literature. I'll go a bit further than this: you actually cannot prove the 12 [Imams] from [Twelver] literature either.", "Folks, you need to be aware of something: these are merely claims. When you have these reports, attributions to [Ja'far], in which he's claiming that he has a 70 Cubit long book by the prophet, peace be upon him, which he dictated, or he's got a [mushaf] that's three times longer than the actual Quran, or that he has knowledge of all matters in the day and the night until the day of the Resurrection, or that Angels speak to him: these are just claims. None of this is proof. If someone says to you: \"this is halal and this [haram], an angel said this to me, now pay me a fifth of your income\", would you believe them? \"But they're reliable, these are reliable people.\" If someone came up to me that I considered reliable, and they said to me: \"an angel comes to me and says this [haram] and this [halal], now give me a fifth of your money\", that's it, I will doubt my own judgment in that person. \"But these are the [Imams].\" They are the imams because of these claims? If these are imams, then prove that they're imams, and then bring these quotes from them. But first you got to prove that they're imams. You don't prove that they're imams just by them claiming that they're imams.", "There are multiple issues with this narration, but I'm going to stick with two main ones: one that's a bit Technical and one that's quite simple. The technical point is there's a problem in the isnad. It refers to this narrator here as \"his father\"; that happens to be [Ibrahim bin Hashim]. He's narrated over 6,000 narrations. However, despite this, there is a lack of information about him. In the 700s after Hijra: \"We did not find any of our people speaking of him negatively or specifically in favor of him, but the more correct view is to accept him.\" You come 500 years later and you're speaking about this narrator who has 6,000 hadiths, and all of a sudden you're claiming that he's reliable?", "Ironically, there's one instance that I was able to find that would give us an indication of the lack of reliability of [Ibrahim]: this quote in which the [Imam] is asked: why do we stay in Mina for three days at Haj? So he said: if you made it to any one of those days, then you have made it to Haj. This report is solely narrated by [Ibrahim] bin Hashim, and this report is not what he goes by, but rather he goes by the report that says that you have to be there on the 10th for your Haj to be accepted. So let me get this straight: however, we're supposed to accept this report by [Ibrahim] bin Hashim, in which Ali bin [Muhammad] Hadi is the divinely appointed [Imam], who's visited by Angels, who tells him this is halal and this [haram], and you have to pay him a fifth of your income? You want me to believe that Ali Muhammed is divinely appointed because of a report by Ibrahim bin Hashim, who no one has praised for 500 years? By the way, this is just the first problem. The second problem is that Muhammad Jawad is appointing his son on this report, and this means nothing to me, because you didn't prove [Jawad's Imamah] yet. So the first thing you got to do is you got to prove [Jawad], that he's an Imam, before expecting me to accept his son's appointment.", "I'm just going to be mentioning two issues with this report. The first issue: what's fascinating is that we actually have a book, and in this book we find the Hadith of [al-Khidr]: \"I testify that your father is the commander of the Believers and the wasi of Muhammad truly, and I continue to testify to this, and I testify that you are his [wasi], and I testify that Hussein is your [wasi]\", until he mentioned the last of them. We have a full list of [Imams], all 12; in [al-Mahasin], which predates by 50 years, we have an incomplete list that clearly says: \"until he mentioned the last of them\". Excuse me folks, but it seems like [al-Mahasin] has the original report, and it seems [al-Kulayni] fills in the blanks.", "The second problem is that this report is narrated by Jawad himself. Think of it this way: Jawad, what is the proof that you're the [Imam]? [Jawad] says: \"Well, the proof that I'm the [Imam] is that my great great great great great-grandfather appointed his son, and his son appointed his brother, and his brother appointed his son, who appointed his son, who appointed his son, who appointed me. That's my proof, and that's the proof that Angels speak to me and they tell me that this is Haram and this is [Halal], and I would like a fifth of your income.\"", "Imagine a [suitor] has come for your daughter or your sister or whatever, and you're sitting in the [majlis], and you say to the [suitor]: \"Please tell me about yourself.\" So the [suitor] says: \"Well, my father says that I'm the kindest, most generous, most honest person.\" So you start looking around and you say: \"Well, where is your father?\" And then he says: \"Oh, my father passed away a few years ago.\" Are you going to rely on what this person has attributed to their father, or would you do the sensible thing and ask more about the person from a third party? Now if you're going to say: \"Well, [obviously] I'm going to ask other people about him\", then I say to you: then do the same [with Jawad]: ask other people, and do not rely on someone's self-appointment. By the way, folks, as someone who studied the texts objectively, I don't even believe Jawad appointed himself. Remember, the report [in al-Mahasin] doesn't even mention [Jawad] here as an [Imam]; it only mentions Ali and Hussein. And just like that, we're done with the arguments.",), "Farid Responds, “Ammar Nakshawani FAILS to Prove 12 Imams from Shia Sources”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hXKOtyy3Mqg", ((2, "0:02–1:30"), (184, "3:04–4:37"), (388, "6:28–7:40"), (460, "7:40–9:31"), (809, "13:29–15:12"), (918, "15:18–15:59"), (962, "16:02–17:33"),))
// U FR127:725-818 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("There were other groups that rejected the imama of every single Imam: their Imam was questionable. Every single time [an] Imam passed away, he had 13 different groups. At this specific moment you haven't proven the imama of most of the imams; you're assuming. You have not established his [Imamah] yet. So far the only thing that you're saying is [twelvers] accept him as an Imam, therefore he's an Imam.",), "Farid Responds, “Shia Debater attempts to prove 12 Imams”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oMXb6e4GNl4", ((2279, "37:59–41:39"),))
// U FR127:1379-1435 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("He mentioned that Hadith al-jawad is sufficient to prove that 12 imams are 12 imams and they're divinely appointed. He quoted [al-Kafi], and that specific report in [al-Kafi] quotes [Ahmad] in his book, but he doesn't mention the names of the imams. Isn't that weird? He never mentions this concept of 12 imams. Kakashi's answer was: you're appealing to [mystery]. I'm not appealing to mystery; I'm just pointing out something that should cause you to be suspicious about the people that came after Ahmed Mohamed. People would usually be surprised: why does this report exist after? Why isn't it in his book? It was first heard in the year 250 after Hijra. He lived for 30 years, but only included the part of the narration that doesn't list all the imam's names. What's going on? And then finally, his last piece of evidence did not exist for a thousand years.",), "Farid Responds, “Shia Debater attempts to prove 12 Imams”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oMXb6e4GNl4", ((4675, "1:17:55–1:19:53"),))

