// U FR174:699-1064 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("I'm saying I'm blameworthy as a sunni for rejecting the imam, and I'm going to go to hellfire. I'm not a kafir in this dunya; alhamdulillah, I'm happy to not be a kafir according to shiism. However, I'm still going to go to hell forever because I denied the [Imamah] of your imams. And these quotes by your scholars are all about the [kufr] of a sunni in this life, and you've provided evidence that the sunni is not a [kafir] in this dunya. Unfortunately, we're all going to go to hell forever. That's the problem.", "I've provided to you the statements of your imams, and according to you, the imams are rejecting being imams. How am I to be blamed for accepting these statements? How am I going to be asked on the day of judgement: \"Farid, why did you reject this imam and that imam?\" I'll say: because they rejected being imams. Most importantly: if I reject that as an imam, even though he has said \"I am not an imam\", according to your reports, I'm going to go to hell forever?", "Let me correct the wording: it's not \"whoever does not give [bay'ah] to the imam\"; it's not even amir; it says: who dies that doesn't have a [bay'ah] upon his neck [dies] the death of [jahiliyyah], because the time of [jahiliyyah], it was just a state of lawlessness where everyone was just running around.", "You cannot place the [nass] upon anyone. The reason why the [nass] cannot be placed upon anyone is because the imam himself denied being an imam. The imam said, in the reports from jafar sadiq in [al-Kafi], that the way to find out who the imam is, is through the clearness. And what is the clearness? It's the clear appointment. Unfortunately, there cannot be a clear appointment when the imam himself denies being an imam.",), "Farid Responds, “Full Debate: Farid (Sunni) vs Bayat (Shia)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iwc31dHLfco", ((2166, "36:06–36:42"), (2208, "36:48–39:04"), (2394, "39:54–40:12"), (2580, "43:00–44:13"),))

=== 42.6 Hasan, Husayn and the divinely appointed imamate
// NODE P11.C01.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR122:1-170 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("Not too many people are familiar with exactly what happened with Hussein, and it's something that we should familiarize ourselves with: we should familiarize ourselves with his struggle, with the tragedy that occurred at karbala, with his character, with his merits, with him as a whole, because remember, Hussein isn't a monopoly for [Shias]; Hussein is for all of the Muslims.", "This opinion that Hussein was divinely appointed Imam actually conflicts with his actions, and if you actually hold this view that he's a divinely appointed Imam, then you've attributed a deficiency to him, because of some of the points that I'm going to be mentioning. I'll be sharing three examples of how his actions directly conflict with this view that he was a divinely appointed infallible man.", "The first issue is [al-Hasan] relinquishing his political power to [Muawiya]. A lot of shias, when speaking to them about this specific fact, they will say something along the lines of: \"Well, al-hassan gave up his political power, but he's still a spiritual Imam, and Imam is a spiritual position.\" But if that's the case, then why make such a big deal? Because one could simply say: \"Well, yeah, the people chose [Abu Bakr], but Ali is still the spiritual Imam, so therefore it shouldn't matter that he did not become the [khalifa].\"", "In any case, when they were in the Medina, they didn't really hold a spiritual Imam position either. They were not teaching the people matters of the Deen in the same way their father did, and in the same way their children and grandchildren, progeny, did, and that's why you barely find any hadiths in which Hussein were teaching people the matters of their Deen. They were not seen as spiritual authorities in the way that Ali was seen as Spiritual Authority. So [al-Hasan] and Hussein, despite their High position in both the eyes of [Sunnis] and shias, they were not seen as spiritual authorities during their time.", "Hassan lived from like the year 40 until the year 50, 10 years as the alleged spiritual Imam, and Hussein lived in Medina for the next 10 years. Hussein himself in those 10 years did not take this position up and did not teach the people their Deen; he did not take the position of an infallible spiritual Imam. [Some Shias will] say: \"Well, yeah, but that's because [Muawiya] wouldn't allow Hussein to take that spiritual position.\" However, if [al-Hasan] did not relinquish power to [Muawiya], then he would be able to teach people their Deen in the same way that Ali told people their Deen during the time that he was in kufa. So there's no good answer to this. Their actions clearly conflicts with this idea of them being divinely appointed spiritual and political imams. So if this appointment was real, then this would be something that they conflicted with, and it would be some sort of a deficiency. I would never attribute this to them.", "The second example of an action that was taken by Hussein that conflicts directly with imama is how he treated the whole situation, in regards to his reaction to the governor of Medina at the time. Him and Hussein conflicted with one another in terms of a piece of land, the marwa, which is north of Medina. When this occurred, Hussein said to the governor of Medina: \"Swear by Allah that you will be fair with me, or else I will take my sword and go to the mosque of the messenger of Allah and call upon the pact.\" Things were getting out of hand; he became fair with Hussein until Hussein was satisfied. So this whole episode conflicts with this idea that Hussein was a divinely appointed Imam, because if Hussein was a divinely appointed Imam, then he would be acting like this towards [Muawiya]. Hussein, of course, he was oppressed by Waleed, and he attained his right when this occurred. However, what he should have done was he should have done the same with [Muawiya], and he should have taken back his right from [Muawiya]: his political rights and his spiritual right to teach people their Deen as an appointed infallible Imam.", "The third and final point is what occurs in karbala itself. When Hussein is at karbala and he is surrounded by the troops of [Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad], he says a specific statement that is recorded both by sunnis and [Shias]. According to [Abu Mikhnaf], who is the earliest, most authoritative [Shia] Source in regards to the events at [Karbala], he says that this specific statement is the position of the majority of the [muhaddithin], and he's quoting his uncle as well. He says that according to the majority of them, Hussein said to the Troops: \"Allow me one of three: either to return from where I came\", meaning to Medina, \"or to place my hand onto the hand of yazid, and he should decide what should occur between us, or you send me to one of the borders of the lands of Islam, to live among them with their rights and their duties.\" Now these opinions and these statements, they would be fine and they would make sense from anyone who would be making them in such a circumstance. However, these specific statements are not befitting of an infallible divinely appointed Imam.",), "Farid Responds, “Karbala Refutes Imamah”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-k5kZ_COavw", ((3, "0:03–0:31"), (56, "0:56–1:30"), (90, "1:30–2:13"), (135, "2:15–3:25"), (212, "3:32–4:50"), (290, "4:50–6:27"), (390, "6:30–7:55"),))

