#passage(("It's a prophecy. A prophecy isn't proof for the existence of him; this is not proof that he existed. I'm asking specifically about proof of the existence of a person, and prophecy is not the way to prove that someone existed. If you have to rely on Prophecy for the existence of a historical character, or a historical personality, that's a problem. You're just going to keep waiting. It's a problem, because if you're going to say what's the proof for the existence of Muhammad, peace be upon him, I'm not going to say Deuteronomy 18; that's a different topic.",), "Farid Responds, “Sunni Shia Discussion on Imam Mahdi Existence”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qg_rjmviNvs", ((338, "5:38–7:15"),))
// U FR056:40-171 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("You have these collectors, like a tax collector, and these collectors, they would collect tax for whoever they believe the Imam was. Remember, the [Imam] is the divine, the appointed guide. So you had the 11th [Imam], his name is [Hasan al-Askari]; he had his own collectors, and at one point there was even this dispute between his collectors. I'm not sure exactly how that dispute occurred, and it seems like there's some vagueness in regards to what the [classical] Works mentioned, but basically one of them seems to have been siding towards [Askari]'s brother, who some other [Shia] saw as the [Imam]. We're not sure exactly what happened; perhaps he was planning to collect the taxes, support his brother or something like that.", "In any case, long story short, [Faris] is actually killed. The tax collector is killed, and when the tax collector is killed (this is authentic according to [Shia] standards, you'll find this in [al-Kafi]) [Askari] is sending payments to the killer. So he's actually involved in the killing of the tax collector that he had appointed.", "So you have a group of people that are now following his brother, collecting taxes for his brother [Muhammad], and then you have another group of [Shia] that are collecting money for [Ja'far] Ali, his other brother. So you got three brothers, and you have all these people running around collecting tax for each of them, and they're all allegedly divinely appointed [Imams]. So there's this Mass confusion occurring.", "Now Muhammed, I believe he's the eldest. I'm not saying he ever claimed to be an Imam; God knows if any of them claimed to be an Imam. All we know is that we have all these Tax Collectors collecting money, perhaps just collecting money for themselves, God knows. In any case, that idea doesn't really float around for too much. However, the idea that [Ja'far] Ali is an [Imam], that had some weight at a specific time. So the tax collector that was killed, his sister starts siding with [Ja'far] Ali, and you have this war among Tax Collectors where people are literally being killed. One of them was someone that called to [Muhammad] Ali, and then he switched over [to Ja'far], and then his body is found in a pond, drowned. So you have literally people being killed over who the rightful [Imam] is. This would be happening around the year 250 after [Hijrah].", "So what happens is, the tax collectors now that were collecting tax, they're in a bit of predicament. That's an issue: the person that they're collecting tax for passes away. What are we going to do now? Who are we going to collect tax for? Keep in mind that there's this huge chance that they're actually taking the money in for themselves. So they have three choices. One is to say he never passed away. Or they could say the imama has shifted over to his brother [Ja'far] and now we're going to collect [tax] for him; that's not going to fly, because you're just killing those people. And the third is to claim they had a hidden son.", "The first thought was that he had not passed away. Now, even though this view seems really strange, this actually occurred multiple times in the past: you had [Shia] saying he never died, he's [in occultation], he's going to come back. When [Ja'far] passed away, the same thing: he never died, he's going to come back. When [Musa] passed away, the seventh [Imam], you had a group of people saying he never passed away, he's going to come back. So this idea was actually brought up multiple times in [Shia] history; it's not a oneoff thing. This idea of he's the [Mahdi], he's disappeared, he's going to come back later in the future, he never really died, that has been repeated so many times, so it's just going to be another one of those. So what they opted for was this other explanation, which is: he's got a baby and the baby is hidden.",), "Farid Responds, “Why Shias believe the Mahdi is 1200 years old”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bT7GyUntorM", ((104, "1:44–3:01"), (181, "3:01–3:26"), (208, "3:28–3:59"), (239, "3:59–5:18"), (335, "5:35–6:30"), (395, "6:35–7:30"),))
// U FR089:291-319 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("You had 13 different [Shia] sects emerging, because they just had no idea what to do. Some of them actually believed in [Ja'far]; they're like, the [Imam] transferred to the brother, even though they had major issues. Some of them disbelieved in [Askari]; they're like, he has no son, therefore we need to stop believing [Hasan Askari].",), "Farid Responds, “Sunni Shia Discussion on Imam Mahdi Existence”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qg_rjmviNvs", ((817, "13:37–14:03"),))
// U FR056:172-253 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Now, as strange as that sounds, there's a coincidence that occurred that allowed them to push for this argument, and this has to do with an inheritance issue. So what happens next is, [Askari], remember the 11th [Imam], he has no kid. There's no kid whatsoever, and there's no proof of this kid ever existing. If you're going to do authentic isnad analysis, the tax collectors are the only people claiming there's a kid: \"Hey, I want some money for the hidden Imam; no one can see him except for me, but just trust me bro\" kind of thing.", "So what happened was, he had written in his will that a third of his estate goes to his mom; her name is [Hudayth]. So she gets word that the brother, remember [Ja'far], also wants a piece of estate, and according to law he is supposed to receive, I think, maybe even more than a [sixth]. So what she did was she went to meet the [caliph], and they had this huge financial dispute. According to inheritance law, if [Hasan Askari] has a son, the brother doesn't get anything. Remember, there was an issue with the brother, and then you have these taxpayers going back and forth and fighting, and it's very likely that [Askari] and his brother [Ja'far] hated each other, never got along. In any case, the mother takes this whole thing to court and she says he does have a son.", "So what the [caliph] does, or the governor or whatever, is that they seize the servant, [Askari's] servant or slave; they seize her and they lock her up just to check if she's actually pregnant or not. She's locked up, I don't know, for two years, maybe even more, and then they were certain that there was no baby. This is a strange one, a side note that [Ibn Hazm] includes, that this financial dispute took seven years to end: a very random piece of information to be found in the Sunni Source. But long story short, by claiming that there is a son, the brother is secluded from The Inheritance, and it's just split between the mother and this alleged son that never really existed.",), "Farid Responds, “Why Shias believe the Mahdi is 1200 years old”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bT7GyUntorM", ((453, "7:33–8:34"), (514, "8:34–10:04"), (608, "10:08–11:15"),))
// U FR089:158-272 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("The strongest evidence is preserved by these authors: third Century, fourth Century, fifth century, they're supposed to have the evidence. Forget about anyone coming after: you even have those Works, they're early enough, contemporaneous to the actual agents or the emissaries of Muhammad [ibn Hasan Askari]. There should be strong proof, and it's a major problem to have [Shia] today believing an infallible guided [Imam] that's appointed by Allah, that's been alive for 1,200 years, and he's just nowhere to be found and he's inaccessible.", "Him being alive for that long isn't the unreasonable belief; believing that someone was alive for that long even though there is insufficient proof of his existence, that's the problem. You should not be believing in someone if you can't prove that he's ever existed.", "There's no evidence that he was born; that's my view, that's what the sunnis believe. Shia obviously believe differently. Most [Shia] actually did not believe in his existence, and then with time the other [sects] died out.",), "Farid Responds, “Sunni Shia Discussion on Imam Mahdi Existence”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qg_rjmviNvs", ((524, "8:44–9:50"), (624, "10:24–11:11"), (724, "12:04–12:32"),))
// U FR089:328-465 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("The main people benefiting from the existence of Muhammed [Hasan] are the agents that are collecting money in his name, and they're the proof of his existence. Imagine an [Imam] says to you: the proof that I'm an [Imam] is my father appointed me. Isn't that a problem? We're supposed to go to the father directly; the father appoints the son. But in this case we're having the [Imam] appoint himself by saying my father appointed me. This wouldn't fly in [Shiism]. Similarly here: the proof of [Muhammad ibn Hasan] existing is, I saw him; now give me your money.", "I would like people that are impartial, that were not collecting, to have seen. I would like evidence of family members affirming his existence; instead we have some family members denying his existence. So there are too many problems with this claim.", "We're not asking for large chunks of biographical information. From what I understand there's no evidence that he even existed. So I'm not saying that you should have as much biographical information; at least he was born, people saw him, they corroborated that they saw him: you should be able to prove at least that.", "On the topic of persecution, if you look into the classical Scholars and their statements, all of them say that [Muhammad ibn Hasan] is hidden out of fear of being killed. They all say that, implying that the [Abbasid] government are out to get him. There's no [Abbasid] government anymore. He's supposed to be available, he's supposed to be accessible; there's no reason for him to not be accessible.",), "Farid Responds, “Sunni Shia Discussion on Imam Mahdi Existence”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qg_rjmviNvs", ((930, "15:30–16:23"), (1022, "17:02–17:46"), (1229, "20:29–20:54"), (1254, "20:54–21:23"),))
// U FR119:268-409 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("Now here's where it gets really interesting: \"the Earth shall not be left without an apparent Imam\"; \"the Earth shall not be left without an apparent or hidden Imam.\" Any idea what's going on here? Once upon a time you had a lot of early [Shiite sects], and they all believed that there is a hidden Imam. The first of these seem to be [Mansuriyya]: they believe that he's [in occultation], he's hidden and he's going to come at the end of times in order to spread Justice. And then you have the [Kaysaniyya]; they also had a similar belief, that he is going to be coming at the end of times and he is going to spread Justice in the world, and he did not die. And then you had the followers of [Abdullah ibn] Jafar, if I recall correctly, and they had the same belief about him. So all these different early Shiite sects, they all believed that a specific Imam was hidden and that he's going to come at the end of times and that he's going to be spreading truth and Justice in the same way that falsehood was spread through the world.", "Now this was such a problematic idea that the first narration was made in order to combat that idea. So [al-Sadiq] says the Earth shall not be left without an apparent Imam: he's just affirming that all these previous alleged divinely appointed imams, They're All Dead, they've been buried, and they're not going to be coming back in this life.", "However, something happened: you had the death of the 11th Imam, and he did not have a son. So you had the mainstream [Imamis], the mainstream [Shia], all of a sudden finding themselves in the situation they have to accept this idea of a [hidden] Imam, and then they came up with this really ingenious idea. They said: we do have an Imam; he was born, a few people saw him, and now he's hidden.", "The person who I was quoting earlier, major Shia scholar, was speaking about this specific idea. He says: well, what's the difference between these imams and the 12th Imam? Because that's how I would argue against: I would say, look at all these people, there were so many alleged [Mahdis], and they're all dead, but you had all these groups claiming that they're hidden and that they're going to appear at the end of times. So what's the difference between you and them? Because you also have the same belief, that he's hidden and he's been hidden for a thousand two hundred years. \"Well, there is a clear difference between our [Mahdi] and all those people on that list.\" And his answer was: we know for a fact those people were dead; they've all been buried, they've all been watched and they've been put into the ground, so we all have observed this, and this is something that cannot be denied, and no one can say that about the 12th Imam.", "Of course, the issue is no one can confirm that the 12 Imam was ever born. What they did was they took a major weakness in shiaism: this idea of the twelve Imam is a major weakness, because we really don't have enough evidence he was born. His uncle denied his existence. His uncle jaffar said that my brother Hassan does not have a son; he never had the son, when this idea was floating around. But this same weakness was used by [Sharif] to argue: since we don't have enough information about his death, we can be sure that he is [in occultation]. That's the difference between us and everyone else.",), "Farid Responds, “Evolution of Shia Theology”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E14dS0zF-UA", ((764, "12:44–14:42"), (884, "14:44–15:21"), (923, "15:23–16:06"), (969, "16:09–17:36"), (1057, "17:37–18:24"),))
// U FR056:254-338 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("One more really fascinating thing that I should not Overlook is that this is actually a copycat of something that occurred earlier in history. This actually happened before: the sixth, his son, his name is called Abdullah. At one point Abdullah was the most popular candidate for imama. It's not [Musa]; today, they all follow [Musa]; at the time [Abdullah] was the most popular. When he passed away he didn't have a kid, so his followers, they claimed he had a kid, and his name was Muhammad Abdullah, and he's someone that never existed. They actually created a son, and they said he went to Yemen; no one has access to him. It actually lines up pretty nice with the [Sunni hadith] that predates all of this, where the Prophet, peace be upon Him, says the [Mahdi's] name is like my name, his father's name is like my father's name. So Muhammad [bin Abdullah]: that [lined up] really well. And that idea may have took off for a while; it didn't really last too long.", "This second idea of [Hasan] having a son, thus the 12th Imam, that idea struggled actually for quite a bit, and you have the early Scholars saying barely anyone believes this. Imagine this: in the early 300s he says that barely anyone believes this anymore, but he's arguing for it; he's trying to convince the Shia's. Compare it to today: today there's certainty by the [Shia] community that this is just a fact. Back then they were just leaving [Shiism]; they were becoming [Zaydis]. And that was the solution: make up this kid and claim that he exists. The LIE has fooled 160 million people, unfortunately.", "Everything I've mentioned today is very easy to argue for. It's not like the meme with the conspiracy guy, the lines; it's quite direct. We want people to wake up; we want people to stop believing in someone who doesn't exist.",), "Farid Responds, “Why Shias believe the Mahdi is 1200 years old”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bT7GyUntorM", ((696, "11:36–12:59"), (779, "12:59–14:10"), (878, "14:38–14:58"),))

