#passage(("That's COMPLETELY false. To claim that every single Companion died and they disappeared or something: if you mean they died like normal human beings, Yes, they did die like normal human beings. But to claim that the Companions of Prophet Muhammadﷺ all died in the battlefield, That is a complete nonsense that anyone can refute. We have many, many Companions that lived into very, very late ages After the death of Prophet Muhammadﷺ, many Companions like Anas ibn Malik And other Companions of The Prophetﷺ, the sons of those Companions also being alive. But the fact is, after The Prophetﷺ, all the Arabian peninsula was a Muslim peninsula, And it was like a hundreds and thousands Muslims within there. So to claim that all of the Companions of The Prophetﷺ died in the battlefield Is completely false within every historical tradition and everyone who opens any historical book.", "Any Islamic historical book: the first caliphate of Islam Was a Companion; second caliphate, third caliphate, fourth caliphate, fifth caliphate, They were all Companions of The Prophet! And there were tens of years, some of them, after The Prophetﷺ: They were Companions of The Prophetﷺ, and they were alive. And within the ages of all of these Companions that were rulers, that were caliphates, Within their ages, there were Companions living as well. In fact, there's something quoted often about Khaled bin alWalid, Who we call \"The sword of Allah\", who was fighting against the Roman and Persian empires, Who was, you can say, the greatest warrior in the Islamic history after The Prophet in that period: They say that he died on his deathbed. So he was fighting all these battlefields and he died on his deathbed.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((3731, "1:02:11–1:03:07"), (3794, "1:03:14–1:03:55"),))

