#passage(("In Surat alAhzab, this verse clearly talks about the wives of the Prophet. Not only that, the Quran speaks generally in the Arabic language in masculine terms, referring to both genders; this is how it works. Or then we remove all the commands of the Quran, or women don't have to do anything? Because all of the commands, most of the commands are this. It's a general way of speaking. But the thing is, not only that, but the context. The context starts with what? The verse that they bring is verse:33, I believe. So the context starts by Allah saying (alAhzab33:32-33) (Ô wives of the Prophet, you are not like..). Then Allah starts to tell them what to do, All in the feminine: Establish the prayer and give charity, all these things. Then Allah says (Allah wants to purify you Ahl alBayt).", "Let me be very, very nice with the Shi'as that would like to have a nice discussion: No problems, \"It's about Ahl alBayt\". Ok, let's say it's not about the wives, it's about Ahl alBayt. Where does it say \"follow\" there? Nowhere! It just says that Allah is going to purify them. Done! Allah says the same thing about the Companions, by the way, In Surat alAnfal (8:11) in the Quran. So Allahﷻ is just saying that He will bring water, or rain, so He can purify you with it, That Allah will remove from you the رِجز, which is the impurity of Satan. So it's mentioned there about the Companions. So it's about purity and purification. The verse isn't saying \"follow\". So, are there any other verses you can bring in Quran that says follow Ahl alBayt, Which is the creed of Shi'a, you have to follow them? So, the first belief of the Shi'a that we found, there's nothing in the Quran that shows we need to do that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Fully Dismantles The Shia Position! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t7vhKiTrvNA", ((840, "14:00–15:11"), (915, "15:15–16:30"),))
