#passage(("At least sunnis and Shia and those familiar with this discussion are familiar with the [Saqifa]. The prophet passes away, and you have the [Ansar] gathered. [Abu Bakr] [and Umar] [and Abu Ubayda] rush in; they're from, of course, the [Muhajirun], they're from the tribe of the Prophet, and they approach the ansar. And again, this is Medina; they're in Medina. And when they do that, the people submit and basically pledge allegiance.", "Now the question is: why didn't the ansar pledge allegiance to Ali? Because the [Shia] claim is that the prophet, peace be upon Him, appointed Ali as his [caliph], and he did this a few months before he passed away. 10,000 people saw this and were aware of this. And then of course keep in mind these are sincere people. This is something that you of course understand very clearly even if you're not familiar with [Sirah], but when you're familiar with the [Sirah] it becomes a lot clearer, because you're dealing with two major tribes, two Arab tribes that are in the city, that have been going to war for 150 years with one another. They believe in the prophet, peace be upon them; they stop fighting with one another; they have the prophet, peace be upon him, as their leader; they appoint him as their leader. The prophet, peace be upon Him, is now in Medina (at the time [Yathrib], and then became Medina), and they have him, an outsider, as their leader. And because they accept Muhammad and his message, now they're in danger. They eventually end up going to war with multiple Arab tribes, Pagan tribes, polytheistic tribes. At one point they were at the brink of annihilation, when they were surrounded 3 to1, and Subhan Allah, they survive, they succeed, and in a short time span, in 10 years, you have monotheism spread. Of course, this is the will of Allah; however, Allah facilitated that to happen through the [Ansar]. So these people, they're extremely sincere, and their acceptance of Islam and their following of the obligations that were brought down and revealed... there's absolutely no reason to doubt their sincerity.", "So now I can understand coming to the conclusion of Abu Bakr stole the caliphate. I can understand you believing that Ali was appointed. But why would the [Ansar], who are clearly sincere, when [Abu Bakr] says the caliph is supposed to be [with Quraysh], why didn't [they] say: yeah okay, it's supposed to be with [Quraysh], but it's specifically supposed to be with Ali; the prophet didn't just say it's supposed to be with [Quraysh], he said it's supposed to be with Ali from [Quraysh]? Why didn't they say that? If the ansar are not going to be rulers, if they're going to submit to [Quraysh], why not be under the person that was actually appointed by the prophet, peace be upon him?", "So due to this you find [Shia] having this negative opinion of the [Ansar], a negative opinion towards the [Sahaba] in general, because you have, what, 10,000 people allegedly being aware of Ali allegedly being the successor of the Prophet, peace be upon Him; however, they all go the other way and choose [Abu Bakr] and elect him. What a betrayal! But what is the wisdom behind this betrayal? What are the ansar gaining? And of course keep in mind, Abu Bakr isn't from a large subtribe. No, on the contrary, he's from [Banu Taym]. [Banu Taym] was so weak that Abu Bakr could not be defended when they were oppressed, and he had to leave to [Abyssinia]. It was on the way, when he was leaving Mecca, actually after he left Mecca, someone from a different tribe decided to defend Abu Bakr. So with all that in mind, what do the ansar have to gain from appointing [Abu Bakr]?", "The second thought has to do with [Sunnis] rewriting history: the Victor [writes] their own history, or so they claim. Alhamdulillah, one great thing about Islam in general, and especially Sunnah, [is it] preserves all sorts of histories: the histories of the winners and the losers, the truthful ones and the liars. You'll find Muslims in general quoting narrations from trustworthy narrators and narrations from liars, and that's why you'll find a very comprehensive history, sometimes with conflicting details. What you got to do is you got to do the homework and look into the reports. But if [Sunnis] wanted to rewrite history, you know what sunnis would have done? They would have Rewritten it to make it undeniable that [Abu] Bakr was appointed. Why don't you have these clearcut reports with authentic chains that say [Abu Bakr] was appointed as the Calif? You don't have that, and that's because they were truthful with their history; it's because they cared about their history enough to preserve it correctly, one; and two, there's no manipulation in that, and they kept it just the way it occurred. So that's a reason to trust the [Sunni] Narrative.",), "Farid Responds, “Abu Bakr VS Ali bin Abi Talib”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UzAxaLGR2fc", ((79, "1:19–2:23"), (148, "2:28–5:06"), (306, "5:06–6:09"), (369, "6:09–7:37"), (463, "7:43–9:17"),))
