#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML136:544-634 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("It's always the same thing with the Shias: all of these sound effects and \"Ooh, look!\", and they're trying to get your emotional state, and they IGNORE parts of the narration. They're trying to present: \"Oh, look at this magnificent thing that is happening\", and how he's looking left or right, trying to make it seem more than what it actually is.", "He says: \"biggest riot\", \"caused the Prophetﷺ ('Alayhi ssalatu wa ssalam: Peace and blessings be Upon Him) to be angry\", \"kicked out of the house\". All of it, which are things that don't exist in the narration, and none of which are true, even though he read the narration himself. But this is the play and the plot that they play with you: they try to MISREPRESENT actual events and what takes place, and they try to play this game with emotions, and you don't actually notice what the narration is saying.", "So what is the narration actually saying? How do we explain this narration? The narration is saying: when the Prophetﷺ, his condition has gotten worse; this was before his death. It was on a Thursday, very, very few days before the death of the Prophetﷺ, and he's gotten very sick and very ill that time. And then the Companions were there around the Prophetﷺ. So the Prophetﷺ, even though he's very ill, he said: (Bring me a piece of paper and I will write for you something that you will not be misguided after I write you that thing).", "But it also mentions the part that he's conveniently leaving out: it also mentions the presence of ALL the other Companions. Those other Companions who were there were different groups of people. And then it ALSO MENTIONS that the people have disagreed. So there were two groups: there was a group of people that agreed with 'Umar, when 'Umar said: (Prophetﷺ is seriously ill).", "So 'Umar, out of his love for the Prophetﷺ, and out of his respect for the Prophetﷺ, and out of his care, because he sees the Prophetﷺ is seriously ill and sick, and he doesn't want to burden him with all of these issues and matters. And he believed that the Prophetﷺ said that, not like an OBLIGATION to do it right now. The Prophet is saying it, but he's ill and sick, and 'Umar is thinking: OK, the Prophetﷺ, when he gets better, no problem, we can bring a piece of paper. But right now he's ill and sick, so let's move away. Let's not do that. Let's not bother the Prophetﷺ. That's all 'Umar is saying. But they will turn this thing into: Oh! He's disobeying the Prophet, and all of this stuff.", "So 'Umar Ibn alKhattab here, out of care for the Prophetﷺ, he says that we have the book of Allah. We have it, and it's sufficient for us. So there was a group of people who were of the same opinion of 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab, and there was another group of people who were against that. So, because they disagreed and their voices were loud, they disturbed the Prophetﷺ, who was already ill. So he said, leave me, or go away. He didn't say \"Get out of my home\", like \"kicked out of the house\", as he tries to push. No! He just said, leave me, because he's sick, he's ill, and they're fighting. Prophetﷺ told them to bring this piece of paper, but they are fighting and disagreeing with each other instead of doing that. So the Prophetﷺ told them to leave him. That's it! That's what the narration is saying.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| The Quran Has Multiple Errors?! Omar And The Pen & Paper Incident| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Q0bo07LFNs", ((1835, "30:35–30:59"), (1909, "31:49–32:12"), (1935, "32:15–32:44"), (1972, "32:52–33:05"), (1990, "33:10–33:41"), (2026, "33:46–34:30"),))
// U ML223:1015-1060 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("So just another point is the \"pen and paper\". We keep mentioning it, especially with the people of Shi'a, because they love to mention it. We say: Prophetﷺ was in a state of sickness. You can be in a state of sickness and your senses are not 100% there. So 'Umar Ibn Alkhattab did not want to give the book to the Prophet when he's not in his senses, in which the Prophet could write something when he is not in his sense.", "And the Prophet did not later on say, how could you do this? How could you disobey? If it was wrong and Haram, it's an obligation upon the Prophet to correct it, because he's a Messenger of God. There's no way you know about the event, but the Prophetﷺ that was in the event didn't know about the event. So the event was known, because not only 'Umar Ibn alKhattab was there. The other Companions would also say, this is haram, this is wrong, how could you say this?", "So the thing is, we cannot take one isolated event and extract it off the Prophet's life. If that individual is bad or evil, Allah would have told him, like he told him the names of the منافقون Munafiqoon (hypocrites). He would have been punished by the Prophet if he was doing evil around him.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Fully Dismantles The Shia Position! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t7vhKiTrvNA", ((2725, "45:25–45:54"), (2757, "45:57–46:30"), (2837, "47:17–47:28"),))
