#passage(("Now, someone can come and say: the Prophet is saying he's going to write something, that must be something very, very important. We will say to you that whatever the Prophetﷺ was going to write is not a new Revelation. What's the evidence for that? Few things. First, the verse of the Quran was already revealed where Allah said (alMa'idah5:3) ٱلۡیَوۡمَ أَكۡمَلۡتُ لَكُمۡ دِینَكُمۡ (Today I have perfected for you your religion). And the Prophet told us that everything is perfected, is completed. And the Prophet was about to die: this was a Thursday, and the Prophet is going to die on Monday, so three or four days later. So there was no new Revelation that was there, because the religion was perfected.", "But not only that, Allah explicitly commands the Prophet to deliver the Revelation. Allah says in the Quran (alMaidah5:67): (O Prophet, deliver the Message from your Lord). So the Prophet, his job and his Message, his mission was to deliver the Message. So if you make the claim that whatever that was going to be written down was new Revelation, then you accuse the Prophet of not delivering the Revelation. Why? Because the Prophet died on a Monday, and this was on a Thursday. So the Prophet had plenty of time to give us that Revelation. If it was important, if it was something that needed to be delivered, Prophet would have written it down later on, next day or the day after, to someone else. He would have told it to someone else other than the people that were present at that time. So that shows that this specific thing was not necessarily Revelation.", "And when 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab said that: (We have the book of Allah, it's sufficient to us), he's using this verse of the Quran: (the religion has been perfected and completed). He knows that there is nothing else, no other Revelation that is going to be delivered. So whatever is going to be written down is not new Revelation that is coming down for Muslims to see. It's nothing new! So yeah, as the Prophet said: (It's something that you will not be misguided). But the Prophet told us, for example: (That I left for you the Quran and Hadith, that you're not going to get misguided after them if you hold on to them). Even the Shia have this narration about the Quran and أهلُ البَيت (Ahlul Bayt: Family of the Prophet), and you're not going to get misguided after them. So the Prophet has given us many things when he said, if you stick to this, you're not going to get misguided after it.", "And 'Umar knows that Allah says in the Quran (alNahl16:89) تِبْيَـٰنًۭا لِّكُلِّ شَىْءٍ (As a clarification for everything). So what 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab is doing right now, he's using these verses and understanding. He knows already that all the Revelation has been revealed. And therefore, whatever the Prophet is going to give us is not new Revelation. It's not a matter of urgency. And he wanted to wait until the Prophet gets better, and then he's able to give us that. Now, this is a correct understanding.", "And whichever other understanding you want to take, you can say that \"('Umar) clearly disobeyed the Prophetﷺ and he didn't want to listen to him\", and you ignore the fact that it was all of these groups of Companions and they disagreed with each other, but you pin it all on 'Umar. You can make that deduction. But if it was the case, why didn't the Prophet punish him the next day? Friday? Why didn't he punish him on Saturday? Why didn't he punish him on Sunday? Why didn't he tell someone else that, \"'Umar disobeyed me, and all of those people disobeyed me, punish them\"? Why didn't he tell that thing which you believe that he must have said? And like some Shias say, he's talking about 'Ali becoming the Khalifa, etc. If it was that important, why didn't he deliver it after? And that shows you that this is clearly your misunderstanding, and you're trying to make it bigger than it is, because of your HATRED for the Companions of the Prophetﷺ.", "But what's interesting is that in صُلح الحُدَيبِيَة (Treaty of alHudaybiya), the same thing -if you want to say \"disobey the Prophet\"- the same thing happened with 'Ali Ibn Abi Talib رَضِيَ ٱللَّٰهُ عَنْهُ (Radiya Allahu 'anh: May Allah be pleased with him). There was the Sulh of Hudaibiyah: the Prophet was writing this Treaty of Hudaybiya, and the Prophet said: (Write \"From the Prophet Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah\"). So the disbeliever person said: \"Remove the Messenger of Allah. We don't believe you're the Messenger of Allah, so don't write in the treaty: the Messenger of Allah. If we believed you're the Messenger of Allah, we wouldn't fight you.\" So then the Prophet said to 'Ali, who was writing, (Remove these words, \"Messenger of Allah\"). So Ali said: \"No\". 'Ali didn't want to remove them, and the Prophetﷺ, he came himself and removed them.", "So can we bring this same understanding? Oh! Ali disobeyed the Prophetﷺ, he didn't want to listen? No! He, out of honor and respect for the Prophetﷺ, he didn't want to remove the words (Messenger of Allah), because he believes he's a Messenger of Allah, and he knows that the person who’s requesting it is a disbelieving person. OK, that doesn't negate the fact that the Prophetﷺ said (remove it), which is an order. But he understands. He took from his understanding that this command is not something like \"death or life\" command, and honoring the Prophetﷺ, he saw that as a priority. So he took that SAME exact thing with 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab.", "Now, by the way, this narration is not only in our books as people of the Sunnah. This narration of 'Ali refusing to remove \"RasulAllah\" also exists in the books of the Shia. It exists in the Mufeed: Shaikh alMufeed narrates it in alIrshad, page:63. You also find it in Tabarsi. He mentions it in his book, مجمع البيان (Majma' alBayan), in part:5, page:118. So you have all of these sources. And I can bring you more sources, these are just a couple of them, where you have Shias mentioning this and saying this incident happened. And they explain it. They say that this is honor of 'Ali Ibn Abi Talib, and he loved the Prophet, etc. So we do the same thing with 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab.", "So don't play with two different scales. Take everything the way it is, and understand that what 'Umar Ibn AlKhattab [did was] out of love and respect for the Prophetﷺ. And he's more beloved to the Prophet, and he loves the Prophet more than you or anyone else ever will. So stop with this theatrics and drama: \"That's not my Khalifa\", and \"If my Khalifa did this\". Ali Ibn Abi Talib did that. So are you going to reject it now? And where is all of this: \"If the Messenger of Allah says, then you say: we hear and obey\"! Why didn't you say that about 'Aliؓ? And why didn't your scholars say that? Why did they find justifications? Because they understood that that disagreement, or that not following that command, was not done out of ill intent. Rather, it was done for honor and respecting the Prophet and the Messenger of Allah,ﷺ.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| The Quran Has Multiple Errors?! Omar And The Pen & Paper Incident| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Q0bo07LFNs", ((2071, "34:31–35:02"), (2107, "35:07–35:45"), (2149, "35:49–36:24"), (2186, "36:26–36:47"), (2209, "36:49–37:34"), (2257, "37:37–38:19"), (2302, "38:22–38:53"), (2335, "38:55–39:34"), (2377, "39:37–40:16"),))
