#passage(("There's a few questions actually. One of them is: why didn't the prophet just write it? He was patient with kufr for 13 years: calling him a madman, accusing him of being a madman, his tribe, his uncle. He was patient with them, stayed with him for 13 years, repeating his message, and you're saying he couldn't do it a few days before his death? He couldn't deliver his message? Is that what you're saying? Is that the claim?", "And by the way, what I really don't understand is this assumption that this is definitely a will about Ali being the next [Caliph]. I don't understand this, because you believe that the prophet peace be upon Him appointed Ali in front of 10,000 people, and this was diffused allegedly because Omar said he's delirious, which he didn't even say. So if the prophet peace be upon Him appoints in front of 10,000 people, that has more weight than writing a will, because wills can be forged. You can't fool 10,000 people.",), "Farid Responds, “Ammar Nakshawani Accuses Umar (ra) of Denying the Will of the Prophet ﷺ”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GBO5TafF4LE", ((541, "9:01–10:07"), (607, "10:07–10:59"),))

=== 43.7 The attack on Fatima's house and her death
// NODE P11.C02.S07
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR233:1-99 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("About a week ago I put out a video. Now, this is a view that some Shiites hold, some, not all. You have major scholars rejecting this view, rejecting the claim, the false claim that Abu Bakr and Omar killed Fatima. Now, I put out this video trying to draw attention to a tweet I made about two years ago, in which I've put out this challenge: if you're going to argue this, if you're going to spend money, if you're going to produce a feature film about this, if you guys are going to keep on writing books and performing plays about this, then you need to back this up with at least one authentic [Shia] narration. It's not too much to ask. It's been two years. No one has been able to prove from Shia sources that [Abu Bakr] and Umar killed [Fatima], let alone Sunni sources.", "Now, a short while after putting that tweet out, I found this tweet by Sayid Mahdi [Modarresi]. He writes: \"Below are 30 (that's right, 30) references from mainstream Sunni sources by authoritative scholars and recognized historians describing the ambush, burning of the house of lady [Fatima], the prophet's beloved daughter, right after his death.\" That's a major claim to make.", "He wrote: \"random troll\" (I'm supposed to be the random troll), \"why don't you debate me? Show me a single reliable report.\" First of all, I didn't challenge [Sayid Mahdi] to a debate, even though if he wants to debate, I'm cool with that. But he said \"show me a single reliable report\". Now, I didn't say a single reliable report. I made it even easier: I said a single authentic Shia report. So it's supposed to be easier than just any single reliable report, because single reliable report could be Sunni. So here's what he did: \"Here are 30 clear reports from your own books.\" Well, there are a few issues here. First of all, 30 clear reports: we're going to get to that, inshallah.",), "Farid Responds, “Debunking Shia Claim: Abu Bakr and Omar killed Fatima!! [Mahdi Modarresi]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u92BmUM3rxM", ((23, "0:23–1:23"), (83, "1:23–1:58"), (133, "2:13–3:29"),))
// U FR233:170-488 | Farid Responds | 12
#passage(("Point number one: guess what, author provides no reference, so this holds absolutely no weight. He's a fourth century historian, he doesn't provide a source. This means nothing to me; it shouldn't mean anything to you. Next: he's quoting from [Ziyad] bin [Kulayb], who is not an eyewitness, so that's a report that we reject as well.", "Point number three: here we have a report going to [Aslam]. And [Aslam], the maula of Omar, was not an eyewitness, and therefore the report is disconnected. Point number four: [Ibn] Abdulbar is quoting from [Aslam]. Once again the report is disconnected. So it's the exact same report; these are not two independent sources, they're quoting the same source. Point number five: the author of [Kanz al-Ummal] is actually quoting point number three. It's the exact same thing. He actually mentions a [shin] at the end of the report, meaning he's quoting point number three. So point number five and point number three is the exact same thing.", "In point number six, this is not even a narration. So this isn't an authentic report; this isn't even a report, it's the opinion of a heretic. That's your source. Point number seven is also quoting [Nazzam]. So you have two sources quoting the same heretic, and for some reason we have them as two points, point six and point seven. I don't get this. Point number eight is actually quoting point number one, so there's no reason for this to even be repeated.", "Point number nine: Sunnah, by Ibn [Taymiyyah]. So Ibn [Taymiyyah] actually has a refutation, like a four volume refutation of Shiite beliefs and views. Seriously, you're using Ibn Tamiya as a source? If you go back the previous page, [Ibn Taymiyyah] is actually rejecting that this incident ever happened. So there goes your source.", "Point number 10: [Lisan al-]Mizan. Now this is really interesting. Now you're saying that this is a Sunni source? Is this recognized the Sunni scholar?", "So number 12: the reports in [Mu'jam] Tabarani, the report in [Tarikh], the report in [Tarikh] al-Islam, all these reports. So number 12, 13, 14, 15, 16 and 17: all of these reports, all these sources are quoting one person. So here you go: Mizan. You guys need to know this book, this very important book. It's a compilation of the names of hadith narrators, not just any hadith narrators: the weak hadith narrators. Now, if you're going to use a book like [Mizan] for evidence, there's a real issue here. That's like an artist who hates his art and chucks it into the garbage, and you're gonna go into his garbage, you find his art that he discarded, not the art that he's showing off to the world, the art that he discarded, and you pick it up from the trash and you say: ah, here you go, his art is terrible. This is where Mahdi [Modarresi] is sourcing this report. All these people, they're coming from [Ulwan bin Dawud], and [Ulwan] has been not only weakened by [Dhahabi], weakened by Bukhari, by [Ibn Yunus], and he actually quotes this report as an example of the garbage that [Ulwan] comes up with. So not really the best source, not really the best reference.", "So let's move on to points number 18, [19, 20]. Go through these reports, read them yourselves: there's nothing in there about Fatima being attacked or her baby being killed. So I don't even know why this stuff is even on the list. Point number twenty one: there are several issues here. First of all, the author, [Baladhuri], is not even reliable. Secondly, he's quoting from Maslama [bin Muharib], who's an anonymous narrator. And thirdly, he's quoting from [Taymi] and [Ibn 'Awn]; both of them were late narrators and were not there to witness the event.", "Point number 22 is actually point number 11. It's being repeated on the list. Point number 23 is also point number 11. Once again it's being repeated. I don't get this. Point number 24 is actually point number seven again. Why are these points being repeated over and over again? Is it just to make it seem like there are a lot of sources? Is that what's going on?", "Point number twenty five is a quote from [Tarikh]. There is doubt as to whether he was a Sunni in the first place. Most importantly, he doesn't provide a chain of narrations, and therefore the narration is disconnected by like three centuries or something. So once again, this is something that no one can rely upon.", "Point number 26 is from El Sheikh Muhammad al-Masrudi. Now, I've never heard of this book until today. I looked into this book itself, I looked into the author, and apparently the author is [a Shii]. The introduction of the book is written by an ayatollah called Alawi. This is the first page in the book, and it says here that this is an establishment that's based upon the teachings of Islam, represented by the book of Allah and the family, the purified household. So this is a [Shii] book.", "27 is a repetition of number six. 28 is number six again. Point number 29: in any case, either way, the report is disconnected. Now, El Johari, he's not a recognized Sunni historian; therefore we reject this report. By the way, the report isn't even authentic according to [Shii] standards as well.", "And finally, point number 31: it's a quote from [Nazzam], which is point number six. Now, point number six has been repeated again and again and again. It was first mentioned as number six, and then it was repeated in point number seven, and it was repeated under number 24, 27, 28 and 31. So this one statement was repeated six times. The quote by [Aslam] is repeated three times.",), "Farid Responds, “Debunking Shia Claim: Abu Bakr and Omar killed Fatima!! [Mahdi Modarresi]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u92BmUM3rxM", ((366, "6:06–6:47"), (407, "6:47–7:32"), (452, "7:32–8:30"), (510, "8:30–8:52"), (532, "8:52–13:10"), (799, "13:19–15:34"), (934, "15:34–16:11"), (973, "16:13–16:45"), (1005, "16:45–17:07"), (1028, "17:08–18:01"), (1097, "18:17–19:20"), (1161, "19:21–20:24"),))
// U FR171:1-433 | Farid Responds | 13
#passage(("This video is a refutation to a video that was made by four Shia polemicists who have claimed that they've refuted me in regards to my challenge. I put out a challenge in January 2019: \"Challenge to Shias: there is not a single authentic Shia hadith that says that Fatima was killed by Abu [Bakr] and/or Omar. Prove me wrong. Post your reply to this tweet. Conditions: quote one hadith only, not multiple; two, authentic chains only; three, clear and not ambiguous text.\"", "The reason why I say quote one hadith only is because there's this tactic that many employ, which is, in order to distract you and deflect from the actual challenge, what they attempt to do is they try to toss everything and the kitchen sink at you. I'm trying to keep it simple: are you able to provide one authentic hadith in which Abu Bakr or Omar kill Fatima according to Shia standards? If the answer is yes, you don't need to make 10 videos that are an hour each. All you need to do is to provide one report. You can do that in a single tweet; you don't even need to make a video. It's a very simple request.", "So two things I want to point out here. First of all, he shrugs off me requesting an authentic report, and he's like \"whatever that means\". If you're conceding that there's no such thing as an authentic report, there's no objective method at arriving at a correct historical narrative, then that concession is not in your favor. You're pretty much saying that we don't know why we believe in what we believe in. That's what you're saying. I'm surprised you're even conceding to this. Secondly, if you were to bring something like that, no, I'm not going to accept it myself. Of course, I'm a Sunni and I don't accept [Shia] reports, and that's a lengthy topic for another time. However, if you are able to, then that would mean that your belief in Fatima being killed would be at least somewhat justified. What I'm saying is: you cannot prove it from your books according to Shia standards, and you want the world to accept this as a fact? That's a stretch.", "These guys are saying it's mass transmitted, undeniable. The issue is, first of all, it's not [mutawatir]. That's the first issue. Here's a simple question, here's a straightforward question: what's easier to prove, that something is authentic through one report, through one person, or that something is mass transmitted? If you guessed single source is easier, you are definitely correct. [Mutawatir] is a lot harder to prove, which is why I made the challenge easy. I requested one authentic report, not multiple, not mass transmitted. I'm not setting the bar that high. I'm keeping it simple for you guys. I want an authentic report from the imam. And you see, even that, I'm helping you guys out: I'm not asking for an eyewitness report from a companion of the prophet [peace be upon] him, or from Ali, or from al-Hasan or Hussain. I'm not asking for that. I'm saying that I do not mind if you quote a report from [Ja'far as-Sadiq] or Abu Jafar [al-Baqir] or [Zayn al-Abidin] or Ali, even though they're quite late and even though they're not eyewitnesses. You guys believe that these are the infallibles, and I will give you that. If you can provide a single report from one of your infallibles that said that Fatima was murdered by Omar, and it's a clear report, I will accept that from you, and your view in my opinion will be justified. Luckily, alhamdulillah, these guys came together, four of their best polemicists in the English language, and they've come up with multiple reports.", "He's quoted a very, very famous hadith in Kafi in which Fatima is described to be [siddiqa]. Now the issue with this is: this conflicts with what I've mentioned in my challenge, which is a clear report, not an ambiguous report. And the reason why this is ambiguous is because it uses the word shahida. Now, here's why this is problematic: the term shahid doesn't necessarily mean martyr. It doesn't necessarily mean that you were killed. For example, we find in Surat al-Hadid: \"And those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, they are the sincere lovers of truth and the witnesses who testify.\" And the word used for shaheed is \"the witnesses\". That is literally what a shahid is: a witness. It doesn't mean that every single believer is a martyr. Similarly, you will find the same understanding: the word shahid in this context doesn't necessarily mean that they're all martyred.", "We find this report also in Kafi, by [Abu Basir], in which he's quoting the imam saying: \"O Abu Muhammad, the dead ones from among you who were upon this matter is the martyr.\" I said: \"Even if he died upon his bed?\" He said: \"Yes, by Allah, even if he died upon his bed, he is alive in the presence of his Lord.\" We understand from this report that every single [Shii] is a shahid. So my response to these guys is that referring to Fatima as a shahidah isn't evidence that she was killed.", "Now let's carry on to the second report. There are a few issues with this book. First of all, the author of the book is quoting a late narrator, a very, very, very late narrator, and his name is Abu Abdullah. Now, this person passed away in the year 447. So the author of this book is a student of someone who passed away in the 5th century. There's an issue with the book: if you open it up from the beginning, it says that this author is someone who passed away in the fourth century, which is quite strange. Now, what's even more problematic is what we do know about Muhammad bin [Jarir al-Tabari]: that he actually died in the third century. [Ibn Jarir] is a student of [Sufyan] bin [Waki'], who passed away in the middle of the third century. So [Ibn Jarir] passed away towards the end of the 3rd century. I know it's a bit confusing, but please bear with me.", "Now, some very reasonable, intellectual [Shii] scholars like [al-Tustari] came to the conclusion that this book was misattributed to Muhammad [bin Jarir]. However, some other scholars came to a very unreasonable conclusion. They said that there are two people with the same name: they're both called [Abu Jafar], they're both called Muhammad [bin Jarir] bin Rustam, they're both from [Tabaristan], they're both Tabari, and they're both Shiis, and they both wrote a book about imam. Now, that's really unlikely, especially since the younger, the late Muhammad [bin Jarir], who allegedly passed away in the 5th century, doesn't have any biography. You don't find classical [Shii] scholars writing a passage for him in their books of [rijal]. They only write one for the one; they don't mention this other Muhammad bin [Jarir bin Rustam]. So this second person doesn't exist. So that is a major issue that you have with this hadith.", "By the way, these are the actual strongest reports that [Shias] rely on to prove that Fatima was murdered. You'd expect something better, right? But this is the best stuff they have.", "What I find interesting is: after he quotes the report, he mentions Rouhani's view in regards to [Mufaddal] bin Omar, who's a very notorious character in [Shii] books. Here's the thing: you're putting forward the statement of Rohani; I'm putting to you the statement of the imam. Now, the imam, this is [Ja'far as-Sadiq], he says: \"What do you want with my son? Do you want to get him killed? Do you want to kill him?\" Now, I think, in my opinion, the statement of [Ja'far as-Sadiq] weighs just a little bit more than Rouhani's view. Of course, I'm not just basing my opinion on what [Ja'far as-Sadiq] says; we even have major Shiite scholars of [rijal]: he's weakened severely.", "Those are the three narrations that these individuals have brought forward, and they do not have a strong argument for the martyrdom of [Fatima]. What I find really interesting is, you can actually come to a conclusion using authentic [Shii] reports in regards to what happened to Fatima, believe it or not, and guess what, it's nothing about her dying due to some sort of attack. Here is a report. This is from [al-Kafi], and this is an authentic hadith. This report comes through [Ali bin Ibrahim], from his father, from Ibn [Abi Umayr], from Hisham bin Salim. It has been narrated from Abu Abdullah: I heard him saying: \"Fatima lived after her father for 75 days.\" So [Abu Bakr] comes to power, they attack the house of Fatima, and then she dies 75 days later? Something doesn't make sense. If she's going to die, she's going to die immediately, not 75 days later.", "\"Fatima lived after her father for 75 days, not being seen smiling or laughing. She would come to the graves of the martyrs twice during every Friday, Monday and Thursday.\" What's meant here is she would actually go to the graves four times, twice on Friday. It's about an hour and 15 minutes away; that's if you're walking, that's using Google Maps. And no, that's not something you'd do if you had a broken rib. You wouldn't go to the graves four times a week. Imagine someone with a broken rib going to the graves of the shuhada four times a week and telling people that's where the prophet peace be upon him was and that's where the mushrikeen were. Does that make sense? It doesn't seem like those are the actions of someone that's on the verge of death. Those actions seem to be the actions of someone who's perfectly healthy.", "In any case, I do understand why you hold those views. The unjustified belief that Fatima was killed: it conflicts with reason. We know that Ali pledged allegiance to [Abu Bakr] after Fatima passed away. We know that in the khilafah [of] Omar, Ali gave his daughter to [Omar]. We know that Omar, when he was on the deathbed, appointed Ali as a possible successor to the throne and to rule the Muslim ummah. Does that seem to be the actions of someone who just killed his wife a few years earlier? It just doesn't make any sense.",), "Farid Responds, “Fatima was NOT Killed in Shia hadith!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dgBUipGEp3w", ((2, "0:02–0:42"), (49, "0:49–1:35"), (111, "1:51–3:05"), (222, "3:42–5:44"), (382, "6:22–7:34"), (457, "7:37–8:13"), (494, "8:14–10:18"), (618, "10:18–11:42"), (713, "11:53–12:12"), (838, "13:58–15:16"), (933, "15:33–16:52"), (1016, "16:56–18:24"), (1104, "18:24–19:27"),))

=== 43.8 The sincerity of Abu Bakr and Umar
// NODE P11.C02.S08
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR019:1-96 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("It's a narration that the vast majority of you guys have not come across. I just came across it for the first time just a few days ago. And one of the reasons why is this narration cannot be found in one of the six books. It's something I just stumbled upon completely accidentally. The isnad is completely authentic. It's upon the conditions of Bukhari and Muslim. It's a wonderful Hadith with this amazing, fascinating twist in the end. And Subhanallah, you see how Islam has affected people.", "This is from Humaydi, Bayan al-Sahihayn, in which Tariq bin Shihab narrates that the people of Buzakha, or delegation from Buzakha, from the tribes of Asad and Ghatafan, came to Abu Bakr asking him for a peace treaty. Now, just a bit of a background here. Buzakha is a place in Najd where you had these tribes that gathered together, and they actually went to war with the Muslims. Their leader at the time was al-Asadi, who claimed to be a prophet. The delegation consisted of people from Asad and Ghatafan. Ghatafan, of course, lengthy history of them going to war with the Muslims: they were against the Muslims in the Battle of the Trench as well. Tulayha al-Asadi, the person who claimed to be a prophet, was from Banu Asad.", "So they asked for a peace treaty, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, gave them two choices. He says, \"You can either choose the devastating war or the humiliating peace. These are your two choices.\" So they said, \"We know the devastating war. We've been through that. What's this humiliating peace that you speak of?\" So he said, \"We take away your weapons and your armor, and whatever we had taken from you in war, we keep to ourselves, and whatever you took away from us at war, you have to return to us, and you pay the blood money for those of us that you have killed. And those of you that were killed are in hellfire. And you allow your people to just chase the camels until Allah and the caliph of the messenger and the Muhajirin decide to excuse you.\"", "Now, Abu Bakr presented this to the people as his desire, as his will, and out of all the people there, one person said that he disagreed with a part of it. And that person was Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said he would like to present it. So he says that what you've mentioned, from a devastating war and a humiliating peace, then yes, that's definitely a good idea. And that which you've said about we keeping what we took from you in war and you returning what you took from us (referring to Banu Assad and Ghatafan), then yes, that's a great idea as well, and Omar is upon that. As for the last clause, that they pay the blood money and that their dead are in hellfire: then those that died from our people, those that were killed, the Muslims that were killed, they were killed due to them following the orders and commands of Allah. And their compensation will be from Allah. We do not accept blood money. And then the people agreed with what Omar said, and that is how the peace was made.", "The compensations wouldn't be small. Compensations sometimes would range from 10 camels per person that was killed to 50. If it was someone of rank, it could go up to 100, 200, 1,000. So such a thing would be debilitating to the tribe that has to pay the blood money, and it would be a massive boost to the finances of those who've had relatives that were killed, or who have had troops and companions that were killed. But Omar refused. Omar was sincere. Omar was someone that was certain that the compensation would be from Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala. It doesn't need to be from the disbelievers that went to war with the Muslims. And that in itself is a beautiful sign of the sincerity of Omar and those that agreed with him.", "Even things like the sincerity of the prophet, peace be upon him, is definitely a proof of prophethood. You have all these reports about him doing these amazing things, but proving that he's sincere in his call adds, of course, to the claim of his prophethood. Similarly here, when you find his companions, the ones closest to him in proximity, through blood ties, when they are showing signs of complete sincerity, you come to the conclusion that, okay, clearly the people that were closest to him, that were most aware of his situation, truly believed in his message.", "And just to drive the point further, here's an additional report, just a part of it, in the famous reports about Omar ibn al-Khattab and how he was killed. The report actually mentions that when he used to pray, the first rak'ah, he used to sometimes read Surat Yusuf or Surat an-Nahl. Now, that in itself says something. How common is that? For the Shi'as: when you think about Omar, you're assuming that he's someone who is completely insincere. Why wouldn't he just recite Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad instead? Why would he recite something as lengthy as Surah Yusuf or Surah An-Nahl? It's because of his attachment to the Quran. It's because he was someone that enjoyed it. And there's so many reports about the sincerity of Omar. However, it's not like they're all gathered in one place. You just read narrations, and you just come across them randomly, like the way I did with the first report.",), "Farid Responds, “Only 0.004854% of Muslims know this hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x2tVOcmQI7w", ((10, "0:10–0:49"), (51, "0:51–2:02"), (128, "2:08–3:19"), (203, "3:23–5:16"), (316, "5:16–6:14"), (381, "6:21–7:01"), (421, "7:01–8:16"),))

=== 43.9 Accusations against Aisha and Hafsa
// NODE P11.C02.S09
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR100:1-460 | Farid Responds | 11
#passage(("Imagine if someone made this claim that Hitler was a cannibal, or that he used to participate in some sort of incest. The average person would be like: okay, that's strange, because I've never heard of that before. A more inquisitive person would probably say something like: do you have proof for that? Because that's not something that we know about him. And you see, even though Hitler is an evil man, not every accusation that's leveled against him is accurate. Unfortunately, in polemics people tend to believe everything that supports their biases against someone they don't like.", "So [Aisha], may Allah be pleased with her: any positive thing that she'll ever do, or that she has ever done, will be twisted to be something negative. If she gave to the poor, according to those that hate her, she's just doing it to show off. If she was in her home praying, asking for forgiveness: you see, she's asking for forgiveness because she's done these enormous sins. And if she gives the prophet peace be upon him medicine, then, well, clearly it's poison, she's trying to kill him. Subhan Allah.", "When I see accusations hurled against the wives of the Prophet peace be upon him, the mothers of the Believers, I feel obligated to respond. And these accusations include, according to [Ammar], that Allah is exposing them: yes, the wives of the Prophet peace be upon him, who he was married to and stayed married to until his death, were allegedly bad people.", "So there are a few things here that I need to emphasize, that [Ammar] doesn't seem to realize or appreciate. The prophet peace be upon him made something permissible impermissible for himself due to him seeking the satisfaction of his wives. So why is he doing that? Well, it's because he loves them. So why is the prophet doing this? Is he afraid of [Aisha]? By the way, she's 16 at the time. All this talk about the conspiracy against Islam, and she's going to demolish Islam, and all: she's 16 at the time. The second thing this verse is saying is that the hearts of the wives are inclined to repentance. If you want to condemn someone as evil, do you say, I know your heart is inclined to repentance? Third point is that [Ammar] seems to think that Allah happily wants to cause a divorce. It's not saying happily, it's saying perhaps. So no, Allah is not happy about making a divorce happen.", "Now, the famous Hadith that provides a background for this is one in which the prophet peace be upon Him used to go to the house of one of his wives, and he used to eat honey, and when he would return to some of the houses of his other wives, they would say things like: did you eat [maghafir]? which is like a foul smelling plant. Now, this is why the prophet peace be upon Him forbade it upon himself. So the question is: why are they doing this in the first place? Why are they saying \"did you eat\"? What causes a wife to say something like that to her husband? What's the motivation? Well, when wives do something like that, it's because they're jealous, and you're jealous because you like someone, because you love someone, because you want that person all to yourself, especially in a case in which you have multiple wives sharing one man. Of course, [Ammar] unfortunately looks at this report [as] something a lot more sinister.", "Now I want to move on from this point and focus on something else, which is: this information is actually coming from herself. [Aisha] is explaining the rebuking that she's receiving in the Quran, and she's making this private information public, because she knew the details of the verse, and most people weren't privy to this. Even [Ibn] Abbas, who specializes in Quran, had absolutely no idea what was going on, and he had to wait for years before one day asking, while they were coming back from [Hajj]: what are the verses speaking about? Who are these wives that the prophet peace be upon him had an issue with? So once again, this is a private matter, and if it was a public matter in the way that [Ammar] claims it was, [then Ibn] Abbas wouldn't have to wait for years to ask these questions. The only reason why you're writing their names there is because the Quran does not expose them. If I look at Quran alone, I really don't know who it's referring to.", "The footnotes aren't from [Bukhari] either; [Bukhari] doesn't write his own footnotes. But what I really find confusing is that [Ammar] actually rejects this report as well. So the first report, which is from the Sunni source that [Ammar] is quoting, which is just 300 years old: it's not a primary source. By returning to the footnotes, we find that the [author] weakens the report due to a weak narrator, as well as the [report] is disconnected. So you have two major issues with the report, and that's why no Sunni should accept the Hadith, and no [Shia] should accept the [Hadith] either. As for the [Shia] reports, even worse, because the book doesn't include chains. This report doesn't even have chains. If you believe that a report is authentic simply because it exists in Sunni and [Shia] sources, then you've opened up a huge can of worms, even though there's no serious scholar, student of knowledge, that holds on to this methodology.", "Now, the biggest problem with what [Ammar] brought in this final narration is: it actually includes the parts that [Ammar] was just rebuking and mocking previously, that were found in Sunni works, but all of a sudden he sees this as acceptable. Let's for a moment accept this report for the sake of the argument. The quote you're providing doesn't even say the prophet peace be upon him rebuked her for this. It says he rebuked her about the matter of Maria. Then it says: but he desisted from rebuking them about the other matter.", "Let's say he made a mistake by marrying them in the first place; he didn't know what was going to happen. Now he can see the signs, now he sees that they are evil people. Why would he stay married to them? What is the hikma from this? I know some people are going to be like: it's a test. But it's like a cope that you can say about anything. Young earth creationists, when speaking about dinosaur bones: it's a test from God, he put them there in order to cause you doubt. That's not really an answer.", "The direction is towards [Aisha's] house. [Abdullah] says: I saw the prophet peace be upon Him pointing towards the east. Learn to reconcile. Do you believe that the prophet peace be upon him will say she is the head [of kufr], she's the Horn of Satan and whatnot, and then he will return to her and continue his marital life with her? Would you do that? If you believe that someone is the head of the kufur, would you just stay married to them? This is childish. I often make these criticisms against Christians: that they're stuck in the '90s with their arguments against Islam, their inability to read Muslim narrations charitably. But unfortunately I'm seeing [Ammar] doing the same: no attempt at reconciliation, no attempt at being charitable, interpreting any Hadith in the worst possible way about a lot of the other companions as well.", "The Quran never exposes the wives of the Prophet peace be upon him, but rebukes them while intentionally leaving out their names. And most importantly, the prophet peace be upon Him stays married to both, because he is pleased with them both.",), "Farid Responds, “Response to Ammar Nakshawani on A'isha and Hafsa Conspiracy (may Allah be pleased with them both)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Sss7-m4gu_0", ((3, "0:03–0:53"), (56, "0:56–1:49"), (142, "2:22–2:51"), (302, "5:02–6:18"), (378, "6:18–7:29"), (480, "8:00–9:45"), (691, "11:31–16:09"), (969, "16:09–17:21"), (1052, "17:32–18:03"), (1202, "20:02–21:22"), (1297, "21:37–21:52"),))

