#passage(("The whole Martyrdom of Hussainؓ (may Allah be satisfied with him) عليه السلام (peace be upon him!): we can use any of these terms, we don't have any issues with them. And of course, we love and respect and follow all Ahlu-Lbayt of the Prophetﷺ (peace and blessings of God be upon him). And we don't believe the misattributions in a lot of the Shia books, like الكافي and ما لا يحضره الفقيه; all of these books has all of these narrations that we believe are fabricated narrations and misattributions to them. So we believe that it was a great casualty, a grave, grave, grave problem that happened in the Muslim community at that time.", "And I would recommend Brother Farid, who has a channel called “Farid Response”. He has a book on the Martyrdom of Hussainؓ. And I would say that book is very, as much as you can be, of course, objective. So I would recommend you read that book, since you're saying the story brought you closer to Islam. But an emotional reason cannot be used to be a foundation for something, because that's no different from a Christian who comes to you. I'm sure you've heard many Christians, of how they come to Christianity, and it's always like the same emotional kind of reasoning. And that cannot be the determiner of why it's true or not.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Shia Follower Gets An Unexpected Reality Check! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6v8T06xoukk", ((71, "1:11–1:40"), (104, "1:44–2:18"),))
// U ML140:77-185 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Many 25-24 year old Sunni Muslims have read a lot of Ahadith from Bukhari and Muslim and Tirmidhi and Ibn Maja, and they can say them to you. Why? Because our tradition is to teach people about God and what our Prophet taught. But the Shia tradition is about emotional stories. And I'm being completely honest with you. Someone goes on the Minbar (pulpit) and he starts telling you an emotional story, and it does attract people who fall for emotional things. They will tell you a story about 'Umar: oh, 'Umar, he kicked the door, and he burned the house, and he killed Muhsin, and all of the stories, which are complete fabrications.", "And they will go into all of these stories of: they took the Imam 'Ali, they forced him, and he had no choice except to do Taqiyyah. The Imam 'Ali, which we call a Lion in war! Which was the person who would call for the challenge: when you have a challenge in battle, when one person challenges the other, he would be the first person to go. But all of a sudden, he watched 'Umar beat his wife, burn his house, and kill his son.. Come on! And the rest of the Muslim Nation is watching the cousin of the Prophetﷺ, and they're watching this happen, but nobody's saying nothing! Come on, these emotional stories, which has nothing to do with facts and reality and reasoning, is what Shias thrive upon. Oh, the death of Hussayn ؓ: they come and they beat themselves, and these emotional stories, and everyone starts crying. The whole religion is about that! It's all about: Ok, Abu Bakr stole the inheritance of Fatima. Abu Bakr made Fatima angry. All of it is stories. The whole religion is stories of: oh, this happened! oh, that happened! and this is really sad, and this sad story. That's the whole religion of Shiism.", "Are you telling me those Khutabas (sermonisers) who go in the Minbar (pulpit), they do not talk about 'Ali bin Abi Taleb, and how the Khilafah was stripped away from him and taken away from him? Are you telling me they don't go on the Minbar and talk about Husayn and his death, and all of the persecution that happened to him, and the curses on Mu'awiyah and whatever? Let me make a challenge for you: give me 10 narrations from the books of the Shia about manners and good things from Ahlu-Lbayt. Go ahead. Ok, give me 3. So you're telling me every time you go to the masjid, you hear khutba about the virtues of Ahlu-Lbayt and all of these good morals and manners, but you don't know 3 things you can tell me?", "Tell me, those people who teach you the Quran, when they go up on Minbar, they teach you the book of Allah? I'm talking about the majority, I'm not talking about once that happens every 10 years. I'm talking about when the Imam goes on the Minbar: does he go into explaining Quran, and reading Quran? Is that what he does? What you're not getting is: this is the information in the books. The books are about the tribulations, and the gravities, and the problems that happen to Ahlu-Lbayt. The whole books of the Shia talk only about this. That's the only thing. Anyone who goes, open any book of the books of the Shia: all the narrations are talking about what I'm talking about. Not to talk about all of these narrations that talk about: oh, the Quran is actually read like this, but Ahlu-Sunnah actually changed. That's what they tell you in this Khutab.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Shia Follower Gets An Unexpected Reality Check! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6v8T06xoukk", ((278, "4:38–5:15"), (318, "5:18–6:30"), (434, "7:14–8:10"), (500, "8:20–9:57"),))
// U ML140:34-75 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("If the whole foundation of what Shiism is built upon contradicts the Quran clearly, and it goes against the teachings of Allahﷻ, clearly and explicitly, as I have shown, then we cannot trust it, because it claims the Quran is the Book of God. So if it claims the Quran is the Book of God, then how can the Book of God refute every single foundational belief in that faith? It's impossible. If that Shia narrative was all false, then the only option you have is to follow the Sunni narrative, because these books of Ahlu-Sunnah that mention hadiths, like Bukhari and Muslim or whatever, they all are attributions to the Prophet. Unlike الكافي and مَن لا يَحضُرُه الفقيه and all of that, which have like 4% or something attributions to the Prophet, many of which are weak, or most of which are weak with the Shia standards of narration, and 95% or more are attributions to alBaqir and asSadiq mainly, and the rest of Ahlu-Lbayt, which are also misattributions.", "The Islam that we follow is based on the Prophet; it's not based on anyone else, because Allah sent the Prophet as guidance for us, as the Quran teaches. Allah told us to follow the example of the Prophet (alAhzab33:21). This is what Allah commanded us to do. He was sent as an example for us. He was sent as a mercy for us (alAnbya21:107). So his life is what we need to imitate, not the life of any human being: the life of the Prophet himself is the main thing that we need to imitate and follow. The only reason we love or respect Ahlu-Lbayt is because of the Prophet anyways. If they were not from the lineage of the Prophet, then where is the virtue? So how can we neglect the Prophet, which is the main reason why they have any virtue, and focus on them and leave the Prophet? And that is the grave problem with this idea of the Shia followers, which is, as you know already, a minority of the Muslim Nation anyway: less than 13% of people are Shia. And most of these Shias are not practising Shias. Like you yourself, I am positive you have not read الكافي. You've not read ما لا يحضره الفقيه. You've not read any of these books.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Shia Follower Gets An Unexpected Reality Check! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6v8T06xoukk", ((145, "2:25–3:20"), (203, "3:23–4:21"),))
