=== 44.3 Al-Kafi, the four books and Shia hadith methodology
// NODE P11.C03.S03
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR130:101-199 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("Firstly I'd like to go into the main [Shiite] works themselves, just to give you a brief background about these works. Firstly we have [al-Kafi] in general. The first two volumes are referred to as [Usul al-Kafi]. I believe that they're seen as the main four works because of their size, and of course due to them being quite early compared to many other sources.", "What's really special about the [Kafi] is it's a book that contains many narrations about the imams: who the imams were, the evidence that these individuals were appointed by Allah, the evidence that these imams were appointed by one another and so on and so forth, and what exactly is Imam, what's the sign of Imam, ideas about [ghaybah], the disappearance of the Imam. The author of this book, he passes away in the year 329 in Baghdad.", "What I've just mentioned to you isn't a summary of who [Kulaini] was; actually it's everything we know about [Kulaini]. That's something I'd like you to consider: the amount of information that we have, for example, about Bukhari. There were volumes that were written about the man himself. We have a wealth of information about him and his life; this wealth of information is missing when it comes to [Kulaini].", "The narrations in his book aren't from the prophet, peace be upon him; a relatively small number are from him. The vast majority of the reports are from the descendants of the Prophet, peace be upon him, his great great grandsons like Jafar [Sadiq] and Muhammad al-[Baqir]. [Shias] tend to compare it in this way: they say [Sunnis], you take from the sahaba. In reality it's not really like that. In reality it's Sunni's sahaba, yes, but Shia's take from the sahaba, so we're all dealing with our own sahaba in a sense.", "Between [Kulaini] and the imams you'll find multiple people in the chain, sometimes four, sometimes five, sometimes six people and so on. What we need to do is: in the same way that we apply [rijal] standards, [Shias] also apply these standards, and they examine these chains and they study these reports and they look into the reliability of these narrators in order to determine whether a specific narration is authentic or not.",), "Farid Responds, “Why are Sunnis and Shia Different? Full Breakdown (AIM Conference)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Un_bX5Zm3o0", ((315, "5:15–6:01"), (377, "6:17–7:08"), (460, "7:40–8:25"), (513, "8:33–9:20"), (563, "9:23–9:51"),))
// U FR094:50-101 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Here's the thing about [Bukhari]: every single Hadith, with the exception of one, can be found in other sources. You can't say that; there are so many hadiths, hundreds if not thousands, that are not corroborated at all, and there are clear examples of distorting hadiths that conflict with his ideology. [Shia] make fun of Sunnis having narrations about Allah having two right hands; well, guess what, it's something that [Shia] would believe in.", "There's no reason for you to trust someone like [Kulaini]. His biography is like two paragraphs; we barely know anything about this man, but he is unfortunately seen [as] the most reliable [Shia] narrator compiler of all time, and yet he's someone who has distorted narrations of the imams themselves when they got to him.",), "Farid Responds, “Eid Message & Gift to Ammar Nakshawani”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eSbnXLfMR6w", ((161, "2:41–3:28"), (242, "4:02–4:35"),))
// U FR130:200-793 | Farid Responds | 20
#passage(("The book has 16 000 narrations, which is a relatively large number, quite large. [Kulaini] has 28 teachers in his book; from those 28 teachers, 18 of them are anonymous, we have no idea who these people are. 30% of his book is from one person. We don't really know much about [Ali bin] Ibrahim; we know he's the son of Ibrahim, who is one of the main narrators that came from Kufa to [Qom], that's pretty much it. His full biography is like two sentences or something like that, very short. To give you an idea of how different: [Sahih] Bukhari relies on around 300 teachers; I think the exact number is like 293 or something like that. So there's a big difference when it comes to the effort put into by each of these two scholars.", "There have been efforts in recent times by [al-Bahbudi], for example, a modern [Shia] scholar, to summarize [al-Kafi] and to extract the authentic narrations into a [Sahih al-Kafi], which was later on referred to as [Zubdat al-Kafi].", "One thing that's really interesting about these two works is the amount of support. [The mustakhraj] is basically a complimentary book in which the author quotes the same narrations as the original source but through a completely different chain, or from the teacher of the author. It's usually similar [chains] from what the author had; however, the author of the [mustakhraj] skips the original author. So basically, if he's someone who's unreliable or something like that, it wouldn't matter, because all of his narrations are preserved through other paths. And you have a few of these [mustakhrajs] that are printed. In any case, [al-Kafi] doesn't have that. So if we were to one day wake up and come to the conclusion that [Kulaini] is someone who's unreliable, well, there goes something like 30 percent, 40 percent of [Shia] hadiths, because [Kulaini] is that important.", "The second of the four books: [al-Saduq], he passes away in the [year] 381. He's the Sheikh of his time. His book is quite different than [al-Kafi], because he doesn't provide chains from him to the people that he's usually quoting; he'd usually mention a name, and then he has a separate [mashyakha] in which he would list his paths to these authors. He has multiple classical works, like a book that argues for the concept of [ghaybah] and occultation and the idea of the [Mahdi] still existing, because in his time, a hundred years after the disappearance of the [Mahdi], there was a lot of doubt among the Shiite masses as to whether the Mahdi existed or not. So he wrote this book to basically cause people to rest assure that they're fine and the [Mahdi], he will come out. Of course, it's been quite a bit longer.", "He has other works, like a book that seems to be quite influenced by [Mu'tazili] thoughts, because a lot of it is a negation of the classical positions that were held previously. Before, the people of [Qom] were people that affirmed the attributes of Allah; that's classical [Shiism] in [Qom]. In fact they were referred to as [mujassima] by later [Shias], which is quite interesting. And he's got a bunch of other works as well, like the books on the rewards for the good deeds and punishments for actions.", "The author of two of the other four books, he passes away in the year 460. The first is [Tahdhib al-Ahkam], which is quite large: 10 volumes, condensed into two large volumes. It's a massive book of [fiqh] and contains the vast majority, I dare say, [of] the opinions that have been shared by the imams. And the other book is really important, and basically the idea of this book is to reconcile contradictions. Since there are a lot of contradictions in Shia Hadith, of course he felt the need to reconcile these contradictions, and reconciliation can be done by simply sometimes rejecting a report, sometimes combining the meanings, sometimes saying that this is general and this is a specific matter. And of course we have the concept of [taqiyya], and the concept can be found in many places in this book, and this is a topic that I'd like to focus on somewhat in this talk.", "So what is the concept? Basically lying in the matter if your life depends on it. Someone puts a gun to your head, asks you if you're a Muslim; they're gonna blow off your head if you say yes, I'm a Muslim: it's permissible for you to lie. And [Shiism], unfortunately, this has been expanded massively, to the extent that small minor [fiqh matters] are explained away as [taqiyya]. So there are some larger [matters] and there are some smaller matters. For example, the Imam is approached, he's asked: are you an Imam, are you someone who is appointed by Allah? He would say no, I'm not. According to Shia, this is a form of [taqiyya]. However, sometimes Imam will be asked, says temporary marriage is Haram. The Shia, of course, they explain this away by saying that this is a form of [taqiyya], which is quite strange, especially in [a fiqh] matter. Will Ali be punished, will Ali be killed, for holding a [fiqh] position? Especially if it's a [fiqh] position that you find precedence for even among [Ahl al-Sunnah].", "Similarly you find many statements by the imams that are seen as [taqiyya] statements, but in reality positions that were held by Sunni scholars. So for example the wiping of the head: how are you supposed to wipe your head, are you supposed to wipe your head once or is it supposed to be twice? And then you have some scholars saying, oh, we need to reconcile these. This is an issue, because you have this difference of opinion among Sunni scholars themselves.", "So what exactly is going on here? Why does this concept exist? Well, what happened was you simply had a lot of authentic reports that are not supposed to be seen as authentic; that's simply it. And they're looking at everything: okay, all this stuff is authentic; the Imam is saying Haram, Imam is saying Halal; okay, how can this happen? Well, maybe the Imam was trying to give you two positions depending on who's asking him. Maybe it's the Sunni who's asking him: the Imam is lying for the [Sunni], he's telling the truth to the [Shia]. It's a lot of mental gymnastics when it comes to this stuff, and it's very unfortunate, because now one who holds this view, one who ascribes this view and [attributes] it to the Imam, is essentially claiming that the Imam is lying about religion, which conflicts with the reason that the Imam exists. And Imam, remember, is a divinely appointed guide, but now he's lying to you, he's lying to the Sunnis, he's telling people I'm not an Imam. So that's a major issue right there.", "And again, why is this happening? It's because of too many authentic hadiths. I'll give you an example of how something like this could happen. It may be a silly example, but you do find examples of people lying, and this is, by the way, Hadith works: people lie about food. You have fruit vendors, vegetable vendors, butchers lying about food: if you eat this you get this much [thawab], you get this much [ajr], things of that nature. Just a few weeks ago I was reading this interesting Hadith, a discussion about what's the best type of meat: the best type of meat is chicken? That's the pig of the birds; in reality the best type of meat is pigeon. So whoever came up with this seems to have been someone that sold pigeon or something like that. And you could imagine how these sorts of dynamics in society led to these conflicting reports. So you'd have someone saying this is halal, and someone else would say this is Haram, and then the second person would say, la, I heard the Imam says Haram, and so on and so forth.", "How do we solve these issues? Well, to a degree, if possible, we look into the books of [rijal]. They may not be as impressive; however, they have works nonetheless. The first of these works, and perhaps the most important of these works, is [al-Kashshi's]. The original work is lost; we have a condensed version by [al-Tusi], the same author of [al-Istibsar]. This book is the best book for someone that wants to learn about early Shia society, because it's filled with anecdotes, filled with all these examples of early [Shias] and how they react to the imams and how [they interacted] with one another, and it's filled with really interesting stories.", "What you find really interesting in this book is it's not [al-Kashshi] who was criticizing narrators and weakening narrators; it's mainly the imams themselves saying this person is going to hell, this person is going to heaven. And unfortunately what we often have is the same Imam, in one Hadith, saying this person is going to hell, in another Hadith, saying this person is going to heaven. So this was simply explained by Shia Hadith scholars that came later on, that the Imam was simply trying to throw people off, because he didn't want people to know that this person is a close friend of mine, so I'm going to tell everyone that he is going to [hell]; so now this person is safe, he's not going to be imprisoned, he's not going to be attacked by the [Abbasids] or [Banu] Umayya or whatnot; this person is safe, alhamdulillah, because I said that he's going to go to hell. So you had that sort of justification.", "In my opinion, what seems to have happened is the same thing that happened with the earlier hadiths about the chicken, the pigeon and whatnot: you had people attributing to the imams that which they did not say. So what's likelier: that the Imam had two positions about a specific person, or the Imam had one position about a specific person? Maybe the Imam simply said that this person is going to go to hell, and then other people attributed to the Imam that he said that the same person is going to go to heaven. This seems to be the likelier scenario. And a good example, for those that are familiar with the books: check out the biography of Zurara. He's one of the biggest narrators. You have a chunk of reports, they're just filled with praise, and you have another chunk of reports filled with condemnation, and if you actually study the narrators in the chains, you'll find a pattern: the children of Zurara are usually there when [Zurara] is being praised. So it seems like they're attributing to the Imam these narrations.", "That's the first of the four or five books of the useful [Shia] books when it comes to [rijal]. The next one, in my opinion, perhaps the most useful, because it's clear-cut, direct. [Kashshi], the previous book, passes away around 300 something; he's a contemporary for [Kulaini]. And Najashi passes away in the year 450; his book is direct: he simply says this person is weak, this person is reliable. He presents his [isnad] to the books of that specific narrator, so this specific book is filled with useful information, at least compared to the other [rijal] works I'm going to be referring to soon.", "The next one is very similar in its order and organization; however, there aren't that many gradings of narrators, so he doesn't speak about their reliability or weakness as much. The work after that is a very interesting work, because it's somewhat larger than the rest in terms of narrators: it includes something like 6500 narrators. However, most of the narrators in the book don't have any sort of description. It's just name after name after name. Once in a while he will say this person is reliable, this person is weak; the vast majority of the time he doesn't mention any of that. Sometimes he includes details about their life, once in a while he'll include information about when they died or something, and it seems like he's getting that information from earlier Sunni sources, because there's some biographies that are pretty much identical with information that you find in Sunni works.", "Finally you have [al-Ghada'iri], which I have right here. It's quite a thin book and simply lists out the weak narrators, and seems to be more of a [muhaddith], more of a critic, a better judge, very critical and very specific in his criticisms, and in my opinion he surpasses the other [rijal] scholars, better than [Najashi].", "Now to give you an idea of the difference when it comes to the wealth of information: remember, this book is early 400s, and this is what we have from their works on weak narrators. Compare this to what [Sunnis] had at the time. What we have right here is nine volumes. So that's a lot more information when studying weak narrators; they put in a lot more time, a lot more effort, and the biographical information is a lot richer. And this is one of the reasons why I strongly believe that [Shias] did not do enough when it comes to pointing out the weakness of narrators, and it's due to this that [Shias] have too many authentic reports, a lot more authentic reports than they're supposed to have.", "I just want to give you a couple more examples of the lack of information when it comes to the Shia system. The concept of [marasil], the concept of [disconnection] in reports: as we all know, the main core things that we look at when we're studying a Hadith is one, the reliability of narrator, trustworthiness, and two, the connection: did each [narrator] hear from his teacher? [Sunnis] have works that were specifically focusing on that. They authored works that specifically speak about: this person did not hear from that person. [Shias], they don't have works in this specific field; you barely find them speaking about this person not hearing from that person. So due to this, you automatically assume it's connected, and again, that's another reason why you had more authentication than necessary from a [Shia] perspective.", "Another issue is the issue of [tadlis]. For those that are not familiar with [tadlis], this is basically narrating from your Sheikh that which you did not hear. So for example, if I said that pork is Haram, now notice I didn't say I heard Sheikh Karim say; I'm not specifying that I heard him say this. Now if I'm constantly doing this, even if I'm getting this information from a reliable source, but basically I'm getting this information from a secondhand source, I will be considered in a category referred to as [al-mudallisin]: someone who is making it seem like he's hearing from his Sheikh, but in reality he's not. And [Sunnis] have [lists]; they have works written about them; they've compiled compilations. This doesn't exist in [Shiism]. And again, due to this you will look at the [Shia] chain based on [Shia] standards, assume it's authentic. So that's another way that the system itself has failed.", "One final example in regards to this is the topic of [ilal al-hadith], perhaps the hardest form of Hadith criticism. Catching a liar is easy; catching the mistake of someone that's reliable is a lot harder. And alhamdulillah you have classical works by Sunni scholars that focused on simply doing that. [Al-Daraqutni] got like 14 volumes, pointing out where the narrators went wrong, what's the correct way this [isnad] is supposed to be mentioned: very detailed stuff. So catching the mistakes of the reliable narrators is a field that is held exclusively, and [in Shiism] you don't have that. And that's why in [Shiism] you don't take that into account, and again, due to this [Shias] authenticated a lot more narrations than they're supposed to.",), "Farid Responds, “Why are Sunnis and Shia Different? Full Breakdown (AIM Conference)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Un_bX5Zm3o0", ((595, "9:55–11:28"), (700, "11:40–12:00"), (726, "12:06–13:51"), (842, "14:02–15:26"), (928, "15:28–16:16"), (986, "16:26–17:58"), (1080, "18:00–19:30"), (1176, "19:36–20:06"), (1209, "20:09–21:42"), (1304, "21:44–23:18"), (1398, "23:18–24:15"), (1457, "24:17–25:36"), (1540, "25:40–26:48"), (1613, "26:53–27:46"), (1669, "27:49–29:09"), (1755, "29:15–29:53"), (1802, "30:02–31:15"), (1877, "31:17–32:37"), (1960, "32:40–34:08"), (2054, "34:14–35:29"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML108:504-562 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("alKafi (The sufficient book) did come 300 years after the Prophet, and it's full of lies and manipulations by liar narrators like Zurara and others. And there's no authentic chain that goes back for alKafi, like we do have for Bukhari and Muslim. So which chain do we take? We take the chain that takes us back to the Prophet. We don't take alKafi's chain, which only takes us to Ja'far asSadiq, who is 70 years later. So if you're 70 years later, how did he know what the Prophet did and didn't do? Are we taking the religion from a man, or are we taking the religion from the Prophet?", "\"Sunni came from thousands of people.\" Then he adds some nonsense which is not true: \"political ties,\" \"memory issues.\" Every authentic narration we have has conditions. One of the conditions that the person has to be a well memoriser. If he had \"memory issues\", then the Hadith is not authentic.", "Go to the books of alKafi and مَن لا يَحضُرُهُ الفَقيه (For one who has no access to a jurist), or books of Shi'a generally, go to the 5 Books, or go to their books of Hadith: you're not going to find this nonsense! It's not father from son; it's narrators, general narrators, with political issues and memory issues. All of the lies that he was saying are actually present about the narrators in the Shi'a books, but he tries to put it about the Sunni narrations, and he says, \"Oh, this came from a family!\"", "Which one is more likely to stay accurate? \"Sunnis have tens of thousands of people narrating, and one family's private household narrates something\": which one is more likely to be preserved? And he thinks the one family, which anyone in the chain, in the members of the family, can change and tell his son a lie, and therefore the whole thing is gone, is more authentic somehow than tens of thousands of people spread across the world, that if one lies, the other person is going to expose his lie! The other 10 people who are narrating the same thing, we're going to learn from them that this person is a liar! So somehow he's trying to convince you that the many numbers of people is weaker than one family, private family household narrating something.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| The Quran Proves Jesus Is God?! Sunnis Don't Pray Like The Prophet?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qbnzQHBdTJo", ((1472, "24:32–25:00"), (1518, "25:18–25:31"), (1551, "25:51–26:15"), (1577, "26:17–27:04"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR130:794-835 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("In conclusion, Sunnis and [Shias] take their beliefs from their own respective sources, and it's impossible to be a Sunni if you're following the [Shiite] corpus; it's impossible to be a Shia if you're following the Sunni corpus. The Sunni authentication system is more robust: you can write books about the narrators in their lives, of course not all of them, but the major [narrators]: the more important the narrator is, the more information that we have about their lives. So therefore, if one had to make a choice between the Sunni corpus and the Sunni system versus the Shiite corpus and the Shiite system, in order to determine where you want to take your beliefs from and which of these two reflects Islam more authentically, then it's a no-brainer: the Sunni system and corpus outshines the Shia system and corpus [in] pretty much every angle.",), "Farid Responds, “Why are Sunnis and Shia Different? Full Breakdown (AIM Conference)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Un_bX5Zm3o0", ((2134, "35:34–37:01"),))

=== 44.4 Mass-transmitted reports in Shia hadith
