#passage(("You're saying a specific report is [mutawatir]. Are you referring to the report in which you have Jawad narrating from his father, from his father, all the way to Ali, in which Hassan mentions the name of the 12th imams? Well, I don't know if it's according to your books, but at least it's one narrator, it's one narrator.",), "Farid Responds, “I Debated the Top Shia Clubhouse Debater! [Highlights]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JKT_QIphTLI", ((626, "10:26–12:32"),))
// U FR174:1078-1255 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("This is not really a weird controversial thing; it's not something that's going to shake the core of our understanding of Christianity. We do believe that Christians saw Jesus die on the cross, we do believe they saw that, and it's transmitted, at least. No, it's not mass transmitted; it's transmitted through four anonymous sources. If it's one person saying the whole town saw something, if I said I saw a zombie apocalypse right outside my house and my whole town saw it, I'm the only person reporting it.",), "Farid Responds, “Full Debate: Farid (Sunni) vs Bayat (Shia)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iwc31dHLfco", ((2737, "45:37–46:43"),))
// U FR174:1351-1638 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("My response is yes, it's not [mutawatir] according to my conditions. My condition is that the number of narrators is high enough that it's impossible for the narrators to have [colluded on] a lie, they've come together to create a lie. So show me a Shia [mass] transmitted report that says opposite to what you've just suggested.", "Every time I quote these reports for you, I say this is according to your standards, because you said two narrators, that's sufficient. Let me explain why it's not mass transmitted according to me, those specific reports that I've quoted: it's because you do not have enough primary sources for there to ever be a [mutawatir]. If you say, can you provide me the hadith, which Sunni sources have it, I would name like 50. When I ask you for the source of a report, the sources are like, they all go back to three or four authors. You lack the variety of primary sources. Even one of your main primary sources, [al-Tusi], is quoting [al-Kulayni] half the time, so that's not even considered to be independent. Name five scholars from the [tabaqa] that have books.",), "Farid Responds, “Full Debate: Farid (Sunni) vs Bayat (Shia)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iwc31dHLfco", ((3544, "59:04–59:45"), (3753, "1:02:33–1:05:55"),))
// U FR174:1910-1969 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("These reports are authentic, so anything a scholar says doesn't mean anything; doesn't matter what any scholar says. You cannot say a scholar says this and this has more weight than the opinion of imam.", "This is about [kufr], and this is about [mustad'af], and he says yes, there are people, not everyone is going to go through, because there are [mustad'af]. And [mustad'af], according to the imam, it is like someone with a mental defect; it's not the average person. I'm not a [mustad'af]; I'm going to go to hell because I did not accept.",), "Farid Responds, “Full Debate: Farid (Sunni) vs Bayat (Shia)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Iwc31dHLfco", ((4807, "1:20:07–1:20:28"), (4883, "1:21:23–1:21:54"),))

=== 44.5 Early Shia sources on the twelve Imams
// NODE P11.C03.S05
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR124:1-148 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("I wanted to get really deep into [Kitab Sulaym] initially, but there was this one issue. I started going through the manuscripts, and the manuscripts that are [attributed] to Abdullah don't seem to fit that well with what you find in classical works, and the manuscripts attributed to Hamad [bin Isa] don't seem to fit too well with the classical reports that you find from Hamad bin [Isa]. That was my initial stumbling block, and it really put me off looking into [Kitab Sulaym] further. I was like: this is just way too complicated, this does not make any sense, it doesn't fit in with what we have from the historical data. That's the first thing.", "But my main issue, or simpler issue, is this: I actually have it written down somewhere; on every single page I came across the number 12 imams. It happened again and again; it's like 20 occurrences, it's all over the book. It's impossible to assume that [Nu'mani] overlooked all of that and found one example that can appear to be 13 due to some sort of [ta'wil]. Clearly what must have happened was: that explicitly said 13, and didn't have like 20 examples that said 12.",), "Farid Responds, “Discussion with a Shi'i”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gAEgq0sDjSA", ((257, "4:17–5:16"), (317, "5:17–6:08"),))
// U FR124:267-768 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("I have a reason to be suspicious of some of these attributions. You do regard [Kitab Sulaym] to be the first [asl], a very important work, explicit about 12 imams. That being the case, why don't you have reports quoting [Sulaym] before [Kulaini] affirming 12 imams? You have a book that's completely focused on imama; it's got 700 reports. If you were there at the time, you'd be quoting narrations that include the number of [imams].", "Before we even get into [Basa'ir], because the [Basa'ir] discussion is just going to be a bit longer: [Barqi's] book mentioned nothing about 12 imams. That's the problem here. [Mahasin] does include the report that's supposed to have 12 imams, in which he asked him and he mentioned the names of the imams, and it wasn't in the manuscript. [Barqi] has no reason to exclude that report from his book; what is his motivation for not including it in his book? Muhammad bin Hassan also doesn't include this report in his book. I believe that this report is either fabricated by [Kulaini] or fabricated by Muhammad; that's just my conclusion, 50 50. It's either [Kulaini], because that's where the information is coming from.", "It's a very important report: it includes the names of all 12 imams, it's very explicit, seems clean as well, and many times we have discussions, this is the first report they quote, because it's in this very important book, it seems clean, it lists all the imams. This shows how important this report is, and yet the most important part, in which all 12 imams are supposed to be named, are not named. Now, you mentioned that there may have occurred some sort of issue in the manuscript itself, but this is not a normal [sahw]. I'd understand [tahrif].", "I regard [Mahasin] to be legit because I can't see how a mistake like that would occur. From an outsider's perspective, and by the way this is not binding upon me, I'm trying to be as objective as possible, it seems to me that the [Barqi] chain was fixed, because it's clearly disconnected. So in [Kafi] the report is fixed and the Imam is changed.", "[Saffar] never includes the Hadith. Imagine you're [Saffar], and you were there at the time of Hassan [Askari], you were there in the year 250, you had 40 years to include this report and you never did. Why wouldn't you include this extremely important report in your book? And by the way, this fits in with historical Shiism: it actually makes sense that we don't have a list of all 12 imams since the time of an early Imam.",), "Farid Responds, “Discussion with a Shi'i”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gAEgq0sDjSA", ((671, "11:11–12:20"), (1010, "16:50–21:28"), (1304, "21:44–22:56"), (1501, "25:01–25:41"), (1596, "26:36–27:13"),))
// U FR124:851-1291 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("I just looked into it right now: the Hadith you're referring to doesn't list the names of the [imams]; it's the same thing. [Ibrahim] [quoting] his father, he's quoting Abu Hashem Jafari, and then it provides the report, and there's nothing there about 12 imams. So these are two sources in which the same report exists, and it would predate the [Kafi], and yet there's no mentioning.", "I am saying that one of the narrators added something or removed something. I do agree with you completely: Kafi is the strongest in terms of manuscript; however, it's too many coincidences. You can't say that this was a mistake from [Barqi] and the mistake from [Ibrahim]. It's clearly the same Hadith; the proof of this is that when [your ulama] quote this Hadith, they'll mention these other sources; they see it as the same Hadith.", "In regards to the argument that there's a mistake in the manuscript, the reason why it doesn't make sense is that the difference is very natural. This is not a mistake; a mistake would be the chain cut off. If El [Barqi] had this report, you're saying that this is a different Hadith, which means two different reports, why wouldn't [Barqi] quote the [Kafi] report, the report that mentions all 12 imams? And the reason is because [Barqi] never had that report. In regards to [Basa'ir], I do have to reiterate there's nothing explicit about 12 imams.",), "Farid Responds, “Discussion with a Shi'i”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gAEgq0sDjSA", ((2205, "36:45–37:54"), (2535, "42:15–45:02"), (2847, "47:27–48:37"),))
// U FR127:133-698 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("Basically, it's a narration in the [Kafi] by [al-Kulaini], in which multiple people are narrating that a man came to Ali, and he was [with] Hassan, and that man said: I have three questions, and if you answer the three questions that I have, then I will accept that you are [the appointed Imam]. Instead of Ali answering, what he does is he points to Hassan, and [Hassan] answers. I can't remember the answer, I can't even remember the questions, but he provides an answer, and Hassan, I'm assuming, is quite young at the time, so it's like, wow, Hassan's quite young, he's answering this. So this man then says: I bear witness that you are the Imam. And then the man leaves, and then at the end of the story turns out to be [al-Khidr].", "I have multiple issues with this specific report. I do accept this: according to [Shia] standards it's an authentic Hadith. But this is a major author who quotes thousands of hadiths and doesn't mention a single Hadith with 12 imams. It's very strange for [al-Kulaini] to attribute a Hadith to him that mentions all 12 imams, but when you go to his book, the Hadith is there, but it doesn't have the 12 imams. Why is he not mentioning the 12, and then all of a sudden you have him [narrating] to a group of people in sources that come after him? What's going on?", "One issue with this specific [Hadith] is it's narrated by [Jawad]; he passes away in the year 220. He's quoting something that happened around 100 and 80 years earlier. Why are we accepting this report? Your response to me is: the imams always narrate from their fathers, from their fathers, and therefore when [Abu Ja'far] quotes, we have to accept it as a complete [chain]. We don't accept any disconnected reports.", "You're saying that any established Imam, if he [narrates from] the prophet, peace be upon him, or if he narrates from his [forefathers], then we have to assume that it's an accepted report, it's connected. My issue with this is that in this specific case his [imamah] is not established. You have to establish his imama first before you can claim something like: oh, he's [narrating] from his father, from his father and his father.", "Imagine I'm reporting a narration in which I am basically being appointed; I'm benefiting from this. So in this report, the Jawad is narrating a narration where he himself is the Imam. Let me add a bit more to that: if Jawad said, let's assume, and I don't believe this, but let's assume that Jawad said: I am the Imam, and the proof that I am the Imam is my great great grandfather; [Ali is] the Imam, and he appointed to his son, he appointed to his son, all the way up to me, I'd call him a liar. You have to explain the connection. What you're doing here is you're saying, because Jawad is reliable, we have to accept his claim that he is the Imam, because he appointed himself as the Imam through his forefathers.",), "Farid Responds, “Shia Debater attempts to prove 12 Imams”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oMXb6e4GNl4", ((560, "9:20–11:15"), (691, "11:31–22:29"), (1419, "23:39–26:38"), (1706, "28:26–29:16"), (1814, "30:14–36:06"),))
// U FR127:822-1378 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("The main issue is that this is a report that comes to existence like a thousand years later and was not quoted by any early scholar; it just shows out of nowhere. So I feel that if a report is not quoted for over a thousand years, then I get to play the card: why didn't [he] quote it, especially since it's a very important report that mentions the names of the [twelve]?", "The fact that [al-Tusi] mentions nothing, the fact that [Najashi mentions] nothing: you can't claim there's [ijma'] by all scholars that [Sa'id bin Jubayr] is an acceptable reliable narrator.", "Let me just share some thoughts in regards to this specific book. One of the classical works of heritage that was of course held by one of the major scholars of the [madhhab], and like most of the books, it is seen as a lost book. However, in the 11th century there are some quotes that all of a sudden appeared, even though they didn't exist before that. And what's really interesting about this book is that the book is clean. In earlier books like [al-Kafi], the works of [Saduq] and whatnot, you find a lot of weak reports, a lot of liars narrating these reports, but in the book of [al-Fadl], this specific book, all of a sudden all the [asanid] are authentic; they're very clean. And by the way, what's really interesting about this book, this is the main point: it's short; however, the book revolves around narrations that have to do with the imama, the names of imams and the [Mahdi], and the reports that can be found in this book cannot be found anywhere; it just appeared out of nowhere; it can only be found in this one specific book.", "The published copy today was first published as an actual book in the year 1350, so 90 years ago, but it's based on the copy that he found, and he says: this is what we found. So it's not something that was [handed down] to his student, to his student; no, it's something that [he] came across. So there is a reason for you to doubt the attribution of this book. And again, the book is very short, and it only contains 21 hadiths, and again it's a very clean book in terms of [isnad].", "So the question is: how did they not quote this book? [Al-Fadl] will write it, and it was lost immediately, and his students forgot to narrate his Hadith, and they disappeared for a thousand years, and now they exist a thousand years later? And especially, how can they lose the works of [al-Fadl], a major scholar from the third century? These scholars that I'm mentioning, they wrote extensively about imama, specifically about imama, but they would not quote these specific hadiths. So these are all signs that shows that the work is a fabrication; the book in itself is a fabrication.", "I mentioned in the article some examples of the changes that you find in this book that cannot be found elsewhere. One example is this: the fourth Hadith [lists] the names of all the imams, and this exact same Hadith can be found, but in the other book it doesn't list all the imams; it only lists six of them. So what happened was the person who wrote this book just added some extra names, the names of the rest of the [imams]. So that's a type of fabrication that you find in the book. In the fifth Hadith you have the naming of all the imams from the narration from Muslim, and this actually conflicts: he asks him, who is the Imam after you? And he did not want to mention the name. He said that you will find the next Imam: humility and serenity, and of course [haybah], this weight within him. So the question is: why would Muhammad [bin] Muslim ask [al-Sadiq] such a thing, when he already is supposed to know the names of the [twelve imams]? And why would he continuously ask [al-Baqir] for this information? And it has a report by [Aban bin] Uthman, and [Aban bin] Uthman is [narrating] Hadith which includes more imams, when in reality not someone that believes 12 imams. It includes some funky reports that did not exist in the past, and suddenly started to exist a thousand years later.",), "Farid Responds, “Shia Debater attempts to prove 12 Imams”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oMXb6e4GNl4", ((2703, "45:03–48:41"), (3720, "1:02:00–1:02:10"), (4204, "1:10:04–1:11:54"), (4335, "1:12:15–1:13:22"), (4404, "1:13:24–1:14:26"), (4482, "1:14:42–1:17:09"),))

=== 44.6 Zurarah and narrators cursed by the Imams
// NODE P11.C03.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR053:1-122 | Farid Responds | 9
#passage(("One of the main narrators in [Shiism], perhaps the main narrator: his name is [Zurarah], and he was cursed by [Ja'far] himself, but Shia still take narrations from him. [Nakshawani] admits that this major Shia narrator was indeed cursed by the imams. So why would someone take narrations from a cursed individual? It's not just a Sunni claim; it's a [Shia] claim, and [Ammar] himself, the most popular English Shia speaker, is affirming this; he's not disputing it. But now he's going to give you a reason as to why that doesn't matter: [Zurarah] is still trustworthy according to Amar.", "[Ja'far], who is the most important Imam, the most quoted [Shia Imam], is now cursing his student, which makes him naturally unreliable. But Amar uses this report, in which Jafar says to the son of [Zurarah]: \"I only cursed your father to save his life, so that they wouldn't kill him,\" referring to the government.", "Now, there are multiple issues with this theory. The first is that [Zurarah] is having his reputation destroyed by [Ja'far]. And it's not like Jaffer is saying this to one or two people. Jaffar wants this to be something that's [well known], that's established, so that the [caliph] at the time wouldn't assume that [Zurarah] is someone that's close to him. So this sort of cursing became common. [Zurarah's] son is now curious: what the hell is going on? Why is my father being cursed? This thing even spread to Sunni circles: Sunnis in their books have a narration that says that [Zurarah] is from the hellfire. So it's not like this limited statement that only a couple people heard of. No, it's something that's become widespread, and it has to become widespread, because this is the imam's attempt to save [Zurarah's] life and to protect him, allegedly, based on this really strange theory.", "So the problem with this theory is that when the Imam curses [Zurarah], he's disparaging his main source of information, that people would naturally go to in Iraq. [Zurarah] is in Iraq; people in Iraq, they're not going all the way down to Medina in order to seek knowledge. They're going to the local scholar there; they're going to [Zurarah]. However, when they become aware that the imam curses, there goes the thousands of narrations that they believed in, or that they are planning to apply; they're now in doubt of the information that they hear from [Zurarah].", "And there's another issue with this excuse, which is that if this was true, if Jaffar was protecting his student so he started to curse him, it has greater implications on [Shia] hadiths, because you have other students that were also cursed by the imams, and now we have to reject the clear obvious critiques by the imams and assume that the imam was probably just protecting them. And I'm not just speaking theoretically. We find practical examples of late [Shia] scholars rejecting the direct criticisms of the imams against these major Shia narrators: oh no, the imam just said it to protect him, in the same way he did to [Zurarah]. You see how dangerous this excuse and this point of view and this theory is.", "And by the way, let's ignore the fact that the ship wasn't for orphans, and that Amar is confusing this with another story. [Khidr] ruined the boat, but for the king and not for the poor people. What Jaffer did is he didn't ruin [Zurarah] for the king or for the [caliph] or for [Banu] Abbas alone. What Jaffer did is he ruined [Zurarah] for everyone. All the Sunnis around the world, the 90% of the ummah, are now not just in doubt of [Zurarah], but they're using these quotes about [Zurarah] to say: look, we can't accept this guy, he's cursed. See how dangerous this is. So the analogy is a terrible one.", "But let's end with the outcome of this cursing. What did [Zurarah] benefit from the cursing that he received? How did it save his life? By the way, that's if we're even assuming that his life was under threat, which Amar hasn't established, and no scholar that I'm aware of has ever established this. [Zurarah] lived until after [the Imam], died 2 months after. Let the man die a shahid with his reputation intact, instead of ruining his reputation by cursing him.", "Now I'd like to end with a final thought, which is that this narrator, [Zurarah], who's one of the top Shia narrators, perhaps the main Shia narrator, who is cursed by the imams: who is he being exonerated by? The person that's exonerating him by narrating this report is his son. So the question is, what are you going to believe? Are you going to believe Jaffar, the [sixth] [Shia] Imam, who's infallible, who cursed [Zurarah], or are you going to believe the son of [Zurarah], who's narrating this thing in order to exonerate his father? And remember the wider implications. Now you can't trust anything that the imam says, because whatever the imam says about a narrator, you get to shrug it off as: maybe the imam meant something else, maybe he was protecting him, maybe there's some sort of a hidden meaning in his clear-cut statements. That's the danger of believing in this one specific statement by the son of [Zurarah], which has had this massive effect on [Shia] hadith.", "You could have had a proper system, because you had 250 years of infallible imams, and you have a lot of quotes in which they praise narrators and reject narrators and disparage them and curse them and promise them heaven. But now you're discarding this stuff in favor of positions [by Tusi] and [Najashi], that never met Jafar or [Zurarah] or anyone like that. They came 300 years later. You're going to take their clear-cut statements, and you're going to reject the clear-cut statements of the imams themselves. Why are you doing this? It's because you are following and accepting the [story] that was concocted by the son of [Zurarah], who's trying to exonerate his father.",), "Farid Responds, “Nakshawani: Top Shia Narrator was CURSED!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c1NNHrhN55g", ((6, "0:06–2:57"), (222, "3:42–4:08"), (248, "4:08–5:08"), (308, "5:08–5:46"), (346, "5:46–6:39"), (442, "7:22–8:19"), (499, "8:19–9:15"), (558, "9:18–10:33"), (639, "10:39–11:27"),))
