=== 44.7 Altered narrations and the evolution of Shia theology
// NODE P11.C03.S07
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR119:1-267 | Farid Responds | 8
#passage(("There's no intention of creating hatred or anything like that. Some Shias unfortunately see any criticism, and not just [Shias], people of all [sects], religions, often feel that any criticism comes from a position of malice and hatred, but that's not the case. I personally have a belief, a specific truth, it's an objective truth, and I'm sharing my findings, and I'm doing this because I want guidance for [the Shias].", "The evolution of Shia theology: there's so much to say about the subject, because when we think about Shiite criticisms against Sunnis today, a chunk of it has to do with Sunni beliefs in regards to the attributes of Allah. You will find [Shias] making the claim: human-like qualities. And we find similar arguments being brought forward by some other [sects] as well. What's really interesting about this accusation is that once upon a time this was a common [Shia] belief, or, not specifically comparing Allah to humans, but the idea of affirming the attributes of Allah. We do find this in classical [Shia] sources, and the proof of this [is] this interesting statement by [al-Murtada], who says: just until yesterday all the people of [Qom]. [Qom] is the main [Shia] stronghold; there were two main [Shia] cities in the third century, Kufa and [Qom]. And he says: just until yesterday all the people of [Qom], except [Abu Ja'far], may Allah have mercy upon him, were anthropomorphists and fatalists; their books and works are an explicit testimony.", "This is very accurate. We do have some works that have survived from early scholars that happen to be [Qummis], and they do have Hadith in which they affirm attributes to Allah. Now here's the thing: those that came after him, they were very influenced by [Mu'tazili] thought. This is a very old accusation and claim and description, and as we all know, the term anthropomorphists is used against anyone that affirms the [sifat of] Allah. So it's very possible that the people of [Qom], that third century Shias, were a lot closer to [Ahl al-Sunnah] than we actually think.", "I'm going to start off with the first example. Of course, everything I will be saying today will be with evidence, with the [dalil]. Perhaps the most important Shia compiler of Hadith, of course he didn't really focus on providing only authentic reports, but he provided all sorts of reports; his book has around 16 to 17 000 narrations. In any case, he quotes a very interesting narration: [al-Baqir] said: the 12th Imam, the current Imam, the hidden [Mahdi], when he rises, Allah... (By the way, this is the current [Shia] belief; at the time in which this report was first propagated, did they necessarily believe it's referring to the 12th Imam?) But he says: when our [Qa'im], the 12 Imam, rises, Allah shall place his hand, [I mean] the hand of Allah, on the heads of the servants, so as to unite their intellect and perfect their understanding.", "The second report, 381, so there's not much of a time gap, says: 12 Imam rises, he shall place his hand on the heads of the servants, as to unite their intellect and perfect their understanding. So the glaring difference here is that the first narration affirms hands for Allah; in the second narration it's attributing the hand to the [Mahdi]. And the only reason that this is done is because once upon a time they used to be [anthropomorphists]. And again, I'm not necessarily accepting this term and attributing this term to the people of [Qom]; however, it simply means that they affirmed attributes. And here we have [Kulaini] in a narration affirming an attribute; however, in the narration of [Ibn Babawayh], also known as [al-Saduq], we find that this attribute, or this hand, is belonging to the [Mahdi]. Again, notice it's a very short time gap, not a major time gap, and yet we have this major change in the Hadith.", "This is a [isnad] tree, quite a simple one, and you have all the narrators being identical, with the exception of the narrators in the end, because Hussein bin Mohammed is having this narration [attributed] to him through two paths. Who should we accuse of distortion out of this list of suspects? If we were to look at all the possibilities, there are four ultimately. One is Hussein Mohammed [narrated] both narrations; I can't really see why he would do that. The other possibility is that kulaini distorted the narration; I'm not sure why he would do that either. And then of course there's the possibility for distortion [by Babawayh]. If someone didn't even come across this quote by [Sharif], one would say 50 50. I think that would be a fair assessment.", "Here's another example: out of Adam's loins all his progeny until Judgment Day, and they emerged like scattered particles, so he taught them and showed them himself; if it were not for this, none would recognize his Lord. [Al-Saduq] said: he, Allah, brought out of Adam's loins all his progeny until Judgment Day, and they emerged like scattered particles, so he taught them and showed them his creations; if it were not for this, none would recognize their lord. So the first narration affirms an image for Allah, and the second narration, that idea of an image for Allah has gone. And again, this happened in a very short time period between [Kulaini] and [Ibn Babawayh], 50 years. There are some dramatic shifts happening in the 4th century.", "Here's another one: Allah revealed to Musa: say to those around you, do not dare to kill the forbidden soul unjustly; whoever among you kills a soul in this world, Allah shall kill him in Hellfire a hundred thousand times, the same way he killed his companion. [Ibn Babawayh] narrates similarly. It says a hundred thousand times he will be killed and revived and killed and revived. My theory as to what's happening is that, let's say someone killed someone by kicking him. If you're going to accept the first narration, if you're going to take it literally, it means that Allah will kick someone a hundred thousand times and kill that person through kicking him. It seems like that's the apparent meaning. So in the second narration, all of a sudden it's gone, and it's \"I shall kill him\", meaning Musa will kill [him] instead of Allah being the person that kills.",), "Farid Responds, “Evolution of Shia Theology”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E14dS0zF-UA", ((66, "1:06–1:39"), (106, "1:46–3:27"), (207, "3:27–4:22"), (270, "4:30–5:48"), (350, "5:50–7:17"), (439, "7:19–9:08"), (552, "9:12–10:44"), (647, "10:47–12:38"),))
// U FR119:410-521 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Here's another example; this is very explicit: tell me who will be the Imam after you. He said: my son so and so. [Ibn Babawayh] has the same narration, but it says: tell me who will be the Imam after you. He said: my son Ali. Fairly explicit; this is a clear distortion. Now here's the irony in this report: the first one, you cannot use this to affirm the [imamah] of any Imam. You cannot say that by looking at the first report; you can by looking at the second report. Now, the major problem with [al-Kulaini's] narration is this: these are all his sons. When you look at the first report, my son so-and-so is the next Imam, that doesn't really mean too much when you know that all these men were his sons. And by the way, for those of you that are curious, the eldest is Ibrahim, and I do believe that you had early [Shias] claiming that Ahmed was also an Imam. So you simply can't use this report. But now again, later on, the fourth Century, [Ibn Babawayh], or maybe one of his teachers, switches it up and turns it into: my son Ali.", "Every single one of these examples that I've mentioned so far, all these changes occur during the time [of Ibn Babawayh], and he is the common suspect in all of these, and there are like over 40 examples of him doing this. And by the way, this is actually a [Shia] position by some late scholars, referring to a specific report: this report is taken from [al-Kafi], and it has suffered from weird alterations, which makes us mistrust [al-Saduq]; it seems he only distorted it in accordance with the school of the people of justice.", "So once again, there once was a time in which it seemed like early Shias would affirm attributes for Allah. Sure, perhaps in some cases they went overboard, they went a bit on the extreme side; however, at one point, at least in some aspects, they seem to be closer to [Ahl al-Sunnah].",), "Farid Responds, “Evolution of Shia Theology”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E14dS0zF-UA", ((1126, "18:46–20:34"), (1318, "21:58–22:49"), (1393, "23:13–23:38"),))
// ARTICLE FA05 pages:16 sha256:520e49c21501c8c434711622c0d58d5e831ea4c17f69446e724e32112a98b5ce
