// U ML337:704-819 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("Division can happen for many reasons. It is only a problem if the religion is the cause of the division. It is not a problem if the religion is not the cause of the division and people choose to divide anyway.", "But the interesting thing is this, about prophecies: Prophet Muhammedﷺ, he said that there will be division. Just like the Christians and the Jews got divided, he said, the Muslim Nation will be [divided] as well. Prophet Muhammedﷺ asked Allah for 3 things. He said: I asked Allah that my Nation is not destroyed with a famine, or a disease, all of them die. And then Allah granted him that, that not all the Muslim Nation will die from one famine or a disease. So that's the first thing he asked, and Allah gave him that. Second thing he asked: that my Nation, not all of it is destroyed by enemies gathering on them, or an enemy comes and completely annihilate them, like many civilizations were annihilated. And Allah granted him that. Then he asked Allah: Oh Allah, I want my Nation not to be divided, and Allah refused him. He said no, your Nation will be divided.", "So division is a part of this, what we call سنّة الحياة, it is the sunnah of life, it is the way how life happens. So sometimes Allah sends division as a punishment for the people, because the people are disobeying God.", "But the most important thing is this: does the religion give you a solution or not? This is why I would say it's not present in Christianity but it's present in Islam: the Quran explicitly says what to do when you disagree. So the criteria is, if me and your husband disagree on anything -we're both Muslims- the criteria of how we know the solution is we go back to the Quran. If what I said is in the Quran, then I'm right, then he should listen. If what he said is in the Quran or in the Hadith, then I should listen to him. So he gives us the way of how to reconcile between any disagreements, gives us the solution, and that's the most important thing.", "But the interesting thing to keep in mind is this: all the sects that you mentioned, they believe in the same Quran. But in Christianity it's not the same. The difference between the two is the following: the schools of thought in Islam are all valid. Meaning: Muslims can follow any opinion of the four schools of thought, and the other person is not going to say you're wrong, because they are valid interpretations and understandings of the Quran. While the differences between the Protestants and Catholics etc., they don't agree that all of these are valid. They believe some are wrong and some are right. That's the first thing.", "The second thing I would say: the problem is in the Scripture, the number of writings in the scripture, chapters etc. So when you have a book that has 66, the Protestant one, and you have 82 or 83 are Orthodox, or 77 as the Catholic, you have different numbers of books that you personally will consider to be Canon, and they will consider Apocrypha. So some of the books that you believe in, the other Christians will say this is not from God, this is Apocrypha writing! So that's the difference. All the Muslims, they will never believe in any other Quran other than the one Quran. They all agree it's 114 chapters, is the Quran that we have today; none of them disagree on the number of chapters, all of them agree. It's one Book, it's one Scripture, and as I said, the schools of thought, they agree that all of them are valid! Our issue is with anyone who brings teachings that are opposite to the Quran or not found in the Quran and Hadith, and that's why we use the criteria that Allah has given us.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Skeptical About The Prophet of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QIxWrKc_J_Y", ((1884, "31:24–31:31"), (1934, "32:14–32:57"), (1984, "33:04–33:26"), (2029, "33:49–34:21"), (2067, "34:27–35:42"), (2144, "35:44–36:36"),))
