#passage(("It's not like Christianity in that way. If you're referring to Sunni and Shia, for example, the disagreement happened later for political reason: who should have been a successor after the Prophet. They don't disagree on the Book or the identity of God or the Prophet, like the Christian sects disagree on who Jesus was, disagree on what God is. We don't have that in Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Lady Questions Muslim On Evidence Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nk4EM0u0VNs", ((1749, "29:09–29:33"),))
// U ML364:161-182 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("So now, if we all come back to the Quran and the Prophet, can we ever have sects? Those people, they misinterpret the Quran or they mix the words of the Quran, change the words of the Quran. That's why I'm saying, if you actually are a sincere person, you come back to what it says, like when the Quran says خاتَم and خاتِم in two different readings. خاتِم means the last, the final, that's what the Arabic literally means. If this is what the Quran is saying, how can I ever come and claim there's another prophet? But what happens is that those people, they reject what the Quran is saying. This is how we start having new sects.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Questioned On Sectarianism In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MnwLenfsZWA", ((471, "7:51–8:28"),))
// U ML284:352-396 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("We have division because of people who have misguidance, people who are ignorant. We got people coming on social media -guy's barely was wearing diapers yesterday- and he goes on YouTube and he starts telling you: actually you don't need Hadith anymore. But for 1,400 years we've been following Hadith, what's going on here? \"How did you find this?\" \"I found this marvellous discovery when I was reading the Quran.\" \"What happened when you were reading the Quran?\" \"I came to this Enlightenment that the Quran is the only thing we need.\" \"MashaAllah! What?\" \"This verse here and there means that, and this Hadith is just fabrication.\" But what about 1,400 years of scholarship? Those people did not have your Enlightenment? They couldn't read the Quran? Or they did not know the Arabic language? What was going on since all of this 1,400 years until you were born yesterday? The Ummah was misguided, and the 'Ulama (scholars) are all evil, and the scholars were all sell-out. No one was guided except me! And my way of how to pray -which every one who rejects has his own way of how to pray, how to perform Islam- \"my way is the right way! So you have to follow me, and no one else.\" And he starts causing division!", "You get another person who starts calling Muslims: \"this guy is a sell-out, this guy is an agent, this guy is a Wahhabi وهابي, this guy's a Madkhali مدخلي, this guy's an Ikhwani إخواني.\" And they start labelling people one by one, and causing more division within the Ummah. It's true there are people of misguidance, no one is denying this reality. But when, day and night, their job is to just cause division by labelling and naming people, of course you're just causing division for the sake of division; you're not trying to actually find the Truth, you're not trying to unite the people upon the Truth. We're saying: you unite the people upon the Truth, you can not unite upon falsehood. Someone who's believing falsehood and [one] believing in Truth, they can not unite. But still there is a way of advising, there is a way of coming closer to Unity, and that is not calling each other names and saying that every person is a sell-out, this person is working for the يهود (Jews), and this person is working for the CIA, and this person has been getting paid for this. Where's your evidence? \"No, I'm just saying it, look at him, he looks like it, I saw it in his face.\" That's your evidence? \"Yeah! 10,000 years ago, he visited the USA. So he works for the Mossad.\" You have these people creating separation and division.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Situation Of The Ummah, The Disease & The Cure | Al Muwahiddeen Mosque - The Netherlands |”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0ycNKKOx2jg", ((1249, "20:49–21:51"), (1318, "21:58–23:12"),))
// U ML422:58-93 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The answer to me is quite simple, and it is that I separate individuals from the religion structure. I don't care what people do. People can do whatever they want to do. But what does the religion say? Is the religion coherent and consistent, and does it have evidence for its Truth and its divine origin? Or is the religion itself telling you to do these things which you are finding problematic? So if the religion is telling me to be good, be kind to other people, don't kill, fight those only who fight you, defend yourself, but I go kill people and persecute them, then to me, I cannot blame Islam for these individual actions. I would look at what Islam says, and I would say: Islam is telling you to do the opposite.", "Someone is driving, and there's a perfect driving law. It's telling you: please, when it's red, stop. Don't be an idiot. And you're just breaking the light, hitting people. I cannot then say \"oh, the driving law is incorrect!\" I would say the person is incorrect. The driving law is fine. So, it's the same in Islam. I don't bother much with what these people are doing, because Islam is giving instructions on what is True. What is the Truth is coherent, and it tells you what you should do and what you shouldn't do. Just because it happens that you're born into a Muslim family or someone says you are a Muslim doesn't mean what you do is a representation of Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslims Question Islam P1! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tjZje2tKQqQ", ((187, "3:07–3:45"), (230, "3:50–4:19"),))

=== 45.2 The hadith of the 73 sects
// NODE P11.C04.S02
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR134:1-467 | Farid Responds | 12
#passage(("The Hadith that I'm going to be sharing my thoughts on is the Hadith of the 73 sects, quite the famous Hadith. Before going deep into that, we need to get through some technical material, because first step is to look into authenticity, and I personally feel that unless something is authentic, then there's no real point of delving too deeply into it.", "Now let's start off with the most important version of the report: he's narrating from Abu Salama, he says that the prophet peace be upon him said: the Jews will split into 71 sects or 72 sects, as did the Christians, and my nation will split into 73 sects. [Tirmidhi] comments on this and says that within this chapter you will find multiple hadiths. There's not too much to say about this specific Hadith in regards to authenticity; Again, this is the most authentic version of the Hadith, this is the strongest chain for the Hadith. Is this the highest level of authenticity? It's not, due to it coming through the path from [Muhammad ibn Amr], who himself is someone who's been somewhat criticized by early Scholars of Hadith, and there is a specific issue: he does make some mistakes when it comes to his narrations from Abu [Salama]. Now that specific criticism, you can go back to classical works of criticisms of narrators, their biographies, and you'll find that sort of stuff there. So I would personally, it's not just me of course, refer to a chain like this as a mid-tier Hadith: not necessarily the strongest and of course not necessarily the weakest, but it would be something that's mid-tier.", "Let's go back to what [Tirmidhi] says. Again, he says this report is narrated by multiple companions, including [Sa'd], Abdullah and [Awf bin Malik]. If there was one who had intercourse with his mother in the open, then there would be someone from my [ummah] that would do the same. Indeed the children of Israel split into 72 [sects], and my ummah will split into 73 [sects], all of them are in Hellfire except for one sect. He was asked: which is it, oh messenger of Allah? He said: [what] I am upon and my companions. In this specific case there is an issue with one of the [narrators], who happens to be Abdullah, and he's been criticized, weakened by multiple Scholars.", "This specific report is not only limited to those narrators or those companions; it's also attributed to [Anas bin] Malik through multiple chains, around maybe eight chains, and there's an issue with every single one of them. He even hints that there being an issue at the first one, and there's like a hidden defect in that. So it's a very strange issue: so many chains for the reports, but the vast majority of them do seem to be quite weak. But let's step back and let's look at what we have so far, and this is where it gets really interesting. It's mainly from [narrators] from Medina, comes from [Basran] narrators, because that's where [Anas] ended up, comes through narrators who were in the Sham, and comes through narrators from Egypt. So you have all these different narrators from different regions, and I'm not even done, there's multiple more narrations, but these are some of them. You already have this report being narrated in multiple regions, through multiple people, through multiple companions, or at least attributed to multiple companions. So this Hadith spread fairly quickly.", "However, due to the weakness of these reports, when I was going through this I was quite hesitant, because I was like: subhanallah, this report doesn't really seem to be as reliable as I thought, despite it being widespread early on. However, I then came across this really interesting [athar] attribution to [Ali] that is actually authentic, and [Ali] is narrating it himself; he's not attributing it to the prophet peace be upon him, but it's his own saying. The exilark, in Arabic, means the head of the community. So the head of the community of the Jews and the head of the community of the Christians, the bishop, were basically summoned, and he says: I ask you about a matter that I know more than both of you, so do not keep it away from me. Oh exilark, I ask you by he who has brought down the Torah upon Musa, made a path through the sea for you, brought from the rocks Springs of water for each tribe of Israel: I ask you, how many [sects] came after Musa? The exilark responded: not a single one. And he responded by repeating three times: you lie, by Allah, The One and Only God, they split into 71 sects, all of them are in Hellfire except for one. He then brought the bishop and said: I ask you by Allah that brought down the injeel upon [Isa], whose mouth was blessed, who cured the blind, told you of what you eat and what you store in your homes. The [bishop] interrupted and said: I will tell you the truth even without all of that. Commander of Believers said: how many [sects] did you split into after [Isa]? The bishop said: by Allah, not a single one. Ali said three times: you lied, by Allah, The One and Only God, you split into 72 [sects], all of which are in Hellfire except for one. And he then said: that is the one that will be saved, and most of them are of evil conduct. As for us, meaning the Muslims, Allah says: and of those we created there is an ummah who guide with the truth and establish justice.", "So, one more time: an actual authentic report [attributed] to Ali, in which he says something extremely similar to what we find being attributed to the prophet peace be upon him, and due to this we should have certainty that the prophet peace be upon him said this. However, keep this in mind: the report by Abu hurairah doesn't mention anything about that other part, the part that says they will all be in Hellfire except for one. So that's an interesting aspect of it, and of course you will find classical Scholars speaking about that portion of the report, criticizing [it as] being an addition. While we can be sure about the splitting of the sects, the ummah being split into 73 [sects], there seems to be some uncertainty in regards to the specific [addition] that says that the 72 will be in Hellfire, only one will be saved, as well as the addition that says they will be from the people that will be upon what my companions are upon. So that also seems to be an addition. I'm not rejecting it completely myself, and again, there are Scholars that did accept it, but I'm personally hesitant in regards to that. But again, who the hell am I?", "Does this really change anything? Do we really need the prophet peace be upon him to tell us that we need to strive to be upon what he was upon and his companions? We don't really need that specific instruction. If we are upon this belief that we're going to be split into [sects], naturally, whether the prophet peace be upon him said it or not, we can logically come to the conclusion that it's all about being upon what he is upon. So even without this addition, that specific idea is something that we should all strive for.", "Now a question that may come to mind would be: but how do we know that all the sahaba were upon one sect? How do we know that sahaba themselves didn't split ideologically? The way I look at it is this: the early Scholars, compilers of reports and opinions of the sahaba, actually were very very thorough in preserving their differences of opinions, extremely thorough. You'll find in many chapters of [fiqh] matters, you'll find them simply saying: this companion believed this, [this companion] believe that, and they differed. However, you don't find them having different ideologies; it's just matters of [fiqh] they differed on. So there was no sect early on; it was just one body of Muslims.", "Another aspect of this is: what do we gain specifically from the [73]? There are a couple of approaches that the [ulama] had in regards to this specific issue of the number in the Hadith. You had Scholars that would actually make these lengthy lists of what the 73 [sects] are made up of, and they would go into these General larger sects, and then they would start listing out what [sects] fall under them that make up the 72. However, you would find that Scholars themselves would differ when coming up with that list. One thing to keep in mind is that this should not be a problem. Scholars, when coming up with lists like that, it happens, because everyone is practicing their own [ijtihad], they're trying their best to come up with a list that's conclusive. At the same time, the difficulty here is: what makes up a sect? Sometimes the differences in ideology will be minor, but there will be other major differences, and sometimes vice versa. Are these two different labels referring to one sect, or should they be seen as two different sects? One example: you have today the main body of [Twelvers]; they have two different groups. They believe in the same books; their methodologies are really different. So should they be referred to in two different labels, or should it just be one label? And that in itself is the cause of a lot of the reasons why you have different lists and different identifications and classifications for these sects.", "This is very common when you have General narrations by the prophet peace be upon him, and of course the prophet isn't going to be listing out 73 [sects] in Hadith. The prophet peace be upon him mention other narrations that are somewhat vague in that nature, and you did have a lot of differences of opinions among Scholars. A very obvious example would be Allah having 99 Names, authentic Hadith narrated by [Hammam], from the earliest work of Hadith: Allah has 99 names. So you have Scholars listing out the 99 Names of Allah and differing in what the names are. You'll find a huge percentage in which they completely agree upon, and then you will find a smaller number in which they conflict with one another, and sometimes that has to do with the authenticity of narrations that mention some of the names, things of that nature. And it even applies to smaller reports. For example, the prophet peace be upon him said that [seven ahruf] were revealed, and then you have the scholars differing: what are these seven? For example, you'd have some saying that these are referring to different dialects, and the language of the people of Yemen; now that's like one view. Whether we know what the specific languages are or dialects are, it doesn't really make a difference. We don't have to be sure of the classifications and identifications and labelings of the 99 Names of Allah, or the list of the 73 [sects], or identifying what the tongues or dialects of the [ahruf], or any other classification.", "The same applies to the famous Hadith that speaks of 12 caliphs coming after the [prophet] peace be upon him. We know they're going to be 12 [caliphs]; however, you will find [scholars] differing in regards to their identification: are they the first 12 [caliphs] after he passes away, or are they referring to the 12 [caliphs] that are agreed upon by the ummah? So this stuff shouldn't be an issue, but for some reason, for some people it is, and it shouldn't be, to be honest.", "Another aspect to this is: some Scholars (this is a minority opinion, but this is an actual opinion) say that you're not even supposed to take the number of 73 literally, and the number 70 is supposed to be like a metaphor for many, like tens and tens, hundreds etc. In general, it's a very large number, so 70 would be like a metaphor for that. That is one position; it's not the most popular position, but it is a position that does exist.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((3, "0:03–0:42"), (44, "0:44–3:36"), (220, "3:40–5:22"), (368, "6:08–7:46"), (469, "7:49–11:13"), (676, "11:16–13:08"), (798, "13:18–13:56"), (839, "13:59–15:06"), (909, "15:09–17:42"), (1068, "17:48–20:25"), (1227, "20:27–21:02"), (1268, "21:08–21:44"),))
// U FR134:527-641 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("What other things can we learn from the Hadith? We can come to the conclusion that becoming a sect is a form of a punishment from Allah; it's a type of test, and we have to navigate and get past that test. And the good news is: the 73 sects does not mean that the [sects] are going to be split equally. So it's not 100 divided by 73 or anything like that. You will have the main body of the Muslims, it's going to be quite huge, and that's what we are today, 85 percent, and 15 is split among the other 72. Or of course maybe some of them from the 72 are extinct. Just because there's a lot of [sects] does not mean that the [sects] are split equally.", "Even in the Hadith, Ali doesn't say those specific words: he does not say that they're going to go to Hellfire. However, yes, you do find it in many reports from the different companions. However, whether or not the prophet peace be upon him did say this, there are still a few aspects to look at. First of all, it is quite a Stern warning. However, just because someone is an innovator does not mean that they're doomed to [Hell] and there's no way out. Allah can forgive, of course; that's the part of the [wa'id], and of course it's a praiseworthy thing for Allah not to break a promise, but to go back from a warning in itself.", "Now the question: how do we follow what he was upon? How do we navigate? There are 73 [sects]; how do we know which one it is? So unfortunately I'm going to have to give you a cliched answer, but what do you expect: it's by simply following the Quran and the Sunnah. It's by submitting your mind to Allah and your will, to submit him fully; not to submit your mind to your Sheikh or your Imam or your peer or whatever. It's submitting to Allah, submitting to the narrations. And again, it's not submitting to your parents, it's not submitting to family members, it's not submitting to the random guy you see on TV, it's not submitting to me, it's not submitting to my beloved panelists who I appreciate so dearly, but it's all about submitting to what Allah has ordered and what he has made prohibited.", "So keep in mind: it's a very lovely slogan, hold on to the Quran, a beautiful slogan, and because it's such a nice slogan, everyone's using it. And even though everyone's using it, not everyone's applying it. In fact, you will find people [that] claim to be [Ahl al-Sunnah] that don't apply this at all, and they'll have some crazy strange conditions that they will put between them and the reports of the Prophet peace be upon him in order to reject them.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((1444, "24:04–25:11"), (1545, "25:45–26:49"), (1644, "27:24–28:38"), (1722, "28:42–29:25"),))
// U FR134:701-820 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("When I'm simply going to my Sheikh and saying: what are your thoughts on this?, and [my Sheikh] isn't telling me that he's getting this from the prophet peace be upon him, there's a possibility that it's from a source that maybe I wouldn't be satisfied with. Maybe his position is based on the position of one of the [tabi'in]; maybe it's a position of a companion. So I would want to know if this is something [the] prophet peace be upon him said, because ultimately, at the end of the day, that's all that matters.", "And here's the thing: everyone has a [Sheikh], everyone looks up to his Sheikh, everyone is saying: my Sheikh wouldn't mess up, my Sheikh wouldn't share an opinion if it's not the right opinion, [my Sheikh] wouldn't be sharing an opinion if it's not based on Hadith. It's not the reality. In reality, in many cases a Hadith may be dismissed for the wrong reasons. Very recently I was having a discussion with some people that were quoting Scholars that were saying that we don't accept the Hadith unless it fits in with our method, then we will accept it. So you see, the method came first and then the Hadith came second, the statements, the attributions came second. This is an issue. You have Scholars that responded to this, refuted this. They said these are our major Companions, and you cannot dismiss what they said.", "Is this based on a Hadith of the Prophet peace be upon him? Can you please provide me a reference? There's absolutely nothing wrong with this. And the classical Scholars, even before they became Scholars, when they were young, when they would go to the [Sheikh], they would say: who did you hear from? Sometimes we'll mention a name, they will say: did you hear from him? And in that case he has to give an answer. In some cases they will say: I did not hear from him. This is something that existed even [among] Scholars that were of high rank, high weights, like even [Sufyan]. There's a famous example: he narrates the report, and he's asked: did you hear it from him? He said: no, I heard it from someone else. The issue is not asking, and holding yourself from asking, because that's where the confusion can occur, and that's how sometimes we end up following something that we wouldn't be following: if you knew there was someone unreliable in the middle, you probably wouldn't be following it.", "Even [al-Shafi'i] himself, sometimes he would say: I heard from the trustworthy narrator. Some other people had an issue with a specific narrator, if he mentioned where he got this information from; if you mention the name, people would [reject] it. Of course they rejected it anyways, because [al-Shafi'i] didn't mention his Source. The scholars were very careful with this, the students of Hadith were very cautious with this. They did not accept everything and anything simply because it's my Sheikh who narrated this. If this was the case, it would be chaos.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((1935, "32:15–32:55"), (1977, "32:57–34:13"), (2071, "34:31–36:04"), (2169, "36:09–36:48"),))
// U FR134:930-944 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("So in summary: simply following, sticking to the Quran, the Sunnah, through the apparent meanings, through the authentic reports, that's the best way to being of that group that the prophet peace be upon him and his companions were upon.",), "Farid Responds, “The Hadith of the 73 Sects - 2nd AIM Conference (2022)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nE0bMMLxFYw", ((2492, "41:32–42:11"),))

