#passage(("Anyone who believes anything that is not found in the Quran or the prophetic tradition, from our perspective as Muslims, we say that's not Islam. Now the Christians, not just some of them believe some wrong beliefs; we're saying the predominant majority of Christians believe in the Trinity, that is not even in the [Bible]. So no, Muslims are never influenced throughout history to believe something not even in their books.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christians Challenge Muslim On Evidences For Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M8FdFU3haa8", ((763, "12:43–13:09"),))
// U ML174:439-500 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("I'll give you a very basic example: we are supposed to call ourselves Muslims; that's what the Quran says. Allah says he named us Muslims. Now you'll have a group of people who refer to themselves as Shia, for example. And by the way, [Shia] are less than like 10% or less of Muslims; they're a minority, a lot of people don't know that. Now [Shia] will have different groups, not even agreeing within themselves; they have different [sects], different groups which disagree with each other. But let's take the overwhelming majority, which is the [Twelvers]. They will believe in 12 people called 12 imams. They will say the companions of the prophets are disbelievers, they're not good people. They will say that you have to follow these 12 imams, they know the Unseen; they will say all of these things.", "Let's use the principles in the Quran: Allah in the Quran does not mention any 12 [imams], does not mention anything about that. He says he's the only one who knows the [unseen]; they tell us: no, these guys also know the [unseen]. So if I start comparing: Quran tells us to follow the companions, they say they're disbelievers. They curse the [wives] of the Prophet; the Quran calls them the mothers of the Believers. So if you're contradicting what the book that we're both supposed to follow is telling me, then is it really hard to tell where to follow?", "Why do we care about what people are doing? There are people who always try to follow their desires: I don't want to follow what the book says, I'll make my own version of what the book says. But again, is the guidance there of how do we know what the truth is? Then you cannot blame a Creator or anything like that, because he's already told you the rules: here's the rules, this is how you know what the guidance says. Why are you not following it? Why are you looking at this guy? He's doing it wrong; who cares if he's doing it wrong, let him do it wrong. You're supposed to do it the right way; you have the means of how to do it the right way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Pakistani Ex-Muslim Confronts A Muslim On Following Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9vJWVgYof2A", ((893, "14:53–15:33"), (935, "15:35–16:03"), (970, "16:10–16:42"),))
// U ML040:1177-1207 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I'm a Muslim. That's what I am. That's what the Quran and Sunnah commands us to follow. Allah says in the Quran: don't divide yourselves into [sects]. Don't call yourself different things. The way I answer that question is using the Quran, and this is what Muslims are supposed to do. They're supposed to respond and follow their faith based on what the text says, not what their opinions are. So the difference is: Muslims can find out what the truth is, but a Christian can't. A Muslim can trace back to the preserved text of the prophet and the Quran.", "I had this discussion speaking to scholars of Shiaism: you guys believe in 12 people that are infallible, called 12 imams, after the prophet, divinely appointed, everybody must follow them. Can you show me in the Quran? You have an issue with the companions of the prophet because they had an argument with Ali. They say most of them are disbelievers. I say Allah says in the Quran they are the examples for us to follow. So what I can do with the [Shia] very easily is: here's the Quran that you say you believe in, and Allah says me and you should come back to it. Here's what the Quran teaches. You can do that. I can do that. Anybody can do that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Catholic Attempts To Disprove Islam Using Catholicism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lzIJWroR7Ec", ((3511, "58:31–59:05"), (3547, "59:07–59:57"),))

=== 45.4 Scholars, madhhabs and juristic disagreement
// NODE P11.C04.S04
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML260:37-55 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("The Quran and the Hadith come before anyone else's say. So the Quran comes first, of course, is God's Words, and then the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, which is bringing the Revelation from God as well. And then what Scholars do is that they expound upon the tradition, what is mentioned in Quran, Hadith, etc. We don't worship Scholars, even the main Scholars. In Islam there's schools of thought, for example, and all of the Scholars, the 4 Scholars of the schools of thought, they said that: \"If what I say goes against what the Prophet says or the Quran, reject it!\" This is a fundamental thing. And Imam Malik, for example, which is the head of the Maliki School Of Thought, he said that: \"Everyone you take from and you reject, except the person in this grave,\" and he pointed at Madina, to the Prophet's grave. Meaning only the Prophet, we take everything he says, because it's Revelation. But any other human being, you take from and you reject; they make mistakes, they say correct things and incorrect things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Born Again Christian Attempts To Prove The Divinity Of Jesus! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfTsV4J2jmI", ((110, "1:50–2:39"),))
// U ML246:213-275 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("No one said in this conversation, from my side, that the books behind me are a revelation. Whatever is in this book, unless it's backed up by the Quran or Hadith, is not binding for me as a Muslim. So there are a lot of things that can be written here in this voluminous work that I can easily reject. I can say there's an opinion of a scholar at a specific point in time, but I do not need to accept it, because that's not a revelation coming from God; that is expounding on the revelation. It's just explaining to me the revelation. But somehow you're conflating the explanation of revelation as a revelation, which obviously no one makes that point ever in history.", "So if a scholar gives evidence from Quran and Hadith, I follow it because it's Quran and Hadith, not because it's a scholarly statement. He's just explaining the meaning of that revelation; it is not introducing a new revelation, or a new meaning, or an opposite meaning to what the text already says.", "I don't base my life on my own interpretation of the Quran. We believe that is what misguided the previous civilisation. I base everything on the Quran and Hadith and the life of the Prophet: how he understood the Quran, how he applied the Quran; and his Companions, how they understood the Quran, how they applied the Quran; and their students, and their students, and the scholars who expound the meaning of the Quran. The Quran itself tells me to learn from them. The reason I would disagree with them or reject their positions is because it's not backed up by evidence, while another position opposite to them will be backed up by evidence. So it's not about my personal preference; it's about what the text shows me to follow and not to follow.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Confronts Muslim On The Clarity Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ozeX5ELYDh0", ((702, "11:42–12:39"), (792, "13:12–13:28"), (825, "13:45–14:52"),))
// U ML025:821-839 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Following the school of thought is more for students of knowledge. For lay men, they would ask someone they trust, their religion and piety, and they're known in the community to be respected, and they will give them evidences, and they will follow their evidences. But following the schools of thought, the schools of thought has assisted, which is why student of knowledge will stick to studying a school of thought, their principles and things like that, and then they can even change to a different school of thought. Nobody has to blindly follow fully a school of thought if they realize that a school of thought is wrong about something, because every school of thought is wrong about something here or there.", "None of them say that they're infallible. So we will go back to the evidence always, and the evidence is that which is going to lead us to be sure what we should follow and what we shouldn't follow. [Jurisprudence] is not hadith. [Jurisprudence] is extrapolating rulings from the Quran and Sunnah using principles. The Quran is there, and then we come to a ruling using them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Inquisitive Agnostic Grills Muslim On Cousin Marriage And Racism In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fvVrT_WHQxg", ((2738, "45:38–46:10"), (2785, "46:25–46:52"),))
// U ML001:263-307 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The correct madhab is the madhab of prophet Muhammad. What the prophet taught, what the prophet said, is what you should follow. Whichever scholar, whichever madhab brings you the correct evidences, what the prophet said, is what you're supposed to follow. Nothing in Islam, anywhere, any text tells you you need to have a [madhab].", "The people who follow Madhab are trying to follow scholars that saved them the time. The scholar saves you the time. He already went through the books. He gives you the conclusion. You gather all the narrations, you bring [them] in one place, and you explain to the person: here's the explanation based on all of the narrations. So the scholar saves you the time. You follow a madhab because the scholar is more knowledgeable in that sense, that he already spent that time. Many people don't have the time or haven't spent the time. So we take the conclusion. It saves you the time. It's like a summary of a book: someone wrote the summary of a book, or the most important points that you need. You open it and you read it. Rather, it saves you the time than going and reading the entire thing. So this is what scholarship is. I follow the evidence. Where is the evidence? The evidence is here. You follow the scholar in giving you the evidence. The scholar has given me the evidence. The evidence is valid, it's correct; I follow the evidence the scholar has given me. That's what you do. You don't make it complicated for yourself.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Confronts A Muslim About The Treatment Of Jews In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IzncpPt4QVo", ((748, "12:28–12:58"), (784, "13:04–14:00"),))
// U ML258:250-329 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("That means you have تعصُّب (Fanaticism) for it. That means that you don't take anything else: this is the only.., this is the way, this is everything. Which a lot of people do, especially in certain parts from the Indian subcontinent etc. So he's saying that this is not permissible. He says that he would say: \"I would only follow the Madhab (School of Thought), I do not go anywhere else, and not take anything else, only the Madhab of Imam Ahmad\", or only the Madhab Shafi’i, etc. He's saying that's not permissible. The only Madhab that you do not go against, that you do not oppose, that you follow in every.. (you say \"that's the end-all and be-all\") is the teachings of the Prophetﷺ, meaning the Hadith, obviously, and the tradition of the Prophetﷺ. This is the only Madhab that you take. This is the Madhab of the Muslims. It's obedience and following the Prophetﷺ. لا متبوع بحقّ إلا محمدٍﷺ (There's one to be worthy of being followed absolutely and completely) is The Prophet Muhammadﷺ. No one else!", "Now, there's a common argument that people make. The people who just stick to the Madhab, and the Madhab is everything, they will say to you: \"But didn't Shafi'i follow the Prophetﷺ? Didn’t Imam Ahmad follow the Prophetﷺ? Wasn't their Madhab following the Prophetﷺ?\" Yes! But that doesn't mean that every single person, what they said is right. And the evidence for that is that they disagreed with one another: Imam Ahmed disagreed with Shafi'i, and Shafi'i disagreed with Malik, and they disagreed with one another, which shows that if they're all, as you try to claim, sourcing the Hadith, that means they will not disagree with each other. If they disagree with each other, that means that some of them can be correct and some of them can be wrong. That means you cannot 100% take every single thing they say as if it is Revelation. You cannot do that with the Madhab.", "Then he says (it's a very strong point): none of the 4 Imams of the Madhahib have said to you \"Just follow me, follow my Madhab!\" In fact, all of them said: \"If what I say goes against the Hadith or the Quran, then reject it!\" With them saying this, it is evidence by itself that they can make mistakes, and certain positions they can take can be rejected. And he's adding to it that they were students of each other. They were the students of one another, and you didn't have Imam Ahmad saying: \"I'm a Shafi'i Madhab\". He disagreed with him! Even the companions of Imam Shafi'i like Almuzani المُزني disagreed with him on certain issues, and the companions of Abu Hanifah etc. disagreed with him. So people need to understand that even the main students of these Imams, they even disagreed with the Imams. And obviously, we're talking about Jurisprudence and Fiqh in these issues. So you cannot come and say: \"This is end-all, be-all, and 100%, what is said is the truth.\"", "Don't think that the Sheikh is saying that you cannot follow a Madhab. What he's saying is that you cannot force people to take a Madhab, which many people do sadly. He's saying you cannot say that you have to be upon a specific Madhab to another individual. You cannot do that. But if you yourself want to follow a Madhab, there's no issue with that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| No Bible Contradictions? Following Mazhab? Mahdi Is Dajjal and more.. | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oDQFEUUc7kQ", ((1038, "17:18–18:33"), (1137, "18:57–19:36"), (1190, "19:50–21:02"), (1312, "21:52–22:08"),))
// U ML236:1151-1232 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("Madhab in the Arabic language means that you direct yourself; ذهب is that he directs himself, his opinion towards a specific direction. That's what Madhab means. When you say the Madhab of Ahmed, this is where he directed his opinion towards; the Madhab of Shafi'i, that's where he directed his opinion towards. When you say ashShafi'iya, then they are the people who took the principles of Imam (Leader) Shafi'i, and applied the same principles in deriving rulings. Same with every other School of Thought.", "Now, how many Madhahib are there? Four? No. Madhahib were many, many more. The ones that reach us today that we're following are four. There was the Madhab of Awza'i, the Madhab of alHassan alBasri; there was many, many Madhahib; the Madhab of Sufyan atThawri. These were Madhahib, but the students of these السلف الصالح (The righteous predecessors), they were not writing and collecting and establishing the same way. Madhab adhDhahiri is very well known; Ibn Hazm, if you hear about him, he's a follower of Madhab adhDhahiri, by Abu Dawood adhDhahiri; it's another Madhab. But the ones that are established and have reached us are 4, because their students, they collected and wrote it and spread it around. And these 4 are the ones we know: the Shafi'i, the Hanbali, the Maliki, and the Hanafi. The only reason is because their students expounded upon them.", "Now, do these Madhahib agree with each other in everything? And if the answer is no, then someone has to be wrong and someone has to be right. We're not talking about reward now, because they're all reward for their أجتهاد (ijtihad: independent juristic reasoning). If they did correct independent reasoning, trying to find the evidences, and they made a mistake, Allah rewards them for trying. If they did and said the truth, they get two rewards: one for trying and one for finding the truth, or saying the truth, or teaching the truth. But if they disagree, then when it comes to Quran and Sunnah, there is one correct and one incorrect. And if this is the case, then that which establishes the truth is the evidences from the Quran and the Sunnah.", "But what about following a Madhab? You can follow a Madhab. And the reason you can, especially if you're going to become a student of knowledge, is it gives you structure. It makes things easier, learning and studying easier for you. It gives you principles, gives you books of explanation of Ahadith. It gives you where you can go, where you cannot go; it gives you structure, it helps you learning. But most of the people, when they become علماء (scholars), they study one Madhab and then the other Madhab and then the other, and then they become a مجتهد (Mujtahid: doing ijtihad) in the Madhab. That's how it happens. But you start somewhere; you start with a Madhab. There is no issues with learning and studying a Madhab.", "But the problem is when your Madhab has a view which is incorrect, that the majority reject, and there's clear evidences, but you still say: I'm following the Madhab. The Madhab is the Madhab, it's my Madhab. No! This case is NOT a valid disagreement, in which there are strong evidences for both sides, and it's an issue which is very difficult. We have Companions here, Companions there, evidences here and evidences there, which is OK; you can take different positions. But when it is something that has no strong evidences, very weak evidences, and the opposite has very clear teachings from the Prophetﷺ, you cannot say: I'm following my Madhab! There's no تعصب (Ta'assub: fanaticism), means just blind, 100% following of anyone, except the Messengerﷺ. So if someone wants to become a student of knowledge and follow a Madhab, you're welcome to do so, understanding this and bearing it in mind: when there is overwhelming evidence against the Madhab that you're in, you take that, but you stick with the other things and the principles of your Madhab.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Islam Unveiled: Compelling Evidence For The Truthfulness Of Islam | Chicago - USA | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AUFsBco_CF0", ((3796, "1:03:16–1:03:42"), (3824, "1:03:44–1:04:37"), (3880, "1:04:40–1:05:13"), (3922, "1:05:22–1:05:54"), (3959, "1:05:59–1:07:01"),))
// U ML354:255-340 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("I follow what is in the Quran and Sunnah. If you want me to follow a مذهب, bring me something in the Quran and Sunnah that tells me I have to follow a مذهب. I take from specific imams that you don’t necessarily need to know who they are by name. But I do take my knowledge from scholars. Where do you think I take my knowledge from? I take my knowledge from the علماء (scholars). And the scholars take their knowledge from the Quran and Sunnah. So it doesn’t contradict what I said. So, when I say I take from the Quran and Sunnah, how does that contradict taking from the Scholars? What مذهب did Abu Bakr follow? I’m on the same مذهب. What مذهب did Abdullah ibn Abbas follow?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Aggressive Ashari Interrogates Muhammed Ali On Stream!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7SGfzzryjY", ((498, "8:18–10:25"),))
// U ML246:277-362 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("I'll give you a very basic example. There are Schools of Thought in Jurisprudence. For example, there's a School of Thought called the Hanafi Jurisprudence الفقه الحنفي. And they have a position on marriage: when you conduct the marriage ceremony, is there a necessity for us to have two witnesses during the marriage ceremony? And the position of the Hanafi, for example, is you don't need two witnesses. The overwhelming majority of the rest of the scholars say: no, you need witnesses. Now, in this case, when you look at the Hanafi position, they don't have evidence saying that you don't need it, while the other side has clear cut evidence from the Prophet that there is no marriage without two witnesses, and any marriage that was conducted without two witnesses is considered invalid. So in this case, someone might ask: OK, why did they take that position to begin with? The reason is that the narration of the Prophet did not reach them. The information did not reach them. So they based the ruling without the evidence reaching them, while we still have evidence from the other overwhelming majority of scholars. So we do not take that position of that scholar, because he does not have evidence to support this claim.", "The overwhelming majority of scholars already have the evidence reaching them, while one person in this case, or one view, or one school of thought did not, in this particular matter, get the evidence. But the evidence was there. So if one individual somewhere in some place did not hear evidence for something, that does not mean that there was a latency of God's Guidance reaching the people. That just means that that one individual did not hear that specific point regarding that specific issue, which is not the majority of the Muslims!", "Abu Hanifa himself -may Allah have mercy on him-, not only him but all of the Imams of the 4 schools of thought in Islam: whenever they saw evidence of opposing some of what they believed in, they changed it. A prominent example of that is Imam Ashafi'i, who was another imam of another School of [Thought], the Shafi'i School of Thought. He had one position when he was in one land, and when he traveled in Iraq and Egypt, he changed many of his views when he came across a lot of the evidence that he did not know before. So he changed his previous positions to new positions.", "And this is the easy way for us; the work is all done. What we need to do as Muslims is we look at the scholarly opinions. We look at the evidence. Most of the time, the overwhelming majority of the scholars will have the evidence, and the people who didn't, they probably have missed the evidence here or there, which is very easily demonstrated, because they don't have evidence to support their point. So we will go with the overwhelming majority of the scholars. And the Guidance is already there and present in every civilisation, and if you read these books and research, you'll find it. If you're referring to Jurisprudence, most of the work is done, unless there's some new thing happening today, technology or this or that. Other than that, really, the work is done. That's why we have massive voluminous works like this, because these massive voluminous works expand on these evidences and positions.", "The reason is very simple. The reason is not everyone defers to the evidence when there's a disagreement. There are people who have diseases in their hearts. There are people who like to follow their own desires. There are people who intentionally want to change the text. There are people with harmful intentions. There are hypocrites that pretend to be Muslims while they're not Muslims. There are many reasons why you will have certain people disagreeing and changing the teachings of Islam, because they just don't like what the text says. And that does not change the reality that the Guidance is there, and if you were to look, you would find the Guidance in the end. And the Quran itself teaches you: if you disagree over anything, you have to return it to the text. It's mentioned explicitly in the Quran chapter 4 (Annissa4:59): (Obey Allah, obey the Messenger and those in authority among you.. And if you disagree over anything, return it to Allah and the Messenger). So Allah tells us what to do, and not everyone follows the instructions given in the Quran.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Confronts Muslim On The Clarity Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ozeX5ELYDh0", ((917, "15:17–16:16"), (996, "16:36–17:11"), (1039, "17:19–17:54"), (1078, "17:58–18:44"), (1147, "19:07–19:50"),))
