#passage(("Learning islamic history is different than western history. Alhamdulillah, we are blessed with eyewitness sources, [we] are blessed with information that will allow us to study these reports. We don't even need to put all different types and levels of analysis to reconcile between various contradicting, seemingly equal reports, due to [isnad]; it really clears everything up.", "Just a word of caution for anyone who's studying [al-Jamal] and the [martyrdom] of uthman, especially in regards to those who want to speak on the subject, write about the subject: it's very important to be objective and to stick with the rules of the muhaddithin, which will of course sometimes cause you to accept reports that you don't like. And there's a statement by sheikh [Muqbil], a beautiful statement where he talks about history, and he says: especially in history we need to focus on the rules of the hadith, and the reason is because every single sect is going to pick and choose whatever they like and say this is the truth. But of course, if you apply hadith rules, then no, that's not going to happen. When you apply hadith rules, you're going to have an objective history and not a subjective history. The general rules, of course: always go to primary sources; if primary sources are lost, you go to secondary sources. Alhamdulillah, a lot of secondary sources have preserved what has been lost in the past.", "Now this topic, the topic of the martyrdom of [Uthman] [and al-Jamal], a question always reoccurs, which is: is it permissible to even talk about these topics? You'll find [mashayikh] who say: la, this is a different time; we're living a different time now, where everything is out, to the extent where if you are quiet about some matters, this could cause a fitna. And history does have some matters that are gray; not everything is black and white, and there are some things that we often do have difficulty expressing.", "At the end of the day, what I'm doing is I'm just sharing what's in history books. I'm sharing the authentic history. I'm not attacking or condemning; I'm just sharing what's already there. I'm not coming up with anything new, not even interpretations. These are just the history as it was documented; what I'm doing is I'm just gathering it in one place.",), "Farid Responds, “The Martyrdom of Uthman”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CeD072hbK0w", ((30, "0:30–1:02"), (63, "1:03–2:16"), (136, "2:16–3:07"), (223, "3:43–4:03"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML064:1275-1308 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("He says that the Companions of The Prophetﷺ were fighting, and fighting for caliphate, and we want to disguise this history. That's also false. We have all of these historical resources talking about the dispute that happened at the time of 'Ali ibn abi Talib and Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, which is a Companion of The Prophetﷺ. We have the differences that happened between them and the battles that happened between them, like \"Siffin\" and \"alJamal\"; all of these battles, history record of them: why did they happen, how did they happen, and all of that, and these differences and conflicts between the Companions.", "In fact, The Prophet himself tells us that the nation will divide after him; Muslims will divide. And he tells some of the Companions that they will be killed. Like he tells us that 'Uthman, the third caliphate, will be killed. The second caliphate will be killed, will die as a martyr. And he tells 'Uthman specifically that there will be a great trial that will happen to him, and he has to be patient. And he was killed in a riot that happened against him, where people killed him. So not only The Prophet tells us these things that happened, but we have a history record of all of these disagreements.", "Muslims are not the ones who hide their history. In fact, most of the critiques that comes against Muslims comes from their own sources, because we record the true history of what happened. So people who come to critique us, they go to the Tarikh atTabari and Ibn Ishaq and whatever, and they take some Narrations, some weak and some not weak, and then they try to make these accusations and these arguments and these critiques against the Islamic tradition, because we have what happened in history in our books. We don't hide it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((4263, "1:11:03–1:11:36"), (4298, "1:11:38–1:12:02"), (4325, "1:12:05–1:12:25"),))

=== 46.3 The martyrdom of Uthman
// NODE P11.C05.S03
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR223:116-1289 | Farid Responds | 37
#passage(("What are the main sources of [Maqtal] Uthman? Other important sources include hadith works, which has a relatively thick section about the [Maqtal Uthman]. As to what is the best source for [Maqtal] Uthman, it's [Tarikh al-Madinah]: he has 200 pages on Uthman, and a large portion, 100 pages, about what occurred, what led to the death of Uthman. The best source hands down on the subject.", "So what's the issue with [Sayf ibn Umar] [al-Tamimi]? Well, the issue that has been brought up recently, well, it's been around for a while, but it's been the focus of a lot of attention recently, it's the issue of him narrating stuff that has to do with Abdullah bin [Saba]. Of course we'll find some people rejecting the very existence of Abdullah bin [Saba], which is an unfair approach, to be honest, because you will find narrations that come through the paths of others, excluding [Sayf]. But Abdullah bin [Saba] really doesn't seem to have to do anything with [Maqtal] Uthman, and seems to have nothing to do with [al-Jamal] in reality. In other narrations, in other lengthy historical reports and short hadith reports, nothing about Abdullah bin [Saba].", "There's another issue, which is he relies on his teachers, who we frankly have no idea who these people are, anonymous teachers, and often these people don't mention who they're getting their narrations from. So we pretty much can't rely on [Sayf]. So what we have to do in reality is we have to rely on mostly short reports, short authentic reports, and we have to put them in place like a jigsaw puzzle, and then we come to reality. You have the pro-[Shi'i] source, and I think there's very clear evidence of fabrication by him, not just him narrating fabrications, but him altering a report to suit his agenda.", "We all seem to think that we know what happened to Uthman, but to be honest, even when I was doing some research, I was learning some new things that I wasn't aware of. The best source for [Maqtal] Uthman, contemporary source, is the book by [Dr. Ghabban], two volumes. The first half of the first volume is just him speaking about the events, and the second half and the second volume is [takhrij], grading of narrations: he lists hundreds of narrations and grades them one by one. It's great work, and it's a very important source for those who are interested in learning about [Uthman].", "So what are the key events? First of all, Uthman comes to power in the year 24 after hijrah, and you find a lot of information about the prosperity, to the extent that there's this report that says that there was a horse that was sold for a hundred thousand dirhams during the time of [Uthman]. That's how rich people became, and that's like the prices that existed at the time. Of course you're not going to have equality and everyone's filthy rich, but there was a lot of money in society at the time.", "10 years later was the day of [al-Jara'ah], in Kufa. What happened is he went to Medina; on his way back the [Kufans] came out and prevented him from returning. So that was the first part of the fitnah, before the fitna really grew. Soon afterwards, in Shawwal in the year 35, Mohammed bin [Abi Hudhayfah] takes over Egypt while the representative, Abdullah bin Abi [Sarh], was out of town. So you have a couple of takeovers happening here and there. What Muhammad [ibn Abi Hudhayfah] does is he sends [Abd al-Rahman] bin [Udays] to Medina; he arrives towards the end of the year. And in the Basra, [Hukaym ibn Jabalah] has his troops, and they head towards the Medina, and they all get there apparently around the same time.", "When Uthman was faced with these rebels, there was a lot of back and forth, and ultimately Uthman sent [Jabir], actually with [Muhammad ibn Maslamah], and they went with 50 men, and they met the Egyptians, who apparently were the biggest force. And one of the Egyptians came with tears in his eyes and a sword in his hand, and he had a [mushaf] strung around his neck, and he said something like: we are ordered by this to use this to fight in regards to what we find in here. And then Muhammad said to him: well, actually, we were ordered by this to use this in what was in this before you were born. And then that pretty much diffused a lot of what happened. A man comes to [Jabir] and says: they're gonna be back. This is an authentic hadith. How does this man know? We have no idea.", "Soon we find out that these people do indeed come back. They claim that they have found a letter that was written by Uthman to Abdullah bin [Sa'd] in Egypt ordering the death of these people. They come back and there's a siege, and Uthman is prevented from drinking water. Now we don't know if the siege occurred from the beginning, but basically Uthman is apparently in siege for around 40 days, and basically Muhammad bin [Abi Bakr] comes into Uthman, into his place, pulls him by the beard, calls out one of his men, who grabs a thick arrow and stabs Uthman, and that's the end of Uthman, at the hands of Muhammad and those that were with him.", "Of course it's very important, before even moving on, to talk about what are the merits of Uthman, just to have this in mind while listening to the analysis of what caused these people to even revolt in the first place. So the merits of Uthman, we're familiar with a lot of them: the angels are shy from Uthman. Rasulullah said to Abu Musa: [allow] Uthman to enter and give him glad tidings of entering the heaven; however, there will be an affliction that occurs to him.", "And of course there are some other matters that we all agree upon and no one can deny, which is that Rasulullah gave Uthman two of his daughters. We don't even need the hadith for this. So some people say: la, they weren't his real daughters, they were the daughters of Khadijah. You have the daughters of your deceased wife, and then she passes away, and then you give this man her second daughter: that implies you have good thoughts, you have good feelings about this person; it doesn't imply this person is an evil person. And there's this funny theory that says that Uthman killed them both because they both died of young age. This is an actual theory, which is of course silly, because if your first daughter dies, you're surely not going to give your second daughter the same person.", "Why was Uthman killed? It seems like the actual reasons, the real reasons, are just a few. [Aisha] is very clear as to what the reasons are, in an authentic report where she says that we were angered by Uthman. Aisha was angered at Uthman due to some things that occurred, and she lists those things as three things: the first is the youth that were put into power, and the fortification of some fortified areas, and whipping and beating with sticks. Unfortunately we don't have any authentic information in regards to the whipping and the beating; we don't know what that's about, so I can't really comment on that.", "The matter of fortification: Uthman himself explains in an authentic narration that this was indeed an issue that was brought up to him. Now there is a ruling that land cannot be fortified: one is supposed to allow camels to graze freely; if someone enters and starts grazing where your camels are grazing, you can't do anything about it. However, this was a matter brought up to Uthman: how come you are fortifying this area for the camels? And Uthman says: well, these are not my camels, these are the camels of charity, of the state, and [Umar] did before me; it's not something that I've done and I just came up with, so what's your issue?", "So the only real issue, to be honest, in regards to these matters is the representative of Uthman, and this is where the accusations of nepotism comes in. We hear that this is an issue with Uthman, that Uthman put into power his relatives. This is a very very interesting topic. So who did Uthman put into power? He put into power [Sa'id]; he's of course from [Banu Umayyah]. He put into power [Muawiyah ibn Abi] Sufyan, who is his father's cousin's son, of course [Banu Umayyah].", "Now we all have this assumption: we assume that when someone puts a lot of people into power, they're these useless people, they're chilling on their couches, and you have a ruler saying: okay, you're the minister of finance, you're the minister of interior, you're the minister of whatever. That's the image that we have in mind: these are just some useless people that were put into positions of power. But if we look into their CVs, amazing people, or at least to some extent. Well, there's an issue with one or two of them, but we'll see as to why those issues are irrelevant.", "In the year 30, he was caught drinking, and therefore he was taken to Uthman. There is a narration that says that he was drinking, [praying] fajr; when he was done with fajr, they said: you prayed [four]; and he said: you want me to pray some more? So this is a point against Uthman, right? He puts a drunkard into power. Well, first of all, we don't know about drinking before; we have not heard of this. He's taken to Uthman, and Uthman, his brother-in-law, his step-brother, he has the man flogged. This is justice: he has the man flogged, he's stripped off his position, he puts in his position, again, his relative. So this is a bad mark against Uthman, right?", "Now the thing is, again, he's got a good CV as well: he is the conqueror of [Tabaristan] and is one of the two main writers of the Quran, [Zayd ibn Thabit] and [Sa'id]; they're the two heads of the committee. So far so good. He also has a great CV: the [Basrans] themselves liked him, so no one apparently had a major issue with that. [Ibn Amir], he's the conqueror of Faris. He's the conqueror of Africa. And everyone has a great CV. He actually was even put into power by Umar, and in his time he conquered Cyprus, and Uthman extended his power throughout all of [Sham]; he initially just had [Dimashq]. And overall it seems like Uthman didn't really do too bad of a job with his choices.", "Now there's a very important matter, which is: is there any other ulterior motives as to why this rebellion occurred? Because if you think about it, the fortification of Uthman in Medina for the camels, what does this have to do with you if you're in Egypt, or if you're in Basra, or if you're in Kufa? To be honest, it doesn't really make much sense. However, if you look into the books of history, you'll find that there seems to be a personal motive involved into the death of Uthman. And remember what I said earlier about [Sa'id ibn al-As] being not allowed to enter the Kufa: this has a lot to do with that.", "So what happens in the year 33: [Sa'id ibn al-As] gathers some [Kufans], some honorable [Kufans], perhaps not too honorable, and he's with one of his guards, and there's a discussion that occurs. Some of the [Kufans] start praising: he is a very generous man. And it was said: of course he's generous, he has [Nashastaj], an area [in] Faris apparently, very wealthy bountiful land that [Talhah] had, so it's natural to be generous. So Abdulrahman, the guard for Uthman, says: I wish that you had the [Sawad]; I wish you had this, and then you could be even more generous. Now the issue is, this land is the land of the [Kufans], and the [Kufans] flipped out; they started to attack Abdulrahman, they beat him up in the [majlis]; I think they even beat up his son in the [majlis], which is ironic; it's just crazy.", "However, this general report is not just narrated by [Sayf]; the same idea is narrated by [al-Waqidi], who is also another liar, but alhamdulillah, it's narrated by [al-Mada'ini], who is a major reliable historian. Now, even if [his] chain is [not] the cleanest chain, however, the very fact that you have all these historians agreeing to the general idea in regards to this report, the [tasyir] that occurs, which I'm about to explain, suggests that this report is indeed authentic, or at least the general idea.", "\"We have some major issues here in Kufa, and we need to solve this matter; do something about these people.\" So Uthman has them exiled, and they're exiled to [Muawiyah] in the [Sham]. There's a report that says that he was nice to them; there's a report that says that he imprisoned them. Later on they're exiled to Abdul Rahman bin Walid [in Hims]. So these people were exiled twice, and then they're not allowed to re-enter the Kufa; some reports say they are. So basically their position as honorable people being exiled back and forth from land to land is one of the major issues. We soon learn, according to an authentic report, I believe in [Tarikh Khalifah], that one of the conditions that were set by the rebels is that those that are exiled are to be returned, implying that this is an authentic narration.", "There's another report that's narrated by [Qatadah], [Ibn Sirin] as well, again in these sources. One of the people that was exiled by Uthman came to Uthman, and [Sa'sa'ah] is a name that we should keep in mind; he's going to come up. First of all, Uthman says: let me talk to one of you and negotiate, see what's going on. This is when Uthman is being surrounded by these rebels. And [Sa'sa'ah] approaches him, and Uthman says: don't you have anyone else other than this guy? And he insults him in Arabic; it's a very interesting insult, a very very strange insult. In any case, [Sa'sa'ah] says: we were forced to leave our homes because we said that Allah is our lord, quoting verses 39-41. And Uthman says: you're lying; the verse is about us; we were exiled from Makkah; we had to leave Makkah because we said our lord is Allah. So that was Uthman's reply. So everything indicates that there is a personal motive as to why Uthman was killed.", "So who are these people that Uthman had exiled? Some are very important names. There's an agreement by these four historians: they all agree that [Sa'sa'ah] is one of these people; they all agree that Kumail bin Ziyad is one of these people, [Kumail], a very common name that we hear about. Three of them, except [Sayf], even mentioned the brother of [Sa'sa'ah]. So some of these names you have to keep in mind.", "Now a very important thing that comes to mind is: what about the Sahaba? What about the involvement of the Sahaba? This is a very important matter. So you have two camps in regards to the Sahaba that were alive at the time: you have pro-Uthman companions; of course this means that you have anti-Uthman companions. The pro-[Uthman] companions include [Ali ibn Abi] Talib, al-Hassan ibn Ali: you have a good number of narrations that speak about al-Hasan defending Uthman, being the last person to leave Uthman, always sticking with Uthman; there's a narration about him being attacked as well. Of course, the [Muhajirun] and the Ansar; of course, very famous: a report that Ansar came to Uthman and they said: Uthman, allow us to be the Ansar twice, once during time of Rasulallah, and now with you.", "And of course now you have the issue, which is those that were anti-Uthman. And by anti-[Uthman], of course I don't mean that you had Sahaba that wanted Uthman dead; what I mean was that there were some Sahaba that were against the policies of Uthman, and that they were initially on the side of the rebels when it came to the rebels' demands.", "Firstly we have Aisha, as I've mentioned earlier. Who is Aisha? As we all know, the closest wife to Rasulullah, the wife of Rasulullah in Jannah. We also know that is where, in the house of [Aisha], Rasulullah passed away. So what caused Aisha to have this negative opinion of Uthman? Well, she says so herself, as I mentioned earlier. Now Aisha goes on to say that we were angered for you due to this; however, we would be unjust if we were not angry at you for transgressing in three: in the sanctity of the khilafah, in the sanctity of the land, meaning Medina, and the sanctity of the month. And Aisha's repentance, of course, her act of showing remorse, was seeking the blood of Uthman.", "Now as for Talhah, as we all know, is one of the ten that's promised paradise. His hand was paralyzed during the day of [Uhud], and Talhah was of course with Rasulullah in all his battles, with exception of [Badr]. There is one theory as to why he felt negative towards Uthman. In my opinion, it's because, flashback 10 years or 12 years earlier, the reason why Uthman is in power is because Omar put the six in [shura], and three of them abdicated their positions: you had Zubair abdicating his position for Ali, and you had Talha abdicating his position for Uthman. So, in a way, he felt that he is to be accountable, and that's why Talhah didn't really do much in support of Uthman. Now Talha himself, again, his act in showing remorse was: he also was with [Aisha] when they were seeking the blood of Uthman. When asked, he says: there was from me in Uthman a matter that there is no repentance except that my blood would be spilled, and seeking his blood: that was what Talhah felt would be sufficient as a repentance for him.", "So now we have people who are anti-[Uthman], but does that mean that they are killers of [Uthman]? Who are the killers of Uthman? Abdulrahman bin [Udays] is mentioned as a Sahabi; I honestly did not find evidence of him being a Sahabi. The other person that was accused of killing [Uthman], or actually it's not an accusation, this is a fact: what does this mean to us? It's possible: you can have someone who's a son of [Abu Bakr]; you have the son of [Nuh], who was a disbeliever. What does this mean to the concept of Sahaba? Does this mean that, therefore, no more Sahaba? Well, first of all: like an infant when Rasulullah passed away, so he doesn't really count as a Sahabi.", "And to be honest, I haven't found anyone who was a Sahabi that was definitely involved in [Maqtal] Uthman. However, even if there was, it wouldn't be an issue; this does not end the concept of Sahaba. We treat examples like these as exceptions, and we do find [the Salaf] doing this; this is something that's perfectly normal. You'll find this in their works: when speaking about questionable Sahaba, they have no issues saying this guy is an exception. Also keep in mind [adalat al-Sahaba]; this is a lengthy topic. But even if we assume that there is a hypothetical Sahabi, we treat this person as an exception.", "So what does all this have to do with [al-Jamal]? We now have an idea what the motivations of the killers of Uthman were. We do know now of the motivations of [Aisha] and Talhah, and why they specifically are the ones seeking vengeance for Uthman: they felt guilty, because they, as opposed to Ali (Ali did what he could, and [Hasan] did what he could, all these people did), [Aisha and Talhah] didn't do anything. They felt so guilty that they are the ones that took action, of course with [al-Zubayr] as well, Abdullah Zubair.", "And most importantly, perhaps, we now have an idea as to why Ali didn't take much action after the death of Uthman. Why didn't Ali take action? Because he tried to do all he could when he was alive. And there's another reason: [al-Tujibi], the killer of Uthman, was killed; he was killed on the same day, and this report is narrated authentically by Hasan Basri; it's in [Tarikh al-Madinah]. A bunch of historians mention this; they speak about [al-Tujibi] being killed. So in Ali's perspective, the killer of [Uthman] was killed, and there's not much more that we can do. Now in the view of Zubair, we're killing everyone who had anything to do with the death of Uthman.", "There are multiple reports about Muhammad: did he hold Uthman and then let Uthman go and left, after Uthman said to him: this is not your place, oh son of my brother? That's not authentic. The more authentic report is [Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr] signaling to a man to come and kill Uthman.", "There are so many examples of Sahaba telling Uthman: we're going to come and defend you; them putting on their armor, coming ready for a battle, and Uthman just telling them to leave. And this is due to a narration: there are a bunch of narrations that explicitly say that he's going to go through a hardship. [Ibn Umar] himself speaks to Uthman (this is also an authentic report), and he basically says to him something along the lines of: what's the most that they can do to you? Can they do anything to you after you're dead? So he makes [Uthman] accepting of what's about to occur. And Uthman didn't want bloodshed.", "There's an authentic hadith in which Rasulallah says: if Allah gives you a shirt, and you are asked to remove the shirt or force remove the shirt, then don't remove the shirt, something along those lines. That's definitely authentic. However, the dream is a narration in which Uthman says: I had a dream last night, and I dreamt that I was with Rasulullah, and he said: fast today, because you will be having [iftar] with us tomorrow. And this report seems authentic from the narration of [Nafi] and Ibn Umar; however, there's a more authentic report that says [Nafi] alone and not Ibn Omar, so there is this hidden defect in the chain, so we cannot assume that this is authentic.", "As for the reports that say that the blood of Uthman was [spilt on the mushaf], I'm not sure about the authenticity of that; it is found in a lot of reports. However, you'll find some [masahif] out there, if I'm not mistaken, that all have [tomato] juice on [fa-sayakfikahum]: it's not blood, basically, it's fake blood. So basically someone has a very old [mushaf]; he wants this [mushaf] to gain popularity, and they'll pour some tomato juice, and that's pretty much it.", "Did the Sahaba anticipate this? Because there was hajj at the time; some people went to [hajj] as well; [Uthman] even sent Ibn Abbas to lead hajj at the time. It does not seem like it was anticipated, to be honest. There's a report by Aisha even saying that this was not expected. Of course, if Aisha knew this was going to happen, she would have done something about it as well. Those that were involved in the murder of [Uthman] eventually become with Ali, join [Ali] and join his troops, and some of them even having higher ranks.", "The issue with these reports that give more detail is that we don't know anything about them: there's a big question mark about them, their status, their credibility, and sometimes we find information from these people that are too detailed. So I stuck with the narrative in [Hasan al-Basri] from Wathab, who's also a servant of Uthman; however, there is a valid reason for us to trust his reports, which is he did take part. It's not like we know nothing about this person. So I'm sticking specifically with the safest information. And definitely we can look into these reports, these reports by anonymous eyewitnesses, because yes, they are anonymous, but they are eyewitnesses, and definitely we can study these reports and come to a conclusion as to which of these details have strength or not; but that would be a topic for another day, the details of specifically what happened to Uthman. So I just chose the safe route, because the main focus isn't how Uthman died, but rather why was he killed and who killed him.", "I believe it's the personal reason that was the main, because if you think about it, the personal reason is petty, but if you have a grudge, you will do some crazy things. Being exiled itself, that's hurting your pride; it is a form of humiliation, no doubt. [Al-Ashtar], by the way, is one of the people that took part in preventing Saeed from entering, and look what it led him to do: preventing the representative of the khalifa from entering, and then ultimately him himself gathering troops and going to Medina. It's not a small thing.",), "Farid Responds, “The Martyrdom of Uthman”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CeD072hbK0w", ((247, "4:07–5:24"), (327, "5:27–6:23"), (395, "6:35–7:48"), (478, "7:58–8:40"), (521, "8:41–9:15"), (557, "9:17–10:37"), (653, "10:53–12:16"), (739, "12:19–13:17"), (816, "13:36–14:19"), (861, "14:21–15:14"), (926, "15:26–16:34"), (997, "16:37–17:30"), (1050, "17:30–18:20"), (1120, "18:40–19:20"), (1185, "19:45–21:20"), (1280, "21:20–22:36"), (1368, "22:48–23:37"), (1417, "23:37–25:00"), (1511, "25:11–25:54"), (1562, "26:02–27:24"), (1644, "27:24–29:30"), (1792, "29:52–30:54"), (1865, "31:05–32:40"), (1963, "32:43–33:06"), (1989, "33:09–34:42"), (2085, "34:45–36:47"), (2225, "37:05–38:32"), (2316, "38:36–39:36"), (2377, "39:37–40:41"), (2445, "40:45–42:03"), (2582, "43:02–43:27"), (2625, "43:45–44:56"), (2733, "45:33–46:25"), (2788, "46:28–47:23"), (2895, "48:15–49:31"), (3045, "50:45–52:43"), (3177, "52:57–54:05"),))

