#passage(("The background of Jamal happens to be Uthman: that's the whole context, and you're going to be lost when it comes to [Jamal].", "Some basic minor notes: if you're ever going to be looking into [Jamal], it's one of those topics that's so controversial that you have multiple points of views, you have conflicting reports everywhere. So the first piece of advice is: never stick to one basic primary source, because it's just going to confuse you.", "Now the thing is about [Jamal]: I never wanted to lecture about [Jamal], but I need to, because [Jamal] has been abused as a political point against the Sahaba; the topic has been used and abused against the Sunnah. And the people that are using it against the Sunnah have absolutely no clue what happened in [Jamal]. If you were to ask [a Shia]: tell me what happened to [Jamal], maybe he'll quote some narrations against you from your sources; but if you were to ask them: present to me what exactly happened in your view, from your books, it's not going to happen. Of course they have the information; they just don't have authentic reports; they can't piece it together. You have an issue with the Sunni narrative? Have you provided the Shiite narrative? What is the narrative, aslan? So this is my challenge to the Shias who want to use [Jamal] as a polemical point.", "What are the sources of [Jamal]? [Sayf's] report is the general common pro-Sunni report, and this is an issue. Now there are of course multiple sources: you have these lengthy narratives that are very problematic, extremely problematic. However, is it really his [riwayah]? Isn't it really his [mashayikh]? Aren't these the narrations of his [mashayikh]? Isn't it his teachers? So the reason we say this is [riwayat] this person, it's because it simplifies things; and there's a general trend, there's a general tone, pro-[Shi'a], pro-[Sunni], et cetera, and you'll notice this when you actually go through these.",), "Farid Responds, “The Battle of al-Jamal (the Camel)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bbpf_VANFAI", ((14, "0:14–0:26"), (46, "0:46–1:09"), (69, "1:09–2:38"), (163, "2:43–4:10"),))
// U FR222:137-1128 | Farid Responds | 35
#passage(("What's the main issue? He's weakened by the scholars; that's the first issue. Second issue is his teachers are usually [majhul]; the narrations are disconnected. Now what's really interesting about [Sayf] is he will say some things that are very anti-Sunni, that no Sunni will accept. So for example, a loved Sahabi is one of the instigators of the fitnah. Now no Sunni sheikh will use this. This is the issue: if you're going to use [Sayf], use everything; don't pick and choose, don't cherry pick. If he's reliable for something, he can be unreliable for something else.", "Now there's some other issues: for example, there are some contradictions between what he says and what is historically accurate: so for example the time of the battle, the duration of the talks (there were talks between the [Basrans] and the [Kufans]), and finally the amount of people that were killed; [Sayf's] numbers are quite exaggerated.", "Now the main Shia source: well, there are two mainstream sources, but one of the more important Shiite sources, the [riwayah] of the main narrator of the events that occurred in Karbala. We've lost the book of Abu [Mikhnaf], but his narrative has been preserved by [Ibn Jarir]; it's a great book for preserving historical reports. However, there's an issue with Abu [Mikhnaf's] chains, and if Shia want to use the narrative of Abu [Mikhnaf] as the reliable narrative, this could create some problems for everyone. First of all, there's an issue with chains: there's an issue with Abu [Mikhnaf's] teachers and his teachers, and it's filled with anonymous and weak reports.", "In one of his narration he says that Ali hated power, he didn't want the power. Of course this conflicts with the Shiite narrative, because the imam is supposed to have power; you can't back away from power. And he has a very very strange report that no one would accept: he basically says that Ali had an issue with Abu Musa al-Ashari, and he sends a letter to Abu Musa cursing him, and saying: oh you son of a tailor, oh you who bites his father's penis. So I don't think anyone's gonna accept that as something that Ali would say.", "And then you have a Shia-leaning source, which is the report of [al-Waqidi]. Now [al-Waqidi] is technically a Sunni, but it's a very [Shia]-leaning report. This report has been preserved by Mufid in his book, of course [Shia] scholar who passed away in the fifth century. The strange thing is, a liar according to [Ahlus Sunnah], he's a liar according to Mufid himself. So again, he's not the best source for [Jamal]. Of course there's an issue with the chains. He has some contradictions: for example, there's a soldier called Muad bin Abdullah, and in one report [al-Waqidi] says that he's with Ali; in one report he says that he's with [Aisha]. Of course this also shows that contradictions are not necessarily from the historians themselves, but rather from the narrations that they present, from their sheikhs.", "He also has some strange things he adds, like Zubair fled after defeat. He also adds that [Aisha] admits to killing Uthman. So that's a very pro-Shia report. These reports are quite leaning, again. And Mufid has two books on Jamal.", "What is the authentic source of [Jamal]? Mainly you have various narrations here, and they're usually quite short. There's one lengthy report of the Jamal, which is the report of [Kulayb]. The best source for [Jamal]: [the Musannaf] has a chapter, a lengthy chapter with many reports of [Jamal]; many of them are authentic, so it's a very very important source.", "[Uthman] was killed in the year 35 after hijra. How did Ali receive the pledge of allegiance? When Uthman was killed, the people that killed Uthman wanted a khalifa, wanted to put someone into power, so they went to multiple people: they didn't just go to Ali; they went to bin Umar, and they went to [Talhah]. And when they went to bin Umar, they actually threatened bin Umar: if you don't become khalifah, we're going to kill you. Ali himself was very very hesitant to accepting, and he was advised by Ibn Abbas, and he was advised by al-Hasan and [Mughirah ibn Shu'bah], to not accept the khilafah. Why? Ali would be accused, or he would have to bear the responsibility for Uthman's death.", "Ali was not accused of killing Uthman until he accepted. When he accepted the [bay'ah], there were some people who spread this rumor, and they accused Ali of being with those that killed [Uthman]. Why? Well, it's because the people with Ali, his commanders, generals, people that were high ranking around him, were [some of] the killers of [Uthman]; they put him into power. So that was the assumption.", "So basically what happens, let's fast forward a bit, a few months down the line: Aisha was actually in Makkah at the time; they leave Medina, they [head] to Makkah, they meet up with Aisha, and they agree that we have to do something about this, we have to avenge Uthman, and they decide together to go to [Basra].", "Now on the way to Basra there's a very important narration, which is narrated by [Ahmad]: when Aisha, on her way to [Basra], arrives at this water source, she hears dogs barking, and she asks them: where are we? And then she says: I think we need to return back. And she quotes Rasulallah, who said, he says this to his wives: who from among you is going to be barked at by the dogs of [Haw'ab]? That's basically it; there's no more additions to that. So she felt this has a negative implication.", "Now there is an addition in [al-Bazzar], through the path of [Isam], from Ibn Abbas, in which the prophet says: who from among you is going to be barked at by the dogs of [Haw'ab], and the men on her left and on her right are going to be slaughtered? So this report was graded as a [munkar] report by Abu Hatim and his sons, and the reason is: where are all the narrators? How come [Isam] is the only person narrating this? This narration has an [addition] that was not in the original hadith, and that's why there was some doubts about this.", "Where it gets really interesting is with Abu [Mikhnaf]: Abu [Mikhnaf] has an interesting addition to this, an addition to the narration of [Isam]. He says: [Isam] narrates to me from Ibn Abbas that Rasulullah said: who from among you is going to be barked at by the dogs, and men from her left and on her right are going to be slaughtered, and all of them will be in hell fire? So this is the addition of Abu [Mikhnaf]: clear-cut proof that Abu [Mikhnaf] is tampering with the report, adding something to the report that was never narrated by [Isam], of course implying that the jaish, the army of Aisha, are all going to hell, basically. Again, it's the exact same chain.", "Now let's head back to the report of [Kulayb]. Now [Kulayb] says that the [Basran] army, this is during the [Uthman] timeframe, were going to head out, and then they received news that nothing's going to happen, and Uthman basically negotiated, or things were settled with the rebels, and everything was settled, alhamdulillah. Aisha, of course, spoke to them, and she provided her reasons why she came out to Basra.", "So the question is: why in Basra? Why did they go to [Basra] in the first place? They felt like they could find some support in [Basra], and there were of course a lot of available men that would be ready for battle in Basra. [Tarikh Khalifah], one of the best books of [tarikh], summarized but really really useful, mentions that Uthman [ibn Hunayf], he makes peace with them, and they agree; of course, the representative of Ali in [Basra], and they decide to wait for Ali.", "Now the [Basrans] asked [Talhah] how he's acting in a way without the approval of Ali: so what exactly is happening? A sword was put to my neck; I gave [bay'ah] to Ali because a sword was put to my neck. That's what he says. Now keep in mind that this was how chaotic things were in Medina. Ali basically sends Ibn Abbas to make sure if this thing even happened, because Ali had no idea it happened. So Ibn Abbas goes, and he looks for someone who can affirm this; he finds Osama bin Zayed. And Osama bin Zayed, for saying this, was beaten up, for just affirming that Talhah was forced. Beaten up by who? By the haters of [Uthman].", "So now we don't really have too many details of what happens in the [Basra]: we have a lot of weak reports; we have [Sayf ibn] Omar providing a very [pro-Sunni] report; we have Abu [Mikhnaf] and then some others providing a very pro-[Shi'i] report. But basically there are some issues in the Basra, and some fighting occurs, and some people get killed. And at around that time there is a battle that occurs which is called the minor [Jamal]. So we have the normal regular battle of [Jamal] that we all know about, and then there's the minor [Jamal]. Now in the minor battle of [Jamal] we don't have too much information for what occurred there, but we do know of [Hukaym ibn Jabalah] being killed. Hokim bin Jabala is the head of the killers of Uthman from [Basra]. So this is one of the reasons why they even went to Basra: to kill the killers of Uthman in Basra, and they were successful in this. According to the report of [Sayf], he mentions another name; [Hurqus] basically escapes death, and he finds refuge in one of the tribes, I'm guessing his tribe, Allahu [a'lam], and they basically can't reach him.", "So at this point in time, Ali sends some of his main companions to Kufa in order to rally the troops, in order to gain support, because you have an army being built up in Basra. So Ali gathers the troops, and when he's trying to gather troops from Kufa, Abu [Musa], who is Ali's representative in the Kufa, tries to prevent this from happening. He says: my [bay'ah] upon my neck is my [bay'ah] with [Uthman], and my khalifa got killed, and we will join this battle after you kill the killers of Uthman. And this is narrated by Tabari. [Ammar], they rally together some troops as well, and I believe [Ammar] goes on to the [minbar], and he says the very very famous hadith. He says: the wife of your prophet in Jannah; however, Allah wants to see: are you going to follow him or are you going to follow her? This is how [Ammar] saw it, and basically you had a lot of people who joined Ali due to this.", "Now we have to go back to [Kulayb's] rendition; it's a good report, a couple of pages long, small font, so great information. Now [Kulayb] says that he actually meets them both. First of all, Ali sends him; he gives him some letters. He asks him: are you the leader of your tribe? And he says: no, I'm not; there are some people that are higher than me in rank, but I am someone who's accepted from amongst my people. So Ali asks him to send some letters to the tribe of [Bani Rasib]. And before he does this, he asks Ali: how do you feel about Uthman? Now, before Ali was even able to answer, his people started to curse Uthman, and [Kulayb] says: I could see in Ali's face that he hated this. And Ali, on the other hand, he would praise Uthman when this happened, which also suggests something: it shows that Ali doesn't really have complete control over everything that's happening.", "Now one of the two letters that [Kulayb] brings to the tribes is accepted, and one is rejected. So basically what you have is you do have some [Basrans] who do eventually end up with Ali. So the army of [Talhah] and Zubair are completely [Basran], while the army of Ali is a mix, mainly of [Kufans], but you do have some [Basrans] as well.", "So now there are some discussions that occur between the camps. Ibn Abbas goes to Zubair, and he says: was Ali unjust to you? Did Ali wrong you in any way? Why are you doing this? And Zubair says: this is due to fear, and this is due to greed, implying that it was Ali's fear that caused him to act in this way, Ali's [greed]. You don't have to accept this from Zubair, but this is [Zubair's] point of view, this is Zubair's justification: Ali's fear from the killers of [Uthman], Ali's greed for khilafah. And this is not a problem, to believe that the Sahaba wanted high positions, that they wanted rule; this is not controversial at all; it's human nature.", "So the discussions last for three days, and during this time a man comes to Zubair, and he says to Zubair: I have an idea; I'm going to kill Ali for you, basically. So Zubair says: how can you kill Ali? Ali has an army with him; it's impossible to kill Ali. So the man says: I will join his ranks, and I will act as if I'm one of them, and then I'm going to betray Ali, and I'm going to kill him. And Zubair prevents him from doing this, and he quotes the hadith from Rasulullah in which Rasulullah prohibits killing through treacherous means.", "Very very interesting narration by [Kulayb]: he says that when he went to meet the [Kufans], they came to him and they were laughing: is it really possible that the [Basrans] are going to fight us? These are our brothers; when we sit down with them, when we meet them, everything is going to be fine. The idea that a war would break out was not even something that anyone expected. He went back to the [Basrans], and they'd say: what did the [Kufans] say? And they said: yeah, we're just going to reconcile. So nothing was expected at all.", "And then [Kulayb] talks about how the battle started. He said: first of all, the boys, young boys from both camps, came out, and they started to curse each other, and then they would throw stones at each other; and then the slaves from both camps came out and started to do the same, they started to curse each other; and then the idiots, they came and they started to do the same; and all of a sudden it was a battle. It wasn't something that was intended. Now what's the proof for this? It's something very interesting: you have two different people, both with Ali, contradicting each other in regards to who started the battle. Ali, when he saw this happening, he said: I see the people have started to march against one another, and I see no [khayr] in this. And [Hasan] said: be quiet; if they hear us, they will kill us (referring to the people around him), and they will say you have begun to doubt.", "So the battle basically started. And this is one of the more controversial things that happened in the battle: [Talhah] tried to hold the forces back; he's trying to hold his men back from completely breaking out and going into a full-fledged battle mode, and tries to stop them, tries to slow things down. They start to throw stones at him, and then he's shot with an arrow by Marwan [ibn al-Hakam]. Now what's strange about this? That's the strange thing: he's with the [Basrans], and he shoots Talhah. And contemporary sources all narrate this. Now you can argue that this is weak, but I'm personally not convinced. I think that, if [Ibn Sirin], and then you have [Qays] bin [Abi Hazim], who most likely was there, being an eyewitness as well, it's fair to say that [Marwan ibn al-Hakam] did kill. The reason why he killed [Talhah] is because Talhah did not do enough to save Uthman, so Marwan held that grudge within him.", "Now Zubair was approached by Ibn Abbas; this is before the battle even begins. And Ibn Abbas says to Zubair: what would Safiya binta Abdul Muttalib say? And when Zubair heard this, he just left; he left the battlefield, and he headed over to [Wadi al-Siba'], which is very close by, and he was basically killed by [Ibn Jurmuz]. Today in Iraq the whole city is called Zubair, by the way. So this narration is narrated by bin [Sa'd]: when Ibn Abbas went to Ali, and he said: to where will the killer of [Zubair go]? And Ali said: in hellfire. So even though they were on opposing sides, there was a clarity in this: Ali did see that the killer of Zubair was from the people of hellfire.", "Now perhaps one of the more emotional events that occurs in [Jamal] is the martyrdom of Muhammad bin [Talhah], who they used to call [al-Sajjad], because he was an abid. When he saw that everything was chaotic, he went to Aisha, who was on the camel, covered of course in the [howdah], and he said to her: ya ummah, or something along those lines. And she said to him: be like the best of the two sons of Adam. And when he heard this, he put his sword away, and he defended himself with his hands, and was martyred.", "Mohammed bin [Abi Bakr] and Ammar [ibn Yasir], they finally got to the actual camel, and they cut off its legs, and that's how the battle ended: when the camel of Aisha was killed, the battle basically ended. So they attacked the camel basically to end the battle. It just ends the battle, because Zubair is gone, [Talhah is] killed; there's no other symbol left. By the way, [Aisha] is not the commander of the army; [Aisha] was there for moral support. [Aisha] is the symbol, but she's not a commander of the battle. [Aisha] is actually covered away from the [battle]; she doesn't see what's going on in the first place.", "So basically, Ali shouted out: don't chase those who are retreating; if there's someone who is injured, don't kill that person; if someone goes into a house, don't enter that house. So those were his rules, basically. Ali, of course, prevented the victors, the [Kufans], from taking the women as captives as well. And in the report (I'm not sure about the authenticity of this report; I think it is authentic) they said to him: you are allowing us to take away their lives, but you don't allow us to take away their women? And then he said: who wants to take away the wife of Rasulullah? So of course that ended it, basically; they all realize that they are in the wrong, and this is not something that can happen.", "So [al-Jarmi] says, this is very interesting, he says: by the time I got to Ali, the battle had already ended. This signifies that the battle was very short. It's not something that lasted for months or years; it was a few hours long, the whole battle. The number of [Basrans] that were killed: some reports say twenty thousand, thirty thousand; the most authentic report says two thousand five hundred. [Sayf] says ten thousand. [Sayf] also says that the negotiations between the [Kufans] and the [Basrans] lasted for a day; in reality they lasted for three days. He says the battle started at night; there's nothing about the night; all that nice stuff, that's all Abdullah bin [Saba's] stuff, so forget about that. This thing happened during the day.", "So, quick analysis: why did the [Kufans] win? So the leaders: the general, by the way, he's leading the cavalry; he gets killed. Zubair leaves the battle immediately. There are no leaders. Now it seems like in one report, it implies that the [Basrans] were reassured, and they expected it less to happen: there was an agreement, there was general peace; no one's prepared for war. That's another reason why things ended so quickly.", "And there seems to be another reason, and I haven't seen anyone argue this, but it seems like the [Basrans] were smaller in number, according to Haytham bin [Adi]. Now Haytham is a terrible source, by the way, but it seems like he got it right when it came to the numbers of men on both sides: he argues twelve thousand for the [Kufans] and eight thousand for the [Basrans], so fifty percent more. Now the reason I believe this is because you have some reports, one in [Ansab al-Ashraf]: it states that the [Kufans] were 12,000. So [Ibn Adi] was correct in one number; I do believe that that means that he was most likely correct in the other number, meaning the [Basrans] were four thousand less. And also keep in mind that the army of Ali had [Kufans] and [Basrans], while the [Basran] army was just [Basrans], so it's logical that they'd have a larger number.", "What were the reactions of those that took part in the battle? Zaid bin [Sawhan] was one of those that was exiled by Uthman, and he says: we don't know; we came to a land, we killed the emir, and it would have been best if we just were patient when we were oppressed. [Aisha], on her death bed, she said: bury me with the wives of Rasulullah, because I did something after him, implying her participation in this fitna. Me and your father are from those that Allah said about them: and we will remove from their chests any bitterness, brothers facing each other upon thrones. When he said this, a man from Hamdan said: Allah is more fair than to do this, than to put both of you in Jannah. And Ali said: then who is it about? Who are these verses about, if it's not about me and Talhah?", "And now we get to the end of Jamal, and the most ironic thing in the whole scenario and all the events, which is, in my opinion, one of the main characters. [Al-Ashtar] got hurt in the battle. He says, and this is a very very famous narration: he was in the battle, he was fighting with Abdul Rahman, and basically they clash with one another, and then they start to wrestle one another, and they start to roll on the floor, and Abdulrahman said: kill me and kill Malik! And people had no idea who Malik was; no one knows his name is Malik.", "And then Ashtar said: when the emir of al-Basra sees you, don't worry about it; your matters will be fine. [Kulayb] says al-Ashtar felt that he was going to become the emir; Ali was going to appoint him as the emir. So [Kulayb] left the room, and a man came, and he said: the emir has appointed Abdullah bin Abbas as the representative of Ali on [Basra]. And then another man comes in affirming this, and [al-Ashtar] says: you hear it? He says: no, I didn't hear it. And then [Attab al-Taghlibi] comes in, and he confirms this. Why did we kill the sheikh in Medina, if this is how it's going to be?",), "Farid Responds, “The Battle of al-Jamal (the Camel)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bbpf_VANFAI", ((347, "5:47–6:28"), (438, "7:18–7:46"), (475, "7:55–9:09"), (566, "9:26–10:25"), (634, "10:34–11:34"), (697, "11:37–12:21"), (781, "13:01–14:16"), (873, "14:33–16:02"), (973, "16:13–16:48"), (1008, "16:48–17:14"), (1038, "17:18–18:09"), (1089, "18:09–19:11"), (1153, "19:13–20:18"), (1224, "20:24–21:12"), (1280, "21:20–22:17"), (1340, "22:20–23:52"), (1439, "23:59–25:54"), (1581, "26:21–27:56"), (1687, "28:07–29:33"), (1778, "29:38–30:06"), (1808, "30:08–31:15"), (1894, "31:34–32:19"), (1945, "32:25–33:06"), (1986, "33:06–34:43"), (2094, "34:54–37:00"), (2225, "37:05–38:29"), (2313, "38:33–39:24"), (2380, "39:40–40:34"), (2436, "40:36–41:34"), (2495, "41:35–43:18"), (2600, "43:20–44:21"), (2661, "44:21–45:34"), (2739, "45:39–47:54"), (2879, "47:59–49:08"), (2973, "49:33–51:19"),))

=== 46.5 Karbala and its sources
