// U FR254:1-35 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Yassir Qadhi's Karbala lecture: this video has received 700,000 views, more if you count other channels; it's been seen by a lot of people. The reason why people watch this video is because Yassir Qadhi attempts to provide the Sunni audience with the Sunni narrative, the Sunni perspective of what occurred at Karbala. He's not going to be using the Shi'i rendition of events. I have right here with me a copy of Abu Mikhnaf's rendition of Maqtal al-Husayn, and it's printed, of course, by a Shi'i publisher, Dar al-Mahajjah al-Bayda; it's edited by Kamil al-Juburi.",), "Farid Responds, “Warning: Yasir Qadhi's Karbala Lecture”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HqUyhLiY774", ((6, "0:06–3:24"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML137:1-235 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("There are historical narratives that the Shia will have, and there are historical narratives that Ahl asSunnah, which is the overwhelming majority, over 90% of Muslims, will have. They're not the same historical event. So we do believe that the people in Iraq, in that region, or the people who actually killed Husayn, were not people from the Sunnah or any of that. The primary texts for these things would be in Arabic, not in English.", "History works through testimony, first-person testimony of events taking place, or people who were around that time, or people who are very close to that time that have written about the event. What happened happened in a Muslim land, in a Muslim empire. The only people who wrote about this were Muslims, and those two Muslim sides had either a view or a view with a different position. So there's only two narratives that you can present.", "Historical narratives from Encyclopædia Britannica, from a source that you cannot even name, and none of which are contemporary, we don't accept. We don't rely on Encyclopædia Britannica to know the history that happened and the history that didn't happen. And he wasn't going to a funeral. Then he was killed in a battlefield because he was going to a funeral? Which makes no logical sense, by the way. I don't believe that Umayyads are the ones who killed Hussein. I believe it was actually the people who called themselves \"Shia of Ali\" are the people who killed Hussein, and they are the ones who sent him a letter, and he accepted.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Christian Attempts To Educate A Muslim On Islamic History! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XzZHWEENo6k", ((48, "0:48–1:25"), (134, "2:14–2:45"), (351, "5:51–7:27"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR254:51-109 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Yassir mentions that this information is based off Tarikh al-Tabari. And if you go back to Tarikh al-Tabari, surprise surprise, al-Tabari is quoting Abu Mikhnaf: the same Shi'i historian that Yassir told us not to take history from, the same Shi'i historian that Yassir said is unreliable. And this information is from Maqtal al-Husayn by Abi Mikhnaf, once again, page 70.", "Now, this one's quite strange, because it conflicts directly with what actually happened according to the authentic Sunni narrative. According to Ahl al-Sunnah, Muslim ibn Aqil has 50 men with him, and it's only after a skirmish, and after the men are defeated and Muslim ibn Aqil is wounded, that's when he is by himself. It's not like they left him intentionally, and it was due to their cowardice that he was left all alone. No, he had 50 men with him, and there was an actual battle. That's from page 77 in Maqtal al-Husayn li Abi Mikhnaf. Once again, Yassir Qadhi is quoting the same source that he warns us about, the same source that he says is unreliable, and the same source that he is trying to avoid. Also, a brief note: the report doesn't specifically mention Shias; it mentions قوم, or people; it doesn't mention Shias.", "This is taken from, again, al-Bidayah wa al-Nihayah, one of the two main sources that Yassir mentions in the beginning of his video, page 565. In this report, we actually do find ibn Kathir clearly stating that this is taken from Maqtal al-Husayn by Abi Mikhnaf: once again, the source that Yassir warns us off, the source that Shias rely upon. This video was watched by Sunnis, was watched by you because you wanted the Sunni perspective. Instead, Yasir is providing you with Abu Mikhnaf's version of the events. That's a problem. If you don't have time to prepare a lecture, if you don't want to put in the effort to provide your audience with the correct information, then my advice to you is: please don't give that lecture.",), "Farid Responds, “Warning: Yasir Qadhi's Karbala Lecture”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HqUyhLiY774", ((277, "4:37–6:20"), (382, "6:22–8:01"), (526, "8:46–10:30"),))
