#passage(("[Khawarij]: to exit something. Where does this name come from? It comes from the Hadith. There was a person, and that individual, he said to the prophet: be just, for you're not just. And then the prophet, he said: they will come from the progeny of this a group of people that will kill the Muslims and they will leave the non-muslims. The ISIS group is an example: look at their catch: most of whom they kill is Muslims. Do you see them doing operations in Israel? Last time I checked, they're doing in [Syria], Yemen; that's what they're doing. They will kill the people of Islam, and they will leave the people of idols, leave the disbelievers. And then the prophet said: they will exit the religion like the arrow exit. This is where the idea of the term comes from. They said the person who commits a major sin is a disbeliever, and they said that we need to rule with what Allah has revealed, and what they mean by rule what Allah has revealed is that we say everyone is a disbeliever; that's what they mean: if you do anything wrong, you do a major sin, you're a disbeliever, and they killed.", "These groups always come back to a name of a person, but when it comes to the Sufis, there is a person who wrote a book, and in his book he called the sahaba Sufis, because that term Sufi, coming from [suf] or [safa], meant that a person is not in need of this worldly life; he's just focusing on the afterlife. That's what it meant. But later on the scholars criticize him, because how can you call the companions this term, when now it's being misused? Now what they mean is to exaggerate in the praise of individuals, the righteous people. If someone is a good person, this person, you kiss his hand, bow his feet, all of the stuff.", "And the other issue they have is the idea of innovating in the [deen] of Allah, innovating new things in the religion. The prophet told us: whoever bring anything in this religion that is not from it is rejected. And Allah told us this religion i